417,/Amhara_Messing_1985_263_KS,EasternAfrica,Amhara,older boys,group,boys of eight or nine who aspire to deaconhood,age_set,within_group,Recurring mock-examinations/hazing of deacon candidates by older boys described as ritualized practice.,"Boys of eight or nine who aspire to deaconhood at the next New Year holy days are mock-examined by older boys who have achieved that status. The candidate must carry each of them several times around the churchyard, and must recite and chant liturgical prayers for them for hours.",mock-examination (hazing),,non_symmetric,teasing,0.7,,2026-07-06T08:56:11.613450+00:00
418,/Amhara_Messing_1985_269_KS,EasternAfrica,Amhara,Arab traders,ethnic group,Orthodox Amhara women,group,cross_group,"Recurring, culturally patterned teasing by Arab traders toward Orthodox Amhara women in market stores.","Arab traders, particularly, like to joke with Orthodox Amhara women when the latter come to their stores; e.g., teasingly refusing to hand over goods after purchase has been made, or if the customer finds the price on a desired item too high, suggesting that payment might be made “another way”.",joke/tease in stores,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.8,,2026-07-06T08:56:19.013882+00:00
419,/Amhara_Messing_1985_323_KS,EasternAfrica,Amhara,zar society,group,sibling devotees,kin_role,within_group,"Text describes recurring, culturally patterned 'sibling jokes' among zar devotees within the zar society.","In either case they enjoy recreation and amusement with the “sibling” devotees, with whom they can gossip and enact sibling jokes, such as playfully accusing each other of minor oversights in the service of the zar (it often takes this form).",sibling jokes,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:56:26.354646+00:00
452,/Bagisu_Heald_1989_163_KS,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,members of different lineages,lineage,members of different lineages,lineage,within_group,Text describes bukulo as a hereditary joking tie linking different lineage segments as bakulo partners.,"In the 1960s, bukulo was a hereditary joking relationship which held between members of different lineages and linked members as joking partners (bakulo) to each other.",bukulo / bakulo,bukulo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Document describes typical links between small neighbouring lineage segments within Bagisu; partners are non-kin and hold licensed abuse.,2026-07-06T08:59:57.435184+00:00
453,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_26_KS,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,minor lineage,lineage,minor lineage,lineage,within_group,Text describes bagulo as a recurring joking tie between two minor lineages within Bagisu society.,A special tie is that known as bagulo which involves two lineages in a joking relationship. Members of lineages who are bagulo joke with one another and may seize one another's property with impunity.,bagulo,bagulo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Document frames bagulo as a culturally sanctioned recurring tie between minor lineages; no specific lineage names given.,2026-07-06T09:00:10.629734+00:00
454,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,maternal grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent joking between maternal grandparents and grandchildren.,"With the maternal grandparents there is a form of joking which is common: they use the terms for sibling of the same sex and spouse to one another and may demand small gifts in an abrupt, rough manner.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:00:20.933165+00:00
455,/Bagisu_LaFontaine_1959_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Bagisu,paternal grandmother,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Text notes joking may also be indulged with the paternal grandmother toward grandchildren.,This joking may also be indulged with the paternal grandmother; it is rarely used to the paternal grandfather.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Joking is less common with paternal grandfather; paternal grandmother included as occasional partner.,2026-07-06T09:00:20.933165+00:00
472,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_15_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,muko,kin_role,isezara,kin_role,kinship,"Recurrent, culturally specified asymmetric licensed familiarity between son-in-law and wife's father.","He may not address his wife's father by his personal name (though his wife's father may so address him); he must address him by terms expressing their different statuses, such as waitu, munyoro, or mukama wange (‘sir’, ‘lord’, or ‘my master’), or by his mpako or praise-name.",rules / inequality of relationship,muko; isezara,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:02:02.778377+00:00
473,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,a man,kin_role,his wife's sister (muramu / baramu),kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly describes muramu/baramu as reciprocal, familiarly joking ties between a man and his wife's sister.","A man calls his wife's sisters, and his brothers' wives, baramu (sing. muramu), or baramukati (i.e. baramu women). ... Thus a man thinks of his relationship with his female baramu as intimate and friendly; they are ‘like wives’. He can talk and joke familiarly with them.",baramu / muramu,muramu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:02:22.509013+00:00
474,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,a man,kin_role,his brother's wife (baramu / baramukati),kin_role,kinship,Brother's wife is explicitly listed as baramu/baramukati with joking familiarity between opposite sexes.,"A man calls his wife's sisters, and his brothers' wives, baramu (sing. muramu), or baramukati (i.e. baramu women). ... Thus a man thinks of his relationship with his female baramu as intimate and friendly; they are ‘like wives’. He can talk and joke familiarly with them.",baramu / baramukati,baramukati,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:02:22.509013+00:00
475,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_16_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,a man,kin_role,his wife's brother (baramu / also baisezara in some contexts),kin_role,kinship,Wife's brother is called baramu but also sometimes baisezara; male baramu have a more formal relation though noted as affinal tie.,"He also uses the term baramu for his wife's brothers, and his sisters' husbands. ... But a man's male muramu is at the same time either muko to the agnatic group to which that man belongs, or else he is one of the group of that man's baisezara. In either case he must be treated with something of the politeness and ceremony ...",baramu (male),baramu,mixed,joking_relationship,0.85,Male baramu have partial joking but also formal duties; text marks dual status (baramu vs baisezara/muko).,2026-07-06T09:02:22.509013+00:00
476,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_18_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,bwihwa relationship (man/nephew),kin_role,group of his mother's agnates,kin_role,kinship,"Text frames bwihwa as an ambivalent, recurrent relation of familiarity/restraint between a man and mother's agnates.","There is, then, a manifest ambivalence in the bwihwa relationship. For in it are focused a man's relations with the whole group of his mother's agnates...and to this group he is at one and the same time a child whom they have borne, and an ‘outsider’.",bwihwa,bwihwa,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Bwihwa treated as Radcliffe-Brown-type joking/ambivalent relation (conjunctive/disjunctive components).,2026-07-06T09:02:33.035663+00:00
477,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_20_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,mwihwa,kin_role,nyinarumi's wife,kin_role,kinship,Explicit culturally permitted familiar joking between sister's son (mwihwa) and his mother's-brother's wife.,He may not 'climb on to nyinarumi's bed' or sleep with his wife (though he may joke familiarly with her).,privileged familiarity,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Text frames joking as a permitted, recurrent liberty within avuncular/nepotic relations; mwihwa appears granted license to tease the wife.",2026-07-06T09:02:41.337216+00:00
478,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,banyinarumi,kin_role,baihwa,kin_role,kinship,Document describes recurrent familiar joking privileges between mother's brothers (banyinarumi) and sisters' children (baihwa).,"This affectionate intimacy also extends to the mother's brothers, banyinarumi.... A sister's son may take certain liberties in his nyinarumi's house; he may help himself uninvited to food, and may take or borrow almost everything he wants. He may joke familiarly with his mother's brother's wives, though he may not sleep with them.",,banyinarumi / baihwa,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:02:56.289095+00:00
479,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_332_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,banyinarumi's wives,kin_role,mwana / mwihwa,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states the sister's son (mwana/mwihwa) may joke familiarly with his mother's brother's wives as a permitted recurrent liberty.,"A sister's son may take certain liberties in his nyinarumi's house; he may help himself uninvited to food, and may take or borrow almost everything he wants. He may joke familiarly with his mother's brother's wives, though he may not sleep with them.",,mwana / mwihwa,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Privilege mainly exercised by the sister's son toward the mother's brother's wives; asymmetry in liberties.,2026-07-06T09:02:56.289095+00:00
480,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,grandparent,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent, culturally permitted familiar joking between grandparents and grandchildren.","The relations between grandparents and grandchildren are friendly and intimate, contrasting strongly with the father-child relationship. It is permissible for a grandchild to touch his grandfather's hair or beard, which he may not do in the case of his father. A boy may joke familiarly with his grandparents, and they are said to spoil and over-feed their grandchildren and treat them indulgently.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:03:06.971915+00:00
481,/Banyoro_Beattie_1958_338_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,grandchild,kin_role,grandfather's wives,kin_role,kinship,Document notes a recurring licensed joking practice: youths jokingly call their grandfather's wives their own.,"Banyoro sometimes say that a grandfather is ‘like a brother’. Consistently with this, a youth may jokingly call his grandfather's wives his wives. This terminological usage need not always have a primarily sexual reference (though it does sometimes, e.g. in the case of a brother's wives).",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:03:06.971915+00:00
482,/Banyoro_Beattie_1960_55_KS,EasternAfrica,Banyoro,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent, culturally permitted joking/play between grandparents and grandchildren.","The relations between grandparents and grandchildren are friendly and intimate, contrasting strongly with those between fathers and sons. A boy may joke and play with his grandfather in a way which would not be permissible with his father, and Nyoro grandparents, like their Western counterparts, are often said to “spoil” their grandchildren.",friendly/intimate joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:03:13.307426+00:00
486,/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,mama (elder brother),kin_role,yaya (maternal uncle),kin_role,kinship,Age-based substitution of kin terms used jokingly between kin roles (Towegale example).,"Towegale inherited his seventh wife, Samweti binti Mbago ( ), from a man whom he called his mama (elder brother), but who was really an absurdly young yaya (maternal uncle), for Towegale's mother also called him brother.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Pattern: deliberate use of an elder-brother term in place of maternal-uncle as a joking/age-adjusted relation.,2026-07-06T09:04:03.660809+00:00
487,/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,maternal uncle,kin_role,cross-cousin,kin_role,kinship,Cross-cousins labelled as maternal-uncle/niece jokingly for marriage eligibility and age adjustment.,"Similarly, Ndaliwali was called Binti Bilali's (39) maternal uncle because she was of suitable age to marry his son Mtengera, but really Ndaliwali and she were cross-cousins of the same generation.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Shows culturally patterned relabelling for marriage negotiation; institutionalized joking kin-term substitution.,2026-07-06T09:04:03.660809+00:00
488,/Bena_Culwick_1935_199_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,father,kin_role,son,kin_role,kinship,Younger man half-jokingly calls older man 'father' due to affinal tie; invokes reciprocal obligations.,"Again, a half-grown youth addressed (half jokingly, it must be admitted) a young man only a few years his senior as “father,” because the latter had taken to wife the young sister of the former's mother. But joke or no joke, the “father” had to feed his “son” when called upon by him to do so, when they were both travelling with the writers.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Joking address creates a recognized duty (feeding), indicating institutionalized licensed familiarity.",2026-07-06T09:04:03.660809+00:00
489,/Bena_Culwick_1935_337_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,mother's brother,kin_role,boys,kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally patterned avuncular familiarity and teasing between mother's brother and boys (nephews).,"The male relative whom the Wabena regard with most affection is, then, not their father but their mother's brother, on whom the boys in particular look as their best friend and counsellor.  ...  This is the more noticeable in that the Mbena is a great tease among those with whom he is thoroughly at his ease.",best friend and counsellor,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:04:13.088248+00:00
490,/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,cross-cousin,kin_role,sibling in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text states cross-cousin or sibling-in-law terms imply freedom of expression and lack of restraint (licensed joking).,"Thus, a person with whom one is on good terms and who is thought to be a relative who is about one's own age and general status will be addressed by cross-cousin or sibling in-law terms implying freedom of expression, lack of restraint concerning joking and matters of bodily functions (i.e. sex and elimination can be freely and frankly discussed).",freedom of expression / lack of restraint,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:04:31.110765+00:00
491,/Bena_Swartz_1968_42_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,grandparent,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,"Text says grandparental terms imply comradeship and freedom from restraint, indicating licensed joking between generations.",A much older putative relative will be the object of grandparental terms again implying comradeship and freedom from restraint.,comradeship / freedom from restraint,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:04:31.110765+00:00
492,/Bena_Swartz_1968_44_KS,EasternAfrica,Bena,vatani,kin_role,vatani,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent mild teasing/horseplay among own-generation kin (vatani), a patterned kin joking practice.","Conversely, anyone classed with the vatani can be treated more freely and although the rather strong pressure for joking behavior noted for other groups toward people in this category (see footnote 5) is absent, there is sometimes horseplay and the exchange of mild teasing and ‘friendly’ insults among vatani.",,vatani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:04:38.751757+00:00
496,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_143_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,two elders (representing opposing parties),kin_role,two elders (representing opposing parties),kin_role,within_group,Ritualized mutual scolding and dirty jokes between elders to mark separation; recurring ceremonial practice.,"The two elders, with the ditch between them, begin to scold each other violently and to incite each other into a fighting spirit. ""Lumae sonu, via kindo kja sonu, kuiho-se msiki lurentse kifa kjakefo — Let us eliminate the disgrace. Say something disgraceful. There is no sib sister anymore on whose account we would have to be bashful."" The other elder follows up with a dirty joke.",Lupariane mfo (ceremonial scolding/toy fighting),Lupariane mfo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.76,"Elders act as ritual representatives of opposing parties/sibs; text frames the scolding and dirty joke as an important, recurrent ceremonial demonstration.",2026-07-06T09:05:22.926453+00:00
497,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_305_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,young men,age_set,young men,age_set,within_group,"Document states momo began as a joking form of address among same-age young men, a patterned age-grade practice.","At the present time, a new designation for the members of the same age grade makes its appearance in the Kibosho district: momo, which in the Kibosho dialect means heifer. It started as a joking form of address among young men, and is now the fashionable word of Kibosho.",momo (joking form of address),momo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:05:39.004536+00:00
498,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_455_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,Rindi of Moshi,village,Sina of Kibosho,village,cross_group,Recurring rivalry allows sanctioned ridicule between the two chieftains/communities; messenger exploited this teasing to defuse danger.,"Rindi of Moshi and Sina of Kibosho were bitter enemies.
...
He stepped before Sina and talked in broken Chagga language: “Here, councellor, cousin yours yours sends it”
...
Sina had to laugh, and all of his men shook with laughter (menge ikavika nawanawa seko).",bitter enemies / mutual ridicule,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,Text implies recurrent teasing/ridicule between the two communities' warriors rather than one-off private joking.,2026-07-06T09:05:54.153313+00:00
499,/Chagga_Guttman_1926_80_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,bride,kin_role,younger sister,kin_role,kinship,"Ritualized, recurrent teasing act (beer theft) between bride and her younger sister in marriage ceremony.",It is customary that the bride ask her younger sister to remove some of the beer surreptitiously from the first barrel while the men come and go to fill the barrel gradually. ... This joke is a token of her love.,,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:06:05.865198+00:00
500,/Chagga_Raum_1940_264_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,novices of the play-group,age_set,older boys / experienced boys,age_set,within_group,"Recurring, culturally patterned practical jokes as initiation within child play-groups; hierarchical older→novice teasing.","The novices of the play-group have to undergo a severe introductory training in the form of practical jokes. ... A species of bird called kilepuru, they are told by the experienced boys, can be caught if approached with closed eyes. The novice gropes about for one and after some fumbling opens his eyes. The birds are gone and he is mocked: ‘Why on earth did you open your eyes!’",introductory training / practical jokes,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:06:16.378823+00:00
501,/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,nurses,kin_role,the girl that wriggles,kin_role,within_group,Routine play-group ordeal where nurses are ordered to test and then laugh at the wriggling girl; patterned teasing role.,"When children discover ants marching along, the nurses among them are ordered to put a finger into the column. The girl that wriggles about uneasily is bitten, while the experienced girl keeps still and is not attacked. The former is laughed at for having eaten the baby's food.",,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.7,Nurses and novice/younger girls occupy recurrent testing/teasing roles in the play-group's ordeal.,2026-07-06T09:06:31.352706+00:00
502,/Chagga_Raum_1940_279_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,boys,group,an unpopular boy,kin_role,within_group,"Concerted, recurrent practice of boys throwing Solanum fruits to punish or tease an unpopular boy; culturally patterned within play-group.","Boys usually carry a few in the folds of their garments. They are sometimes thrown in fun at a child looking in another direction. Occasionally such an attack develops into a concerted action, punishing a boy for his unpopularity, his boasts, his unfair methods of play, and his betrayal of pasture secrets.",,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.6,Extracted as a patterned intra-group joking/punitive practice rather than isolated pranks.,2026-07-06T09:06:31.352706+00:00
503,/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,young men,age_set,girls of one's own age,age_set,within_group,Text states young men may jest with girls of their own age; framed as institutionalized joking facilitating courtship.,"Manly conduct demands detachment from the female sex and the younger age-groups. For this reason, young men should avoid jesting with their juniors or womenfolk. The only exceptions are the wife of the mother's brother and girls of one's own age, who have been taught not to revile men.

The implications are deeper. The joking relationship with young women facilitates the contact of potential marriage partners.",joking relationship with young women,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:06:50.703012+00:00
504,/Chagga_Raum_1940_330_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,young men,age_set,wife of the mother's brother,kin_role,kinship,Explicitly described as an exception: an institutionalized joking tie with mother's brother's wife to reduce awe.,"The only exceptions are the wife of the mother's brother and girls of one's own age, who have been taught not to revile men.

The similar relationship with a member of the parental generation, the mother's brother's wife, is a mechanism to counteract the awe of her husband which has been inculcated.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:06:50.703012+00:00
505,/Chagga_Raum_1940_348_KS,EasternAfrica,Chagga,members of an age-group (young men),age_set,girls of his own age,age_set,within_group,Text describes a culturally recognized joking relationship tied to age-groups between sexes.,"Like the Nuer, the Chaga stands in a joking relationship with girls of his own age, but for the latter this does not preclude marriage, whereas for the former it does.",joking relationship,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:06:57.591721+00:00
550,/Ganda_Mair_1934_163_KS,EasternAfrica,Ganda,the peasants,group,the chiefs,group,within_group,Text describes recurring peasants' humorous speech directed to chiefs as a cultural practice.,"In the old days, the peasants would visit the chiefs and use all sorts of humorous phrases, thus pleasing them greatly. They made feasts for them and gave them gifts.",joke-telling to chiefs,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:12:12.909660+00:00
551,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_116_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,the ones who did not have their ears pierced,group,those who had their ears pierced,group,within_group,Piercing marks an age/peer boundary; explicit mutual exclusion from joking is culturally patterned.,"The ones who did not have their ears pierced were like fools, because they were trying to cut themselves off from the community. We would not joke with them, nor could they joke about with us.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,Age/peer grouping implied by 'agemates' and ear-piercing practice; text frames reciprocal prohibition on joking.,2026-07-06T09:12:23.280573+00:00
552,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_120_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,girls,group,young uncircumcised boys,age_set,within_group,"Recurring, culturally noted joking between girls and younger (uncircumcised) boys in the community.","Girls continue to joke around with younger boys even when they are grown. ... And now, young uncircumcised boys are being called “men” by girls, so they feel grown-up and can joke about with the girls.",joke around,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,Configured as within-group age/gender patterned joking; text implies a culturally patterned licenced familiarity tied to age/circumcision status.,2026-07-06T09:12:33.550451+00:00
553,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,circumcised girls,age_set,uncircumcised girls,age_set,within_group,Text states joking norms tied to circumcision status and age groups within society.,"One became mature. It was very important in those days, because when one got circumcised, one would not joke about with uncircumcised girls and they would give us respect.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Institutional norm: circumcised girls refrain from joking with uncircumcised girls; indicates regulated joking by riika membership.,2026-07-06T09:12:58.435436+00:00
554,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,circumcised young women,age_set,uncircumcised young men,age_set,within_group,Explicit recurrent permission to talk and joke across sexes tied to circumcision/age status.,"Once circumcised, a young woman could talk and joke with young, uncircumcised men, but we did not move with them sexually until one got married.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,"Patterned, sanctioned teasing/interaction between riika-status women and men.",2026-07-06T09:12:58.435436+00:00
555,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_173_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,girls who went for Irua,age_set,girls who went for Irua,age_set,within_group,Text describes recurring joking among girls initiated together (Irua riika members).,"And another thing, there are no mariika today. The girls who went for Irua stayed friends and would talk until dark, asking each other questions… about the day and joking with each other.",,Irua,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Irua initiates form an age-group joking network (riika membership).,2026-07-06T09:12:58.435436+00:00
556,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_83_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,members of the same mariika,age_set,members of the same mariika,age_set,within_group,Text describes culturally patterned licensed joking and greeting among same mariika (age-set) members.,"People of different mariika would not abuse each other or even greet each other. But you, if you were of my same age group, when we met we would greet one another and laugh and tell jokes or do anything we wanted together.",playmates / licensed joking among same mariika,mariika,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:13:09.883898+00:00
557,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_90_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,those who had been circumcised,age_set,those who had not been circumcised,age_set,within_group,Text describes recurrent teasing/licensed insults between circumcised and uncircumcised as culturally patterned.,"Wamutira: But nowadays, things are better because nobody calls another karigũ [silly uncircumcised girl] or kahiĩ [same applied to a boy], and so there are no fights between the circumcised and uncircumcised.

Muthoni: Now, you can't tell the difference between one who is circumcised and one who is not. Long ago, if you were of my age group and we met and my ears were pierced, you would never joke with me or abuse me until yours were pierced, too.",circumcised vs uncircumcised teasing,karigũ; kahiĩ,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:13:21.925597+00:00
558,/Gikuyu_Davison_1996_91_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,agemates,age_set,other mariika,group,within_group,"Text states joking is practiced with one's agemates but not with members of other mariika, a patterned intra-societal licensing.","With agemates, you can joke about things like that—where you spent the night and who was there. But with those of other mariika, you had nothing to talk about.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Document frames joking as licensed among age-mates and absent toward members of other 'mariika' (unspecified subgroup).,2026-07-06T09:13:31.429782+00:00
559,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,young men of his own age-group,age_set,young women of her age-group,age_set,within_group,"Age-groups collectively ridicule, ostracise and dine with transgressors—patterned sanctioning between same-age male and female groups.","The fine for this is nine sheep or goats and three big, fat sheep (ndorome) as the kiama fees. Besides this, the man is made a social outcast or “sent to Covertry” (kohingwo) by all the young men and girls of his own age-group. ... She pays a fine by providing a feast to the men and the girls of her age-group. She is also liable to ridicule (kohingwo and gocambio).",,kohingwo; gocambio,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Text frames sanctions and ridicule as actions by the age-groups toward transgressing members of opposite sex in the same age-set.,2026-07-06T09:13:48.281925+00:00
560,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_160_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,people who know,group,young man dancing with his cousin,kin_role,kinship,Community members publicly laugh and joke to warn a man who mistakenly dances with his cousin—recurrent warning via joking tied to kin relation.,"During festival dances, where people from various districts meet and dance together, it is not always possible to recognise a relative who comes from far away, and a man may sometimes be seen dancing with his cousin by mistake. When this happens, people who know will start laughing and joking to warn the young man of his error.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,Actors described as 'people who know' warn the man; coded as kinship because joking targets cousin taboo.,2026-07-06T09:13:48.281925+00:00
561,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurring freedom in playing and joking between grandparents and grandchildren.,"When the grandparents are not living near the homesteads of their daughters or sons, the children pay frequent visits to their grandparents and stay with them for some time. Sometimes it becomes difficult to get the children to return to their parents homesteads, for they feel more free in playing and joking with their grandparents than they would with their own parents.",play and joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:06.199802+00:00
562,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,mother's sister (tata),kin_role,children of her sister (nieces and nephews),kin_role,kinship,Described avuncular indulgence and recurrent affectionate playful relations between aunt and her nieces/nephews.,"The mother's sister is called tata, the same as the father's sister, but the relation between her and the children of her sister is entirely different. She is looked upon by the children in the same way as their real mother. The affection and indulgence that she gives to the children and the sympathetic attitude towards them is even greater than that the children can expect from their own mother.",avuncular indulgence / privileged familiarity,tata,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:06.199802+00:00
563,/Gikuyu_Kenyatta_1953_16_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,children of both sisters,kin_role,children of both sisters,kin_role,kinship,"Children of sisters address one another with specific terms and behave like siblings, indicating patterned joking/playing between cross-cousins.","Children like to visit or to be visited by their aunt, and whenever such a visit is made it is always an occasion of rejoicing and feasting. The children of both sisters address one another as moro or mware we tata (son or daughter of my or our aunt), the behaviour pattern between them is almost like brothers and sisters.",cousin play / sibling-like behavior,moro; mware we tata,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Extracted as recurring patterned relations among cross-cousins (children of sisters).,2026-07-06T09:14:06.199802+00:00
564,/Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly calls the reciprocal grandparent–grandchild terms a joking relationship.,"A child is named after one of its grandparents and “in religious ceremonies the children are treated
in the same manner as their grandparents.” A boy is called “my husband” by
his grandmother, and a girl “my co-wife”; the grandfather calls his grandson
“my equal” and his granddaughter “my bride.” It is implicit in Kenyatta's
account that this is a joking relationship.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:14:18.311210+00:00
565,/Gikuyu_Middleton_1953_25_KS,EasternAfrica,Gikuyu,wife,kin_role,husband's brother,kin_role,kinship,Recurring reciprocal nicknaming between wife and husband's brother implies patterned licensed familiarity.,"An affine is referred to as muthoni “which means one who is bashful or
polite”; the term applies to “all clan members” of families linked by marriage. Kinship Terminology
Muthoni is apparently used between all affines except that a husband's
brother is “given a nickname as a sign of endearment” by the wife, and he
reciprocates, and the26 husband addresses his wife's sister as maramu.",nickname/reciprocal endearment,muthoni,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Text implies recurring cultural pattern though not labeled explicitly as 'joking'; treated as institutionalized licensed familiarity.,2026-07-06T09:14:18.311210+00:00
566,/Gusii_Hakansson_1997_261_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,polygynists,group,monogamous men,group,within_group,Recurring cultural ridicule at beer parties targeting monogamous men by polygynous men—a patterned social teasing practice.,"In the 1950s, to be a monogamist was, for older men, a source of ridicule and jokes at beer parties, and monogamous men were often urged to take a second wife.",ridicule/jokes,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:14:26.239912+00:00
567,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,kin_role,brother,kin_role,kinship,Text states clansmen may make sexual jokes with their 'brother'—a recurrent kin-role licensed teasing.,"With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:40.917890+00:00
568,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,kin_role,grandfather,kin_role,kinship,Explicitly permitted recurrent sexual joking between a clansman and his 'grandfather'—institutionalized across generations.,"With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:40.917890+00:00
569,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,The text names 'grandchild' as a kin role with whom sexual jokes are permitted—patterned licensed familiarity.,"With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:40.917890+00:00
570,/Gusii_Levine_1959_972_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,clansman,kin_role,members of adjacent generations,kin_role,kinship,Adjacent generations are marked as abansoni—presence induces sexual shame and requires avoidance of sexual talk.,"With his “brother,” “grandfather,” and “grandchild,” he may make sexual jokes and discuss sexual matters, but with members of adjacent generations he must avoid all mention of sex. Persons of the latter group are said to be abansoni, in whose presence one experiences ensoni, sexual shame.",,abansoni,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:40.917890+00:00
571,/Gusii_Levine_1959_978_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,boys and young men,age_set,girls,age_set,within_group,"Recurrent, culturally patterned sexual joking between youths in marketplace courtship.","Each marketplace has its day of the week when activity is greatest and trading goes on; this is when girls dress up and go in groups to sell some family produce and be seen by boys. The boys and young men also attend, singly and in groups, looking for attractive girls. If she is friendly and laughs, however, the boy is encouraged and may begin some sexual joking. If the girl is favorably inclined to him she will respond in kind, usually using terms of obscene abuse.",sexual joking / courting teasing,,symmetric,teasing,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:14:49.098726+00:00
572,/Gusii_Levine_1966_162_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,Grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent, culturally patterned sexual joking and insults between grandparents and grandchildren.","Grandparents initiate sexual joking with young children, and insults, including sexual abuse, flow back and forth between them in a manner which is almost as relaxed as that of agemates. A child must never strike his grandparent, however, and he tends to obey the older person's imperious commands despite the surface egalitarianism.",sexual joking / jovial teasing,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:15:09.018022+00:00
573,/Gusii_Levine_1966_171_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,women accompanying her daughter,kin_role,mother (host) of the initiate / Bosibori,kin_role,within_group,"Ceremonial license for accompanying women to insult/annoy host mothers during initiation; patterned, expected behavior.","When the women accompanying her daughter arrived at Bosibori's house, they pulled up more than half of her potato garden, gorging themselves on raw potatoes and taking others with them. She became furious and tried to stop them, but they said, “This is a happy occasion; we can do anything, even annoy you.” This license is expected, and even when carried to excess, as at Bosibori's, it is not recognized as ground for legal action or any other kind of retribution.",ceremonial license to annoy / sanctioned misbehavior,,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,Tied to female puberty/circumcision rites; accompanists exercise licensed liberties against hosts.,2026-07-06T09:15:16.342660+00:00
574,/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,boys circumcised in the same year,age_set,boys circumcised in the same year,age_set,within_group,Age-cohort pals use recurrent joking abuse as a culturally recognized intimacy marker.,"Boys circumcised in the same year, who regard each other as “pals,” consider the joking abuse which they hurl at one another as the most important indication of intimacy.",pals,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:15:27.513625+00:00
575,/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Grandparent–grandchild interactions are described as recurrently playfully abusive, indicating institutionalized joking by kin role.",The interaction between grandparents and grandchildren can also be described as playfully abusive.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:15:27.513625+00:00
576,/Gusii_Levine_1966_187_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,young men courting girls,group,girls (being courted),group,within_group,"Courtship includes patterned teasing: young men jokingly threaten and girls return insults, showing a recurrent teasing institution.","Young men courting girls jokingly threaten them with forceful violation, and the girls roundly insult them in return.",,,mixed,teasing,0.85,"Roles differ: men threaten, women insult; culturally patterned within courtship context.",2026-07-06T09:15:27.513625+00:00
577,/Gusii_Levine_1966_33_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,grandchild,kin_role,grandparents (real or classificatory),kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent, reciprocal obscene insults and sexual joking between grandchildren and grandparents.","The rules governing relationships between clansmen not of adjacent generations require less restraint and involve less embarrassment; they lack the elements of sex avoidance and respect which characterize classificatory parent-child relations. This lack of restraint is particularly striking in relations between an individual and his real or classificatory34 grandparents. He may discuss sex and insult them jokingly. In fact, the characteristic pattern of interaction in this relationship involves obscene insults, humorous inquiries about sexual activity, and deprecation of the other's sexual competence.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:15:37.109971+00:00
578,/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,his parents-in-law,kin_role,his parents,kin_role,kinship,Text states the two sets of parents carry on a joking relationship; this is kin-based affinal joking.,"They may not enter the house of their son-in-law. They do, however, carry on a joking relationship with his parents.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:15:54.943571+00:00
579,/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,a Nyansongo man,kin_role,his wife's brothers and sisters,kin_role,kinship,"Author describes an egalitarian joking relationship between a man and his wife's siblings, including sexual joking with sisters.","In like manner, a man has an egalitarian, joking relationship with his wife's brothers and sisters; with the latter, elaborate sexual joking is carried on.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:15:54.943571+00:00
580,/Gusii_Levine_1966_52_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,bride's affinal group,group,groom's affinal group,group,kinship,The wedding ritual involved reciprocal insulting between the two affinal groups—an institutionalized joking/insult exchange.,"It included an additional payment of cattle, aggressive interaction between the affinal groups in the form of wrestling matches, dancing contests, and reciprocal insulting of the bride and groom, as well as the placing of anklets of iron on the bride to indicate her married status.",,,symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,Extracted as affinal-group ritual insulting during wedding; coded kinship because it is between affinal kin groups.,2026-07-06T09:15:54.943571+00:00
581,/Gusii_Levine_1966_64_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,women,group,men,group,within_group,"Recurring, culturally expected lighthearted/boisterous behavior by women contrasted with men's solemn ritual role.","In ceremonies conducted by men, women often take a lighthearted attitude which is recreational for them and inconsistent with the seriousness of the males in charge. ... The female tendency to be boisterous in a formal ritual setting is also exhibited during the leading-in ceremony of the male initiation rites ... Women are expected to be highly expressive and even obstructive on such occasions.",women's lighthearted/boisterous behavior versus men's solemn ritual role,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,"Gender categories treated as recurring, culturally patterned licensed liberties during male ceremonies.",2026-07-06T09:16:03.461487+00:00
582,/Gusii_Levine_1979_12_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Text states grandchildren habitually visit and joke with their grandparent, indicating a recurrent kin-based joking tie.","After menopause, in her forties, she should have circumcised grandchildren, and her primary interests are focused on her son's and daughter's children, some of whom will live with her, visit her, joke with her.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:16:14.704389+00:00
583,/Gusii_Levine_1980_89_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,omoηina,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Text states grandchildren visit and joke with their omoηina, a recurring grandparent–grandchild role relationship.","After menopause, in her later forties, she should have circumcised grandchildren, and her primary interests are focused on her sons' and daughters' children, who will visit and joke with her and perhaps live with her.",visit and joke with her,omoηina,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:16:23.527513+00:00
584,/Gusii_Levine_1982_53_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,maternal grandmother,kin_role,grandson,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes mother's mother as the recurrent, culturally recognized joking partner for her grandson.","the maternal grandmother who is known in Gusii lore as the primary joking partner for her grandson and the one who calls her daughter's children by their praise-names, that is, the names they are called only at their mother's home and which connote so much warmth and affection that informants report their use brings tears to their eyes.",primary joking partner,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:16:30.611658+00:00
585,/Gusii_Levine_1994_130_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,husband's family,kin_role,woman in labor / new mother,kin_role,kinship,Recurring hazing and hostile joking by husband's family toward women in labor; kinship-based in‑law practice.,"Those women who were on good terms with their husband's family would probably be hazed only at first birth, regardless of whether their bridewealth had been paid; those not on good terms with their husband's family would be hazed even on their third and fourth births if bridewealth had not yet legitimized their unions. ... The Gusii women we observed in labor endured this hazing without overt response. ... the hostile laughter and joking sexual abuse that accompany it.",hazing,,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.8,"Text frames hazing/joking as recurring, socially patterned during childbirth tied to bridewealth and in‑law relations.",2026-07-06T09:16:37.947316+00:00
586,/Gusii_Levine_1994_218_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,mother,kin_role,child / infant,kin_role,kinship,Mothers use joking threats toward infants; children treat maternal threats as nonserious—a patterned mother–child teasing relation.,"Sometimes mothers seem to be joking in their threats, and as children get older, they appear not to take them seriously: In one observation, for example, a 30-month-old girl asks her mother for a banana. When the mother says, “Go and get one,” the girl accuses her mother of having eaten her banana; the mother defends her authority by saying, “Today your uncle will beat you hard,” whereupon the little girl says, “I'll run away.”",maternal joking threats / playful threats,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,"Text frames this as a widespread, recurrent pattern in child socialization rather than a one-off incident.",2026-07-06T09:16:45.796762+00:00
587,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_15_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,esimbore singers,group,Kipsigis,ethnic group,cross_group,Song during initiation includes ritualized taunts naming Kipsigis; recurrent communal singing suggests institutionalized teasing between groups.,"When one or two groups of novices have been dealt with, the circumciser leads off the singing of the esimbore. ... The song enumerates, as will be seen, things unmentionable for initiated males, except as deliberate or joking insults.
...
Orwane Sigisi, orwane Sigisi! Fight the Kipsigis, fight th",,esimbore,unclear,teasing,0.7,Text lists neighbouring groups in ritual song as targets of exhortation; treated as recurrent ritualized taunts.,2026-07-06T09:16:59.811659+00:00
588,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_15_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,esimbore singers,group,Abasuba,ethnic group,cross_group,Ritual esimbore song names Abasuba in exhortations; recurrent singing implies institutionalized cross-group taunting.,"When one or two groups of novices have been dealt with, the circumciser leads off the singing of the esimbore. ... The song enumerates, as will be seen, things unmentionable for initiated males, except as deliberate or joking insults.
...
Orwane Bosoba, orwane Bosoba! Fight the Abasuba, fight the",,esimbore,unclear,teasing,0.7,Same ritual context as the Kipsigis taunt; esimbore singers repeatedly perform these lines.,2026-07-06T09:16:59.811659+00:00
589,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,the lads,group,the novice (omoisia [uncircumcised boy]),kin_role,within_group,"Recurrent, ritualized teasing of novices by older boys during initiation ceremonies.","As he chokes and splutters, they laugh and shout, and beat him with sticks torn from the bower: ‘Eat, eat, you omoisia [uncircumcised boy]!’ To cry at this stage carries no disgrace comparable to that of flinching during circumcision.",,omoisia,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Patterned, collective teasing within initiation ritual; 'lads' function as a ritualized teasing group.",2026-07-06T09:17:10.753913+00:00
590,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_19_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,'tamer',person,the novice (omoisia [uncircumcised boy]),kin_role,within_group,Named ritual officer performs painful humorous rite on novice as part of initiation sequence.,"Now the ‘tamer’ catches the penis firmly in a leaf, the inside of which has been coated with a paste of leaf-ash and water. The ash is caustic; the resulting pain is ‘a really nasty one’, making the novice cry out in agony.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Ritualized action accompanied by laughing and entertainment; role ('tamer') institutionalized in ceremony.,2026-07-06T09:17:10.753913+00:00
591,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_22_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,novices,age_set,girls,group,within_group,Recurrent culturally sanctioned teasing event where girls provoke novices (arousing desire).,"‘Arousing Desire.’ During the first few days after esubo, at which time the wounds of circumcision have not thoroughly healed, parties of girls come to the seclusion huts ‘to trouble the novices’ (as the Gusii say) by what is called ‘arousing desire’ (ogosonia). Removing all their clothes, they dance around the novices in provocative attitudes and challenge them: ‘You boy, you said you would become a young man [omomura] and have intercourse with girls; now, are you ready? Here it is — what you wanted. Come Come and copulate!’ ... The boys do their best to joke in reply.",arousing desire,ogosonia,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:17:17.570780+00:00
592,/Gusii_Mayer_1952_27_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,mature women,group,novice's mother,kin_role,within_group,Formalized playful insults by mature women toward the novice's mother during girl's seclusion—recurrent ceremonial teasing.,"The leading of a girl into seclusion follows the same broad pattern as that in the case of a boy, but some extra episodes are interpolated. In these episodes mature women take part. Their action consists in formalized attacks on and insults directed against the novice's mother. However,28 these are playful or joking insults: the tone is of high good humour.",playful insults,,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:17:24.548128+00:00
593,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_32_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,tatamoke,kin_role,korera,kin_role,kinship,Text describes licensed bawdy joking tied to specific kinship terms/roles between these categories.,"‘I always used to call Anyiega my tatamoke omoamate (small father in the clan). But now that his second son has married a daughter of the half-brother of my second wife Nyanchama, I am entitled to call Anyiega korera (parent of spouse of child). And now when Anyiega wants to joke bawdily in my presence he calls me korera, though at other times, as my tatamoke, he behaves to me with restraint and will not enter my house.’",bawdy joking / licensed familiarity,tatamoke; korera,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:17:33.051019+00:00
594,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,small father in the clan,kin_role,child in the clan,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent reciprocal etiquette (familiar behaviour, licensed liberties) between 'small father' and 'child' roles.","The general ‘finding out how to call one another’ does more than fix reciprocal etiquette patterns between two people. When a ‘small father in the clan’ and his ‘child in the clan’ meet in company, e.g. at someone else's beer drink or ceremony, one will find himself allotted to the group which behaves familiarly with the host, the other to the group which behaves familiarly with the host's son, or father.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:17:47.388707+00:00
595,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,brother/cousin (momura ominto),kin_role,brother/cousin (momura ominto),kin_role,kinship,"Example shows two men 'finding out' to call each other brother/cousin (momura ominto), enabling familiar behaviour (use of stool, joking).","The two men had never met before, but proceeded to ‘find out’ that they were to ‘call each other brother/cousin’ (momura ominto). This showed that the stool could be used; it was offered and accepted.",,momura ominto,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:17:47.388707+00:00
596,/Gusii_Mayer_1965_33_KS,EasternAfrica,Gusii,caller (guest),kin_role,old woman called 'grandmother' (magokoro),kin_role,kinship,"Describes a man, after 'finding out', addressing an old woman as 'grandmother' (magokoro) and thus engaging in familiar contact (handshake).","After a lengthy preliminary process of ‘finding out’ he stepped forward and shook her hand, explaining for our benefit that ‘it seems I must call her “grandmother” (magokoro)’.",,magokoro,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Caller is a guest who, by applying the kin-term, is licensed to familiar physical contact and presumably teasing/familiar speech.",2026-07-06T09:17:47.388707+00:00
763,/Luguru_Adem_2020_7_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Each clan,clan,a joking partner from another clan,clan,within_group,Text states clans have recurring partnered joking relationships used in mortuary rites; intra-societal clan pairs.,"Each clan had a joking partner from another clan who would be called to conduct certain mortuary rites, usually involving sweeping the floor of the deceased’s hut, washing the corpse and wrapping it in a burial shroud (sanda), and proclaiming the circumstances of death;",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,Document names no specific clans; recorded as generic clan↔clan institutionalized joking partnership tied to mortuary duties.,2026-07-06T09:32:44.620408+00:00
764,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Luguru,ethnic group,Mbunga,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly states Luguru have joking relations with Mbunga; cross-community pairing.,"Joking relations exist between Luguru and certain tribal groups such as the Mbunga, Pogoro, Zaramo and Nyamwezi;",utani or ugonga,utani or ugonga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:01.928256+00:00
765,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Luguru,ethnic group,Pogoro,ethnic group,cross_group,Document lists Pogoro among tribal groups with institutional joking ties to Luguru.,"Joking relations exist between Luguru and certain tribal groups such as the Mbunga, Pogoro, Zaramo and Nyamwezi;",utani or ugonga,utani or ugonga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:01.928256+00:00
766,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Luguru,ethnic group,Zaramo,ethnic group,cross_group,Text names Zaramo as a tribal group with institutionalized joking relations with Luguru.,"Joking relations exist between Luguru and certain tribal groups such as the Mbunga, Pogoro, Zaramo and Nyamwezi;",utani or ugonga,utani or ugonga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:01.928256+00:00
767,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Luguru,ethnic group,Nyamwezi,ethnic group,cross_group,Nyamwezi explicitly listed as having joking relations with Luguru; inter-community institution.,"Joking relations exist between Luguru and certain tribal groups such as the Mbunga, Pogoro, Zaramo and Nyamwezi;",utani or ugonga,utani or ugonga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:01.928256+00:00
768,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,some Luguru clans,clan,some Luguru clans,clan,within_group,Text describes patterned joking and ritual/burial partnerships between Luguru clans.,they also exist between some Luguru clans which provide certain ritual and burial services for one another;,,utani or ugonga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Document does not name specific clans; quoted phrase used verbatim as entity.,2026-07-06T09:33:01.928256+00:00
769,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_28_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,classificatory crossc2adcousins,kin_role,classificatory crossc2adcousins,kin_role,kinship,Document explicitly states joking relations occur between classificatory cross-cousins (kin-based).,and they exist between classificatory cross␀c2␀adcousins.,,utani or ugonga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:33:01.928256+00:00
770,/Luguru_Biedelman_1967_33_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,matrilineage,lineage,clan joking partners (watani),clan,within_group,Text describes recurring ritual roles of 'watani' as clan joking partners with matrilineages in burials and propitiation.,Burial and mortuary rites are conducted by clan joking partners (watani). ... The rites (kutambika) are directed by the head of the matrilineage but some are conducted in part by joking partners (watani).,watani,watani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:11.857442+00:00
771,/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,linked sub-clans,clan,linked sub-clans,clan,within_group,Text describes ugongo/utani as a system of joking relationships between linked sub-clans.,"Another aspect of the social structure, which must be mentioned is the question of ugongo (Luguru), utani (Swahili), a system of joking relationships between linked sub-clans which occurs all over the culture area",ugongo / utani,ugongo; utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:26.156998+00:00
772,/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,clans (and tribes),clan,clans (and tribes),clan,cross_group,Author links utani to joking relations between clans and tribes (inter-group partners across corporate units).,"Utani in Swahili means a joking relationship , in which the partners are called mtani, plural watani. This relationship is called ugongo in Luguru, and the partners are termed mgongo, plural wagongo.",utani (between clans and tribes),utani; ugongo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Treated as inter-community (clan/tribe) joking; author cites wider area comparisons.,2026-07-06T09:33:26.156998+00:00
773,/Luguru_Brain_1971_822_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,cross-cousin (mtani),kin_role,cross-cousin (mtani),kin_role,kinship,The term mtani denotes a cross-cousin and is discussed in relation to joking behavior.,"But here the problem arises. The term for a cross-cousin in Luguru is mtani. Naturally many watani will also be wagongo, and so we are left with the puzzle - are cross-cousins called watani because one behaves towards them in a joking fashion, or is the relationship referred to as utani between clans (and tribes) because one behaves as one would towards a cross-cousin?",,mtani; watani; wagongo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Text links kin-role term mtani with clan-level joking; coded as kinship joking between cross-cousins.,2026-07-06T09:33:26.156998+00:00
774,/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,sub-clan,clan,sub-clan,clan,within_group,Utani/ugongo (wagongo) are institutionalized joking ties between neighbouring sub-clans within Uluguru.,"It must be fairly clear that in Uluguru, where all the sub-clans live in contiguous areas of relatively fertile land in the mountains, each sub-clan will have such ties to other such units on all sides of it, these ties being regarded in a variety of ways. On the one hand wagongo are cousins, but they are also affines (cf. Fox 1967, LEACH 1961 and NEEDHAM 1962).",ugongo / utani (wagongo),ugongo; utani; wagongo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:41.807349+00:00
775,/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,children of a man,kin_role,son of father's sister,kin_role,kinship,The son's of a father's sister are labeled mtani (joking partner) vis-à-vis the man's children; this is a named kin joking tie.,"She has important functions to play with respect to her brother's daughter at the time of the latter's puberty ceremonies. Since she is the mother of her brother's heir, her son is termed “father” by her brother's children after the brother's decease, although before that time he is given the term of mtani, which, as we have already noted, also means joking partner.",mtani,mtani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Entity phrasing follows text: 'her son' (father's sister's son) and 'her brother's children'.,2026-07-06T09:33:41.807349+00:00
776,/Luguru_Brain_1971_823_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,man,kin_role,younger brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,The ethnography states men have a joking relationship with their younger brothers' wives—an institutionalized affinal joking tie.,"On the contrary, elder brothers are treated with extreme respect, as are their wives, whereas in the reverse direction we find that men have a joking relationship with younger brothers' wives.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:41.807349+00:00
777,/Luguru_Brain_1971_832_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,elder brothers,kin_role,younger brothers' wives,kin_role,kinship,Text states elder brothers have a recurrent joking relationship with their younger brothers' wives.,"younger brothers call their elder brothers sekuru “grandfather”, and have to treat their wives with respect, whereas elder brothers have a joking relationship with their younger brothers' wives.",joking relationship,sekuru,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:33:50.050896+00:00
778,/Luguru_Brain_1978_178_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mgongo,clan,wagongo,clan,kinship,Text identifies ugongo as an institutionalized joking tie between linked/clan marriage partners (cross-cousin marriage).,"Ugongo is an institutionalized joking relationship between linked clans which are conceptualized as being the exact structural equivalents. ... A man frequently marries an mgongo, who should at the same time be a cross-cousin. ... Rites for the spirits, tambiko, can only be performed with the assistance of the joking partners from the linked clan, the wagongo, symbolically indicating the interdependence and complementarity of the two structurally equal and opposite groups.",ugongo (utani),ugongo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,mgongo denotes the spouse (linked) clan; wagongo denotes the joking partners from that linked clan.,2026-07-06T09:34:00.395062+00:00
779,/Luguru_Brain_1980_372_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,wagongo,clan,mgongo,clan,within_group,Text describes ugongo as an institutionalized joking relationship between linked clans (wagongo/mgongo).,"Ugongo is an institutionalized joking relationship between linked clans which are conceptualized as being the exact structural equivalents. ... one frequently marries a mgongo, who should at the same time be a cross-cousin.",ugongo (Swahili utani),ugongo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Document uses wagongo (joking partners) and mgongo (the preferred marriage/joking partner); coded as clan-level within-group institutional joking.,2026-07-06T09:34:09.566661+00:00
780,/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,initiands,age_set,joking partners,group,within_group,"Text describes recurring, institutionalized teasing role between initiands and their joking partners.","On one occasion a large adolescent boy, one of the initiands' joking partners, was carried round clowning the part, to laughter from the crowd.",joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:34:17.856703+00:00
781,/Luguru_Brain_1980_378_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,kindred,kin_role,joking partners,group,kinship,Text shows recurrent social practice where kindred and joking partners share ceremonial food and licensed teasing.,"On one occasion cooked rice and catfish were handed round to the kindred and joking partners, who all ate together, men and women, standing up, simulating great haste.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Kindred treated as kin corporate unit; joking partners distinct social role.,2026-07-06T09:34:17.856703+00:00
782,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1315_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,clan,mtani,clan,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, culturally recognized utani joking ties between Luguru clans (watani/mtani).","Every Luguru clan claims to have a utani relationship, involving joking and obligations, with one or more clans in their vicinity. ... The greatest degree of joking is carried on between watani of the same generation, and cross-sexual joking is common, with obscene and suggestive language and horseplay.",utani (ugongo),utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Document does not list specific clan names; uses local terms watani (pl.) / mtani (sing.).,2026-07-06T09:34:25.074143+00:00
783,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,kin_role,watani,kin_role,kinship,Text states joking is permitted between watani across generations as a culturally patterned relation.,"While a Luguru should show deference toward an elder, joking is permitted between all generations of watani, and the only avoidance observed is toward the mother-in-law.",watani joking,watani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:34:35.029689+00:00
784,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1316_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,son-in-law,kin_role,his wife's clan,clan,kinship,Man marrying into wife's clan will have been joking with that clan for years; culturally patterned joking with affinal clan.,"For one thing, a man is likely to be marrying into the clan of his father or grandfather, but, in any case, he will have been joking with his wife's clan for years.",,mtani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Document uses term 'mtani' for preferred marriage partner; extracted as son-in-law ↔ wife's clan.,2026-07-06T09:34:35.029689+00:00
785,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mtani elder,kin_role,young mtani,kin_role,kinship,Text describes permitted reciprocal joking between alternate/contiguous watani generations (elder and youth).,"A high degree of familiarity is permitted between alternate generations, and it is permissible for a youth to deride a mtani elder if at some time during the exchange he shows the proper respect. For example, a elder named Mbewa ... approached the house of a young mtani and the latter started calling to those around to “kill the rat, kill the rat.”",watani / utani,watani/utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:34:49.762044+00:00
786,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Author explicitly notes patterned bantering and joking between grandparents and grandchildren.,"One type of bantering and joking that is not limited to watani is that between grandparents and grandchildren. The kinship terminology permits alternating generations to refer to each other as “brother” or “sister,” and a young man may tease his grandfather about marrying his wife.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:34:49.762044+00:00
787,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1317_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani clan(s) of the deceased,kin_role,clan of the deceased,clan,kinship,"Watani are described as having expected, privileged joking liberties directed at the clan of the deceased during funerals.","Considerable horseplay by the watani clan or clans of the deceased is permitted and expected at a burial. ... They have been known to pour a pot of water over a wailing woman under these circumstances. Or again, a mtani might say to the clan of the deceased: “You are crying too much. Give me one woman to take to my house so that I can give you a child to resemble the person who is dead.”",,watani,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Privilege is one-sided: watani exercise liberties toward the deceased's clan.,2026-07-06T09:34:49.762044+00:00
788,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1318_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mtani,person,watani,group,within_group,"Text describes culturally sanctioned, recurrent appropriation rights by watani/mtani at funerals and daily life.","It is also permissible to appropriate the possessions of watani, but rather definite rules govern this type of acquisition. For one thing, it should be done openly and in the presence of the owner or his family. Confiscation is most likely to occur at a funeral when watani of the deceased may walk off with a goat, fowl, yams, or even such items as a hoe or panga.",appropriation rights of watani/mtani,watani/mtani,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,mtani (singular) used in text as actor; relationship is a role-based joking appropriation between watani (neighbors) and owners/households.,2026-07-06T09:34:58.718735+00:00
789,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,unmarried cross-cousins,kin_role,unmarried cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states cross‑cousins (watani) have permitted sexual joking and liberties; clearly kinship‑based.,"The greatest amount of sexual license is permitted between unmarried cross-cousins, and an affair between these two is rarely condemned except by acculturated Christians. Cross-cousins are always considered watani and may be referred to as such whether or not their clans observe the utani relationship.",watani / sexual license,watani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:35:09.182001+00:00
790,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1319_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani (male),kin_role,watani (female),kin_role,kinship,Author explicitly describes joking between watani permitting suggestive/obscene comments used to probe partner availability.,The joking between watani that permits highly suggestive or obscene comments thus may be utilized to ascertain the availability of a mtani as a sexual partner.,joking between watani,watani,symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:35:09.182001+00:00
791,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1320_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,kin_role,clan,clan,kinship,Text explicitly identifies watani as joking partners with clans and duty-bearers at funerals.,"The function of the watani, in addition to the joking noted above, is assisting the clan in preparation of the body and interment. ... The watani clan would be offended if they were not notified, for in addition to the slight, it would deprive them of their right to get even regarding confiscation of food.",utani/watani joking relationship,utani/watani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:35:17.709412+00:00
792,/Luguru_Christensen_1963_1321_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,watani,clan,clan members,lineage,within_group,Watani perform sanctioned teasing/rebuke of clan members during ancestor rituals; culturally patterned ritual role.,"During the prayers to the clan ancestors the watani may rebuke or coerce the dead, saying “You who have died, do not come back and cause trouble for the living,” or they may admonish the elders to carry out the ceremony in the prescribed manner so as not to offend the ancestors. Joking may enter in here again, for when the names of ancestors are mentioned that a mtani does not recall, he may enquire “Who are they?”",rebuke/teasing during ancestor ceremonies,watani,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:35:24.950962+00:00
793,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_236_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states cross-cousins are culturally recognized joking partners during ceremonies.,"No matter what happens or the kind of complaints that are delivered (these may be significant, especially since cross-cousins are joking partners), the person must sit without reaction.",joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:35:29.483077+00:00
794,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Lamba (rainmaker),person,adult woman joking partner,group,within_group,Rainmaker routinely accompanied by an adult woman joking partner in rituals; patterned institutional pairing within society.,"In a different area of Mgeta I was told that rainmaking did not involve children, but instead the rainmaker would be always be accompanied by an adult woman joking partner (there is a linkage between clans with preferential marriage and those in which joking relationships occur).",adult woman joking partner,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:35:38.796343+00:00
795,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_265_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Lamba (rainmaker),person,children joking partners (boys and girls),group,within_group,"Children are described as required joking partners assisting the rainmaker in tambiko rituals, indicating a recurrent institutional role.","It is widely agreed that while Lamba was accompanied by one or two adults who were joking partners, his main assistants in this tambiko were children. This was described as consisting of one boy and one girl, or two boys and two girls.",children joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:35:38.796343+00:00
796,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_278_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,lineage members,lineage,his joking partners,kin_role,kinship,Text describes lineage members eating food who were/not his joking partners — culturally patterned kin joking ties.,"He listed some of the possible mistakes that this man made, bringing bread and tea for the ancestors to Mbagho's ritual space at Rudewa, as well as the traditional black sheep, having other lineage members eat the food who were not his joking partners.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Document does not name specific lineages or partner groups; extracts generic kin-based 'joking partners' institution mentioned.,2026-07-06T09:35:44.765583+00:00
797,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,women,kin_role,husbands,kin_role,kinship,"Recurrent, culturally patterned jokes by women about 'posho' given to their husbands; gendered kin-role teasing.",One hears many jokes made by women regarding the “food rations” (posho) that women distribute to their husbands.,,posho,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:35:56.672386+00:00
798,/Luguru_Gemignani_2002_280_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,mother-in-law,kin_role,daughter-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Specific anecdote shows patterned teasing between mother-in-law and daughter-in-law about sexual/food matters.,"Mama Mwenda told a funny story about how her mother-in-law once asked her if she was going home to give “food” to Baba Mwenda. When she asked, “What food?” her mother-in-law stated, “the food that you close in a satchel and walk around with everyday”",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Mother-in-law is the jokester in the cited anecdote; implies an institutionalized in-law teasing pattern.,2026-07-06T09:35:56.672386+00:00
799,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_15_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,kigori (young girl),kin_role,the family,group,kinship,Text explicitly says the family establishes a recurring joking relationship with the kigori role; clearly kinship socialization.,As a young girl (between 9–13) before reaching puberty she was referred to as “kigori”. The family would establish a joking relationship with her while at the same time she would be socially nurtured towards attaining womanhood.,,kigori,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:36:06.461234+00:00
800,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Different hamlets in the neighborhood,village,Different hamlets in the neighborhood,village,within_group,Text states neighboured hamlets have recurring joking relationship called utani/ugongo.,"Different hamlets in the neighborhood have a joking relationship called “utani” or “ugongo”. These relationships used to be very important, much more than they are today.",utani / ugongo,utani; ugongo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Document gives no specific hamlet names; relationship is between neighboring hamlets within the Luguru area.,2026-07-06T09:36:27.627763+00:00
801,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,the Luguru,ethnic group,Wa-Mbunga,ethnic group,cross_group,"Explicitly lists Luguru joking relationships with Wa‑Mbunga, indicating institutionalized inter-ethnic joking.","The Luguru have joking relationship with several other ethnic groups including the Wa-Mbunga, Wa-Pogoro, Wa-Nyamwezi, and Wa-Sukuma. (Lucas, 1974).",utani / joking relationship,utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:27.627763+00:00
802,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,the Luguru,ethnic group,Wa-Pogoro,ethnic group,cross_group,"Explicitly lists Luguru joking relationships with Wa‑Pogoro, indicating institutionalized inter-ethnic joking.","The Luguru have joking relationship with several other ethnic groups including the Wa-Mbunga, Wa-Pogoro, Wa-Nyamwezi, and Wa-Sukuma. (Lucas, 1974).",utani / joking relationship,utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:27.627763+00:00
803,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,the Luguru,ethnic group,Wa-Nyamwezi,ethnic group,cross_group,"Explicitly lists Luguru joking relationships with Wa‑Nyamwezi, indicating institutionalized inter-ethnic joking.","The Luguru have joking relationship with several other ethnic groups including the Wa-Mbunga, Wa-Pogoro, Wa-Nyamwezi, and Wa-Sukuma. (Lucas, 1974).",utani / joking relationship,utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:27.627763+00:00
804,/Luguru_Hamdani_2001_4_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,the Luguru,ethnic group,Sukuma,ethnic group,cross_group,"Explicitly lists Luguru joking relationships with Wa‑Sukuma, indicating institutionalized inter-ethnic joking.","The Luguru have joking relationship with several other ethnic groups including the Wa-Mbunga, Wa-Pogoro, Wa-Nyamwezi, and Wa-Sukuma. (Lucas, 1974).",utani / joking relationship,utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:27.627763+00:00
805,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,clan,Wabunga,clan,within_group,Text explicitly names privileged familiarity/joking relationship between these two sibs.,"Between certain sibs there exists privileged familiarity or a joking relationship, as, for example, between the Wamangi and the Wabunga.",privileged familiarity / joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:39.016498+00:00
806,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_62_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,clan,Mbunga,clan,within_group,Reciprocal ritual/drum-playing and aid in ngoma described as recurring inter-sib relationship implying privileged familiarity.,"He considered it a useful instrument for his ngoma (band). Of course, in order to use it properly he had to call in the aid of the Wamangi sib, and thus it came about that the Wamangi formed part of the Wambunga ngoma. As a matter of fact, it is the Wamangi who play the drums when an Mbunga dies, and vice versa.",reciprocal drum-playing / ritual partnership,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Text links ritual cooperation with privileged familiarity; relationship framed as recurring practice.,2026-07-06T09:36:39.016498+00:00
807,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_63_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,clan,Wambunga,clan,within_group,Text names these two sibs as reciprocal watani/kutaniana—an institutional joking relationship within the society.,"The Wamangi are the watani of the Wambunga, and vice versa. ... Sibs between which privileged familiarity or the joking relationship prevails are said to kutaniana.",watani / kutaniana,watani; mtani; kutaniana,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:50.028381+00:00
808,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_64_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,Wamangi,clan,Wabunga,clan,within_group,Text describes recurrent inter-sib joking rights (watani) between Wamangi and Wabunga within the same society.,"As a matter of fact, it would seem that the chief pleasure in life for a Mangi is to have a laugh at the expense of an Mbunga or to ‘pull something over on’ an Mbunga,—the Wamangi and the Wabunga being, as we have mentioned, of sibs between which the joking relationship obtains. Such inter-sib watani may do or say whatsoever they like when there is question of their watani.",watani; joking relationship,watani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:36:59.202025+00:00
809,/Luguru_McVicar_1935_65_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,members of joking relationship sib's,clan,members of sibs of common origin,clan,within_group,Text explicitly identifies recurring 'joking relationship sibs' as institutionalized patterned ties within the society.,"I have heard it said that nowadays marriage is taking place between such members of joking relationship sib's and also between members of sibs of common origin, but I do not know how true this is, for it must be very new, and I am not acquainted with any specific instance.",joking relationship,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Document refers to 'joking relationship sibs' but does not name the specific sibs or give local term; coded as within-group clan-level joking.,2026-07-06T09:37:15.041608+00:00
810,/Luguru_Young_1960_44_KS,EasternAfrica,Luguru,clans,clan,reciprocating clans,clan,within_group,Text describes recurrent reciprocal joking (ugongo/utani) as institutionalized between clans within Luguru.,"A more important relationship between clans is that of ugongo (L.) or utani (S.), i.e. reciprocal or joking relationship. Not all clans possess a link in this manner, but where they do there is also a trend to preferential marriage between the two linked clans.",ugongo / utani (reciprocal joking relationship),ugongo; utani,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,"Document does not name specific clan pairs; institution described as recurrent reciprocal joking between clans, including triangular three-clan links.",2026-07-06T09:37:24.679607+00:00
811,/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,jogam,group,sisters of Adhiambo,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurring flirting/jokes between jogam and Adhiambo's sisters, a culturally patterned play relationship.","The food was eaten slowly, with some flirting between the jogam and the sisters of Adhiambo and some jokes passed back and forth with the brothers.",playmates / flirting,jogam,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,,2026-07-06T09:37:35.858226+00:00
812,/Luo_Cohen_1989_106_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,sisters of Adhiambo,kin_role,brothers,kin_role,kinship,"Text reports jokes exchanged back and forth between sisters of Adhiambo and the brothers, indicating patterned kin joking.","The food was eaten slowly, with some flirting between the jogam and the sisters of Adhiambo and some jokes passed back and forth with the brothers.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.5,Brothers are not explicitly tied to named kinline; coded as kin_role. The passage implies recurring joking but limited detail.,2026-07-06T09:37:35.858226+00:00
813,/Luo_Cohen_1989_108_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,the jogam,kin_role,Adhiambo's people,group,kinship,Text describes recurrent teasing during marriage negotiations between jogam and bride's people (in-law context).,"The record-player was brought back to the room and there was music and dancing, with a good deal of teasing and joking between the jogam and Adhiambo's people.",,jogam,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,,2026-07-06T09:37:42.354366+00:00
814,/Luo_Wilson_1961_100_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,brothers of the bride,kin_role,brothers of the groom,kin_role,kinship,Ritualized selection (DIERO) with mock battles and sanctioned taking of a beast between bride's and groom's brothers.,"35. The brothers of the bride have meanwhile been waiting patiently for the news that the bride has accepted the CHIENO, for now they gather their strongest and most skilled fighters from among their LIBAMBA and their neighbours to take the first head of cattle from the herd of the groom to their own village. This group is known as the JODIERO, the selectors, and they go to select a beast from the herd of the groom. The brothers of the groom naturally resist the selection and the two groups wrestle and fight mock battles with sticks until both parties agree on the beast to be taken.",DIERO / JODIERO,DIERO; JODIERO; RWADH DIER,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:38:03.598207+00:00
815,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,JONDARIA,group,the young men of the groom's village,age_set,within_group,Text describes recurrent flirtation and joking between JONDARIA and groom's young men.,During the rest of the day is a period of flirtation and joking between the JONDARIA and the young men of the groom's village.,JONDARIA,JONDARIA,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:38:19.061760+00:00
816,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,JONDARIA,group,the village of the groom,village,within_group,"Text states JONDARIA have culturally licensed liberties in the groom's village (enter granaries, take food).","On the other hand, some state that the JONDARIA have great licence in the village and enter the granaries and take anything edible they find which they cook and eat by themselves.",licensed liberties,JONDARIA,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.8,Licensed liberties granted to JONDARIA by host village; action is by JONDARIA so non-symmetric.,2026-07-06T09:38:19.061760+00:00
817,/Luo_Wilson_1961_95_KS,EasternAfrica,Luo,JONDARIA,group,the village (hosts),village,within_group,"Alternate, recurrent custom: JONDARIA are ignored and not given food—an institutionalized avoidance pattern.",Most Luo tribes state that the JONDARIA are ignored in the village and are not given food but must wait until their return the next day to their own village before they eat.,,JONDARIA,non_symmetric,avoidance,0.8,Different tribal variants present both licensed liberties and avoidance between same units; treated as separate institutional patterns.,2026-07-06T09:38:19.061760+00:00
820,/Maasai_Merker_1910_82_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,the assembled warriors,group,the parents of the boy,kin_role,kinship,Warriors collectively abuse parents when a boy shows pain—sanctioned teasing tied to circumcision kin roles.,"During the circumcision itself the parents of the boy remain in their hut, for if the latter moans or cries out from the pain, they are punished with abusive 83 63 cont. words and blows by the assembled warriors, because they did not rear their son to have the hardening and self-control necessary for a warrior, while the weakling is reviled for a long time afterwards as eb[unknown] iryo = coward, or t[unknown] or[unknown] on[unknown] o = the bad one.",abusive words and blows / reviling,,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.6,Text shows a culturally sanctioned pattern at circumcision festivals where warriors publicly admonish/abuse parents for sons' lack of stoicism.,2026-07-06T09:38:43.563122+00:00
821,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,manyata moran,age_set,elders,group,within_group,"Repeated, culturally sanctioned teasing of moran by elders/village watchers about adultery privileges.","The elders can only demand that visiting moran should behave as guests with an outward respect, and the moran are aware that their comings and goings are being carefully watched from a discreet distance.
...If a manyata moran stays in the elders' village area for more than the occasional night without manyata permission, he must account for himself on his return. ... he can expect to be teased.",teased by elders / watched and teased on return,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:38:58.265995+00:00
822,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,manyata women,group,manyata moran,age_set,within_group,Manyata women habitually take up a joking script to taunt moran who have stayed in elders' villages.,"When other manyata moran see him returning, they may call out: ‘The donkeys have come.’ The manyata women then take up the joke: ‘He's just a donkey, for he ran away to the elders' villages ... Let's grab him and drive him to get some water! Let's put a hide on his back and load him up!’",women teasing returning moran,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:38:58.265995+00:00
823,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_110_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,manyata moran,age_set,manyata moran (returning from elders' villages),age_set,within_group,"Peers repeatedly call out and mock comrades who have stayed with elders, a patterned teasing practice.","When other manyata moran see him returning, they may call out: ‘The donkeys have come.’",peer teasing / taunting,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:38:58.265995+00:00
824,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_121_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,father,kin_role,son,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes a recurrent, sanctioned teasing when fathers quarrel and sons must run away, creating joking.","If a father quarrels with his son and starts to shiver, the son should run away whatever his age, as if the father has raised his stick. The incident then becomes a joke at the expense of the younger man.",joke at the expense of the younger man,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:39:04.631024+00:00
825,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,firestick patrons,group,moran,age_set,within_group,"Recurring, ritualized harangues and sanctioned teasing between elders (patrons) and moran during eunoto.","The response of the patrons throughout the morning was similar to that described for all such occasions when they assemble to harangue the moran. ... During the breaks in moments of pique, certain patrons occasionally cracked their long whips at unsubdued groups of moran as though they were frisky cattle. The moran would dutifully retire to a safer distance, ‘running away’ from the elders' anger, although still sometimes giggling.",authoritarian ritual of counter-rebellion / harangue,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:39:23.774557+00:00
826,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_147_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,age mates,age_set,age mates,age_set,within_group,Patterned joking/support among an age-mate group after ritual conflict (entertaining with joking and good humour).,"As the elders dispersed, he remained with a small group of age mates, entertaining them with a display of joking and good humour. They encouraged him, rather as moran closely support an age mate after a bout of shaking.",supportive joking among age-mates,,symmetric,banter,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:39:23.774557+00:00
827,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,firestick patrons of Terito age-set,age_set,moran,age_set,within_group,Repeated mock aggression and abusive joking by patrons toward moran at disbanding; age-set patron–moran institutional ritual roles.,"Their firestick patrons of Terito age-set systematically coerced them from this defiant stand. ... Then, striding away from the meeting, the patrons left the moran with little option other than to rush up with fistfuls of grass, forcing the patrons to recant, but at the same time submitting to the inevitable.",patron–moran mock aggression at disbanding ceremony,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Patrons exercise licensed mock aggression; institutionalized within ceremonial context (enoonkureeta).,2026-07-06T09:39:37.558061+00:00
828,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,moran,age_set,manyata-mothers,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent joking abuse between moran and their mothers during ritual contexts.,"The abusive idiom of the patrons recalls the first forest feast when joking abuse between moran and their mothers was indirectly at the expense of their absent fathers, whom they were about to desert for the manyata.",moran–mother joking abuse,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:39:37.558061+00:00
829,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_164_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,firestick patrons of Terito age-set,age_set,manyata-mothers,kin_role,kinship,Patrons theatrically hurl mock curses and sexual abuse at manyata-mothers as recurrent ceremonial practice.,"The patrons are expected to come with long whips and an assortment of warrior headgear in the spirit of a burlesque on moranhood. ... they crack their whips as they approach the manyata, and hurl mock curses and accusations of incest. Their aim is to frighten the manyata-mothers who are less certain that these mock curses are harmless play.",patrons' mock curses at manyata-mothers,enoonkureeta,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Assigned ceremony term as local_term_raw because enoonkureeta frames the interaction.,2026-07-06T09:39:37.558061+00:00
830,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,patrons,group,moran,group,within_group,Elder patrons repeatedly perform sanctioned verbal abuse toward moran during disbanding ceremonies.,"Forty-nine firestick patrons cowl their heads with sheepskin caps, inkureeta, whence the name of the ceremony, enoonkureeta. Their abuses against the moran incorporate such phrases as: ‘become bitter; be finished off; become lost for ever; you have fucked your mothers when they denied us [this right]; I have come and hear and see that your mother has nothing left to fuck’.",disbanding ceremony abuses,enoonkureeta,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:39:45.711951+00:00
831,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_165_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,moran,group,mothers,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly notes joking between moran and their mothers at a named ceremony (first forest ox).,"As with the joking between moran and their mothers at the first forest ox (of-the-earplugs), the Matapato acknowledged but did not stress the element of joking abuse.",joking at first forest ox,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:39:45.711951+00:00
832,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_26_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,courier (clan ‘sister’ or the wife of an age mate),kin_role,the girl (prospective bride),kin_role,kinship,Repeated anointment ritual: women kin (couriers/age-mate wives) ritually tease the prospective bride.,"The suitor discreetly approaches one of the village wives to be his courier, perhaps a clan ‘sister’ or the wife of an age mate. In an unguarded moment, she calls over the girl and taking her by surprise, traces a smear of the butter from the bridge of her nose to her crown. Realising what has occurred, the girl is expected to rage. She snatches at leaves or grass or anything on hand to wipe off the butter, while the other women and even girls laugh at her.",anointment (esiret),esiret,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Institutionalized as repeated with each suitor and culturally proscribed (girl excluded from sharing gifts).,2026-07-06T09:39:53.716148+00:00
833,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,bride (new wife),kin_role,women closely related to her husband,kin_role,kinship,"Persistent, culturally patterned taunting of the bride by husband-related women during reception ritual.","She is left by herself outside this village, and is then greeted by the women, who come out to taunt her persistently until she breaks down in tears. They may tease her for being her husband's current favourite who will in her turn be discarded.",taunting during reception,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:40:05.356970+00:00
834,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_31_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,bride (new wife),kin_role,girls (younger women) of the husband's village,age_set,kinship,Younger women (girls) specifically participate in recurrent taunting of the incoming bride.,"She is no longer a girl and now even girls come out to taunt her. These are jokes that point up some of the sombre realities of wifehood, and mark the most radical transition in her life with a totally new role and surrounded by new faces.",taunting by girls,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:40:05.356970+00:00
835,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,those close to the initiate and others that have recently been circumcised,group,the initiate,kin_role,kinship,Text describes kin and recent initiates deliberately provoking the initiate in ceremony; role-centered kin-based teasing.,"Those close to the initiate and others that have recently been circumcised may deliberately try to provoke his anger, scorning and threatening him, and distancing themselves from him if he does flinch, but also firming his resolve not to do so.",provoking / scorning during circumcision,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:40:21.494798+00:00
836,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,a band of about ten initiates,age_set,the youth (the initiate) led from his mother's hut,kin_role,within_group,Describes initiates as a unit teasing and manipulating the newly circumcised youth during ritual—age-set patterned teasing.,"a band of about ten initiates, who had recently recovered from the operation and wore their full regalia, led the youth from his mother's hut, singing and holding each arm... They paused at certain points, singing and making pointed references to the fact that none of them had flinched.",initiates' teasing of new initiate,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:40:21.494798+00:00
837,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,The elders,group,smaller boys,age_set,within_group,Elders intentionally call over and tease smaller boys after the operation—festival-patterned teasing across generations.,"The elders then called over smaller boys to tease them, playfully dragging them towards the doorway and asking them if they would like to be circumcised.",teasing of younger boys,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:40:21.494798+00:00
838,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_73_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,some women,group,several young children,age_set,within_group,"Women seized children and hooted while simulating teeth-prising, a recurring playful emphasis on forthcoming ordeal.","Outside, catching this jocular mood, some women seized several young children. One of them started to prise out their lower incisors as if to emphasise to them the ordeal that awaited them, hooting with laughter while the children howled.",women's playful teasing of children,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:40:21.494798+00:00
839,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,novice moran,kin_role,fathers (family heads),kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurring feast joking where novice moran make fathers the butt of jokes; kin-role pattern.,"The various allusions are to the period when they will leave home and take their mothers to live in the manyata (their warrior village); and their fathers, who are conspicuously absent, are the indirect butt of the joke.",butt of the joke,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:40:30.647368+00:00
840,/Maasai_Spencer_1988_84_KS,EasternAfrica,Maasai,novice moran,kin_role,mothers,kin_role,kinship,Joking at the feast highlights sons' forthcoming control over their mothers (possessory teasing tied to manyata role).,"If mothers have sexual relations with their own fathers and with the moran friends of their daughters and come under the overbearing protection of their own sons and feed them, then their husbands - the fathers of the moran - are superfluous.",possessory teasing / control,manyata,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:40:30.647368+00:00
900,/Nuba_Faris_1989_166_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuba,danndanna (male),kin_role,danndanna (female),kin_role,kinship,"Text states sexual joking is appropriate with danndanna of the opposite sex, a recurrent kinship-based joking practice.","Sexual joking with danndanna of the opposite sex is appropriate, as it is an appropriate marriage category.",,danndanna,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Interpreted as patterned joking between opposite-sex danndanna (cross-cousin marriage category).,2026-07-06T09:46:38.958323+00:00
901,/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuba,a man,kin_role,his classificatory sister,kin_role,kinship,Text states men 'might even joke' with classificatory sisters about sexual dreams—recurring kin-based licensed joking.,"If a man dreamed in this way about his classificatory sister, his cousin, or possibly his brother's wife, he would think nothing of it and might even joke about it with the heroine of his dream.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:46:56.026266+00:00
902,/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuba,a man,kin_role,his cousin,kin_role,kinship,"Author reports men may 'joke' with their cousins about incest dreams—patterned, kin-based licensed teasing.","If a man dreamed in this way about his classificatory sister, his cousin, or possibly his brother's wife, he would think nothing of it and might even joke about it with the heroine of his dream.",,narane,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:46:56.026266+00:00
903,/Nuba_Nadel_1947_285_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuba,a man,kin_role,his brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,"Text indicates men may 'joke' with their brother's wife about erotic dreams—an affinal, recurrent joking license.","If a man dreamed in this way about his classificatory sister, his cousin, or possibly his brother's wife, he would think nothing of it and might even joke about it with the heroine of his dream.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:46:56.026266+00:00
915,/Nuer_Colson_1952_152_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,a man's wife,kin_role,her husband's brothers,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states licensed joking between a man's wife and her husband's brothers; clearly kinship-based.,"For a man's wife, avoidance is impracticable, but she treats her parents-in-law with great respect and, in the early days of marriage, keeps herself apart from them as much as possible. She is on joking terms with her husband's brothers since, among brothers, the wife of one is, in the social sense, the wife of all.",on joking terms,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:48:49.131065+00:00
916,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_261_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,ric (age-set members),age_set,ric (age-set members),age_set,within_group,"Text describes recurrent joking, play, and encouragement among members of the same ric (age-set).",Age-mates are on terms of complete equality. A man does not stand on ceremony with an age-mate but eats and plays and jokes with him as an equal.,,ric,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Institutionalized conviviality and teasing among age-mates; includes encouragement of boy fights as licensed play.,2026-07-06T09:48:55.468654+00:00
917,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1936_262_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,members of the same age-set,age_set,wife of a man of his own age-set,kin_role,within_group,Text states ritualized licensed liberties allowing age-mates to joke with a fellow age-mate's wife.,As we have seen at initiation feasts a man is permitted to joke and take liberties with the wife of a man of his own age-set.,take liberties / permitted to joke,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:49:04.456127+00:00
918,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1940_257_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,members of the same age-set,age_set,age-mates,age_set,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, culturally expected joking and play among members of the same age-set.","Members of the same age-set are on terms of entire equality. A man does not stand on ceremony with his age-mates, but jokes, plays, and eats with them at his ease.",comradeship; joking and play among age-mates,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:49:15.654740+00:00
919,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_100_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,mother-in-law,kin_role,son-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally patterned joking and licensed mirth between mothers-in-law and sons-in-law.,"The mother-in-law relationship among the Nuer is a subject of mirth, and the foulest slips of the tongue are attributed to mothers-in-law when talking to their daughters' husbands.
A man may even joke with his mother-in-law when there are several children.",mother-in-law relationship,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:49:23.433515+00:00
920,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,a man,kin_role,his wife's brother,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent obscene joking between husband and wife's brother after children are born, a kinship-linked licensed teasing.","After the birth of children a man and his wife's brother can, if great friends, jokingly curse one another in obscene language.",jokingly curse one another,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:49:33.063267+00:00
921,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_101_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,a man,kin_role,his wife's sister,kin_role,kinship,Husband may at any time joke and exchange abusive language with his wife's sister — a culturally patterned joking relationship.,The only in-law whom a man need not at any time treat with reserve is his wife's sister. He can joke with her and even exchange abusive language with her.,joke / exchange abusive language,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:49:33.063267+00:00
922,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_159_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,"gaatgwanlen, male ortho-cousins, the sons of brothers",kin_role,"gaatgwanlen, male ortho-cousins, the sons of brothers",kin_role,kinship,Text states male ortho-cousins (gaatgwanlen) have a culturally patterned joking/teasing relationship.,It is perhaps this contradiction between social bonds and personal feelings which gives rise to the joking or teasing relationship between ortho-cousins.,joking or teasing relationship,gaatgwanlen,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:49:42.693137+00:00
923,/Nuer_EvansPritchard_1951_39_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,nei ti gwagh,kin_role,sons (young men),kin_role,kinship,Text states sons may joke with 'nei ti gwagh' (unrelated people); a culturally licensed kin vs non-kin joking relation.,"The nei ti gwagh, the unrelated people, they can joke with and make love to without fear of censure or retribution, so long as they are not wives of other men, for fornication with an unrelated girl is not regarded with disapprobation.",licensed joking with unrelated people,nei ti gwagh,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Document excerpt frames joking permission as contrast with kin; actors interpreted as sons/young men taught by parents.,2026-07-06T09:49:51.499886+00:00
924,/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,legal representatives of the bridegroom,group,bride's family,group,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, ritualized night-long joking between these parties at marriage feast.","After the dancing is over, those who are not closely related to either party go home, but the legal representatives of the bridegroom and the bride's family and some of their close kinsmen, both male and female, go into a but specially prepared for them which is known as the dwil koam for the occasion. There they sit all night joking and conversing with their hosts until dawn.",dwil koam joking,dwil koam,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:50:12.914000+00:00
925,/Nuer_Howell_19xx_93_KS,EasternAfrica,Nuer,young people of the bridegroom's party,age_set,young people of the bride's party,age_set,within_group,"Ritualized, recurrent courting badinage sung and exchanged between the two parties' youths at marriage.","Another hut, known as the dwil twacni or dwil kwen, is set aside for the young people of both parties, and the bride and bridegroom, their gwan twac and ngi nyal, sit there with their friends and coevals singing and exchanging courting badinage all night through.",dwil twacni / dwil kwen courting badinage,dwil twacni; dwil kwen,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:50:12.914000+00:00
940,/NyakyusaandNgonde_Wilson_1977_170_KS,EasternAfrica,NyakyusaandNgonde,Nyakyusa,ethnic group,Ngoni,ethnic group,cross_group,Text states Nyakyusa and Ngoni were recurring joking partners at labour centres (institutionalized cross-group teasing).,"The Ngoni were spoken of as equals who, without question, could be joking partners of Nyakyusa at labour centres (M. Wilson 1957b).",joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:51:31.230424+00:00
941,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,members of his own generation,age_set,members of his own generation,age_set,within_group,Peers of the same generation have culturally licensed rude joking among themselves.,"Between a man and the members of his own generation considerable latitude is allowed. As the Dorobo put it, Kicupekei, kiurerencinkei, kiilencinkei, ‘Kumin kon, ngut kum kamet’, amu ki lakok. (“We use bad language to one another, we play together, we say to each other, ‘May your father lie with you, or may you lie with your mother’, because we are children”, i. e. we are all of the same age.)",licensed liberties,"Kicupekei, kiurerencinkei, kiilencinkei",symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:51:53.114085+00:00
942,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,brother,kin_role,sister,kin_role,kinship,Sibling pairs are described as allowed 'perfectly innocuous intimacy' including joking.,"Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:51:53.114085+00:00
943,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,macokor,kin_role,mama,kin_role,kinship,Macokor and mama (kin categories) are listed together as permitted to use joking and language among them.,"Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:51:53.114085+00:00
944,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,macokor,kin_role,kesenda,kin_role,kinship,Macokor and kesenda are named among kin roles permitted mutual joking within the same generation.,"Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:51:53.114085+00:00
945,/Okiek_Huntingford_1951_35_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,mama,kin_role,kesenda,kin_role,kinship,Mama and kesenda are paired in text as kin roles between whom joking and intimate language is allowed.,"Between the sexes of the same generation there may be a perfectly innocuous intimacy, between brother and sister, between macokor, mama, and kesenda, which allows any kind of joking and language but stops short of actual sexual intercourse;",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:51:53.114085+00:00
946,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_451_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,Okiek,ethnic group,Maasai,ethnic group,cross_group,"Text explicitly states recurrent joking characterizes Okiek–Maasai interactions, an institutionalized interethnic pattern.","Okiek are represented as trickster figures: cunning, greedy, unrestrained, and violators of accepted standards of social life and behavior in various ways.
Joking, apparent or real accommodation, and a certain diffidence characterize many Okiek interactions with Maasai, whatever the actual balance of goods, services, and reliance.",joking,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:52:02.881972+00:00
947,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_457_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,men,group,women,group,within_group,Recurrent joking between male and female ritual communities about hierarchy in ceremonies.,"Faced with the challenge of women's own corresponding ritual community and ceremonial organization, men sometimes try to introduce an evaluative hierarchy and male priority into the trial and secrets of initiation as well. Some may claim that their pain is greater, or that shared themes in secret songs were taken from them by women. However, these attempts are usually joking or rather half-hearted, in partial recognition of their absurdity.",attempts to introduce male priority (joking),,mixed,joking_relationship,0.8,"Text frames joking as recurrent, ritual-contextualized banter between men's and women's ritual communities.",2026-07-06T09:52:12.264207+00:00
948,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,men,group,women's house of ritual,group,within_group,Text names recurring 'joking attacks' by men directed at women's ritual house; intra-societal gendered ritual teasing.,"A certain ambivalence does emerge, however, as the situation highlights the contradiction and brings out the tension ordinarily defused by the dispersal of women. It emerges in the encouraging speeches made to girls, in side comments made during initiation ceremonies, and in joking attacks made by men on the women's house of ritual.",joking attacks,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:52:27.019541+00:00
949,/Okiek_Kratz_1990_458_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,girls (initiates),kin_role,audience (adults),group,within_group,"Pesenweek confessions are a ritualized, recurrent setting where adults publicly tease/initiate girls; culturally patterned teasing.","The confessions can be actuely embarrassing for initiates and uproariously funny for the audience. Girls are asked to describe times when they refused to help with work or to go on errands for adults, and to name those involved.",pesenweek confessional teasing,pesenweek,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:52:27.019541+00:00
950,/Okiek_Kratz_1991_832_KS,EasternAfrica,Okiek,children,group,adults,group,within_group,Pesenweek confession ritual produces recurrent teasing and scapegoating between children and adults.,"The child instigates a pesenta through disrespect and disobedience, but the actual threat originates in the adult's grudgeful anger. As pesenweek are told and then announced, blame shifts subtly from child to adult. ... it becomes a laughing matter. Adults are usually more than willing scapegoats, adding to the hilarity with other comments.",pesenweek confessional teasing,pesenweek,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:52:41.519898+00:00
962,/Shilluk_Howell_1946_26_KS,EasternAfrica,Shilluk,drummers,group,dancers,group,within_group,"Ritualized, recurrent signal-response producing mock terror and laughter between defined ceremony roles.","On each day, at the close of the ceremonies the drummers whisked the drums from their holes and rushed them out of the village. This was a signal to the dancers to disperse, and they did so with great speed and with mock terror. The spirit of the late reth was said to return on the drums being drawn, and hands were clapped by the onlookers as before.",mock terror signal-response,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,"Treated as a recurrent, culturally patterned joking/teasing interaction tied to funerary ritual roles (drummers vs dancers).",2026-07-06T09:54:22.571724+00:00
964,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_114_KS,EasternAfrica,Somali,the pastoralists of the woodland,regional group,"the Hawiyya tribes settled in the villages of the ""black land""",ethnic group,cross_group,Text states woodland pastoralists routinely joke about Hawiyya villagers; recurrent cross-group teasing.,"Thus the pastoralists of the woodland joke about the unexpected attitudes of the Hawiyya tribes settled in the villages of the “black land” of the valley of the W[unknown]ebi (B[unknown]adi ‘Add[unknown]a, M[unknown]olk[unknown]al, M[unknown]obil[unknown]en, etc.), tribes that have fewer scruples in some of their attitudes than the people of the woodland remaining more attached to the customs.",,saddehliya,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T09:54:53.791595+00:00
965,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_120_KS,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiye,ethnic group,M[unknown]obil[unknown]en,clan,cross_group,Proverbary mockery by Hawiye targets named group repeatedly; institutionalized inter-group teasing.,"“You should spend midday under a maray tree and at night ask a M[unknown]obil[unknown]en for hospitality!”

“The maray tree does not give shade because of its very scarce leaves, just as the M[unknown]obil[unknown]en are scarcely hospitable because of their notorious parsimony.",proverbary mockery,mâlin maráy kú härgál,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T09:55:17.005368+00:00
966,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_120_KS,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiye,ethnic group,Dínl[unknown]a Mant[unknown]an,lineage,cross_group,Ethnographer records proverb comparing Dinlä Mantan's cunning; recurring cultural proverb mocks that lineage.,"“The astuteness of the Dîhlä Mant[unknown]an and the ground under which one has dug are equally deep.”

