972,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_93_KS,EasternAfrica,Somali,"Hawiyya tribes (Hawâdlä, B[unknown]adi ‘Addä, G[unknown]al-[unknown]gä‘el, M[unknown]obil[unknown]en, D[unknown]a[unknown]ud, Hilli[unknown]bi, Murúsada)",group,Gälädi (Digil),clan,within_group,Poem ends with a joke about Gälädi and traverses jokes about multiple Hawiyya tribes—recurring internal tribal teasing.,"The middle stanzas are all devoted to Hawiyya tribes: [unknown] Haw[unknown]adlä, B[unknown]adi ‘Addä, G[unknown]al-[unknown]gä‘el, M[unknown]obil[unknown]en, D[unknown]a[unknown]ub, Hilli[unknown]bi, Murúsada. The last hemistich (minkäy ma koyto) imitates the dialect of the G[unknown]alädi.",vaunt/poetic mockery,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.75,Extracted as patterned intra-societal joking: poet's vaunt-poems target multiple named Hawiyya tribes and Gälädi (Digil).,2026-07-06T09:56:08.819794+00:00
973,/Somali_Cerulli_1959_96_KS,EasternAfrica,Somali,Hawiyya peoples,group,Murúsada,clan,within_group,Singer (Hawiyya) makes a recurrent joking stereotype about Murúsada; intra-Hawiye teasing.,The verse refers to the widespread belief that the Murúsada were werewolves; and thus here they seem to have stolen the ferocity of the wild beasts.,stereotyped as werewolves / mocked,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.6,,2026-07-06T09:56:31.455355+00:00
