755,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1993_235_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,older Bedouin women,group,"some Bedouins from the cities (""followers of the model"")",regional group,cross_group,Text describes recurrent mocking by older Bedouin women of urban 'followers of the model' as a cultural stance.,"Their reactions to the sanctimonious Egyptians—and now to some Bedouins from the cities, who were becoming, as they put it, “followers of the model,” meaning the life of the Prophet—have been mixed. The older women are not cowed. They argue... They resent being told that some of the ways they have demonstrated their devoutness are wrong.",mocking / making fun,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.7,"Group-level, recurring cultural mockery of urban Islamizing Bedouins; labeled in text as a common topic.",2026-07-06T09:31:35.713352+00:00
756,/LibyanBedouin_AbuLughod_1993_235_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,aunt (Dhahab),kin_role,niece (Kamla),kin_role,kinship,Aunt repeatedly teases niece in a culturally patterned family exchange (licensed mockery).,"One evening, having recited some poetry and told some traditional tales for my benefit, Kamla’s aunt Dhahab turned to her niece and asked, “Hey Kamla, have you given Lila any songs?” ... Her aunt mocked her, “What’s this? You’ve become pious?” Everyone laughed as the old aunt continued, “God’s blessings! God’s blessings! So, you’re joining the ‘Beard Family’?”",mocking / teasing,,unclear,banter,0.4,Anecdotal family teasing; included cautiously as kin-based licensed mockery though the text doesn't explicitly frame it as institutionalized.,2026-07-06T09:31:35.713352+00:00
757,/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,young men,group,father,kin_role,kinship,Text describes patterned joking by young men targeting others' fathers—an institutionalized kin-based teasing practice.,"In their joking relations, young men usually refer to eachother’s father as being a dog or a theif or a cheat, but none could refer to the mother.",joking relations,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:32:03.666281+00:00
758,/LibyanBedouin_Mohsen_1974_130_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,young men,group,mother,kin_role,kinship,"The text states a recurrent prohibition against referring to another's mother, indicating institutionalized avoidance in joking relations.","In their joking relations, young men usually refer to eachother’s father as being a dog or a theif or a cheat, but none could refer to the mother.",taboo / prohibition,,symmetric,avoidance,0.85,Prohibition presented as culturally enforced within joking interactions; extracted as avoidance relation.,2026-07-06T09:32:03.666281+00:00
759,/LibyanBedouin_Obermeyer_1973_265_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,Saadi,ethnic group,Marabtin,ethnic group,cross_group,"Text reports recurring derision/joking by Sa'adi toward Marabtin vassal tribes, a culturally patterned teasing relationship.","Since the time of Muhammad, the Bedouins have been considered notoriously bad Muslims. Today, the Bedouins tend to joke about their lack of zeal, and often point out that “holy men” are not to be trusted. This is seen in Saadi behavior toward the Marabtin tribes who are their vassals. Theoretically, the Marabtin who trace descent to the Prophet and might be considered “sacred Tribes” should be respected by the “secular” Saadi tribes. But the Saadi of al-Qasr tend to deride the Marabit.",deride / joke about,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:32:14.922962+00:00
760,/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_201_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,sister’s son,kin_role,mother’s brother,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent, culturally permitted teasing/licensed liberties between these kin roles.","With the mother’s brother a man enjoys all the freedoms he is denied in his relationships with his father and father’s brother. He is permitted to joke with him; they indulge in exchanges of obscenities. ... Small boys pursue their mother’s brother, feign to kick or strike him, and he takes it all in good part.",The sister’s son-mother’s brother relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:32:24.034658+00:00
761,/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_71_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,men,kin_role,those linked through women (maternal relatives / affines),kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally recognized rights of men to joke with people via links created by women (maternal/affinal ties).,"Women, the marriage links they create, the forms of relationships that derive from
marriage, the right of men to joke with others 72 by virtue of a link through
women, their rights to demand favours of men who are not their agnates, their
right to expect contributions to bridewealth or blood money from maternal
relatives — all these, and other forms of behaviour, have been dealt with as
patterns of behaviour which interfere with or disturb agnation, or that cut
across lineage affiliations.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,Text frames joking as a recognized right tied to marriage links and maternal relatives; explicit named local term not provided.,2026-07-06T09:32:31.818085+00:00
762,/LibyanBedouin_Peters_1990_72_KS,NorthernAfrica,LibyanBedouin,mother's brother,kin_role,sister's son,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurrent, culturally patterned joking between mother's brother and sister's son.","Their reasoning, however, is that relationships with the mother’s brother are characteristically easy, that they are not demanding, that there is much joking in them, and that to kill within this range is, in terms of the general pattern of behaviour, stupid.",characteristically easy relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:32:37.182621+00:00
904,/Nubians_AlKatsha_1978_183_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,the groups of women,group,the groups of men,group,within_group,"Recurring, ritualized joking by gendered groups during post-wedding ṣabāhiyya ceremony.","While the “opening of the mouth” ceremony was going on, much joking and loud laughter issued from both the groups of women and men. Sometimes, peepholes had been made into the bridal quarters so that watchers could report to the group how long the girl was able to bargain and thus delay consummation.",ṣabāhiyya / 'opening of the mouth' joking,ṣabāhiyya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T09:47:07.223485+00:00
905,/Nubians_Callender_1971_50_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,fiancé,kin_role,engaged girl,kin_role,kinship,"Recurrent, culturally patterned teasing around forbidden contact between engaged partners (kin roles).","Whatever the rationale underlying this prohibition, most men treated the custom as a joke and plotted to evade the rule. This often turned into a game, with some girls trying to help him while others warned his fiancée.",forbidden contact treated as a joke,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:47:17.995004+00:00
906,/Nubians_Callender_1971_61_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,boys and young men,age_set,the groom,kin_role,kinship,Recurrent wedding teasing where youth force groom to stand; tied to nuptial role and collective rights.,"After the feast, boys and young men teased the groom by forcing him to stand. One of them walked up to him and offered a standard greeting, Salaama aleykam, ya arts. Greeted thus, he rose to his feet to respond properly, and could not sit again until the greeter told him to do so.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Institutionalized at weddings; violation led to formal tribal court and light sanction (pigeons).,2026-07-06T09:47:26.592431+00:00
907,/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,groom,kin_role,bride,kin_role,kinship,Agilbaygit is a ritualized contest of teasing between husband and wife at marriage.,"Agilbaygit means literally “opening the mouth.” Most Kenuz seemed to view the transaction in these terms. Like the groom's opening his wife's shogga on their wedding night, it was regarded as a contest. A man might try to trick his wife into speaking by asking her how much money she wanted, and she had been warned to respond to any questions with gestures. A woman who spoke prematurely was teased.",agilbaygit (opening the mouth) contest,Agilbaygit,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:47:39.094028+00:00
908,/Nubians_Callender_1971_66_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,women,group,women,group,within_group,Women form a patterned joking audience and make standard jokes about agilbaygit among themselves.,"This practice was also a common source of amusement and the subject of standard jokes among women, such as speculating about how much agilbaygit a man might give before he realized that he had married a woman who was dumb.",standard jokes among women,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Extracted as patterned intra-sex joking category rather than isolated remarks.,2026-07-06T09:47:39.094028+00:00
909,/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,the women charged with bringing a bride to her husband,group,the groom,person,within_group,Ritualized prank: attendants substitute a boy for bride as patterned pre-sebu horseplay targeting the groom.,"Joking and horseplay were also prominent features of the days before the sebu. The women charged with bringing a bride to her husband on the night after their wedding sometimes substituted a boy, whom they had wrapped in a shogga.",substitution prank / horseplay,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:47:55.880176+00:00
910,/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,young men,group,the groom,person,within_group,Recurring teasing: young men steal slippers or spy; groom must pay pigeons ransom — patterned licensed teasing.,"Young men used to climb up to push these aside and spy on the pair. They also tried to steal the couples' slippers, which were left outside the door at night—noiselessly slipping up to the door for this purpose, or standing outside the courtyard wall with a fishing rod and hook. If they succeeded, the groom had to give them pigeons as ransom before they would return the slippers.",slipper-theft ransom / spying,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:47:55.880176+00:00
911,/Nubians_Callender_1971_67_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,women,group,newly-married couples,group,within_group,Patterned eavesdropping and teasing of newlyweds before sebu described as prominent ritualized horseplay.,"Women eavesdropped on newly-married couples, particularly on their first night, trying to learn how much time elapsed before the bride spoke, and what her first words were.",eavesdropping / teasing,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:47:55.880176+00:00
912,/Nubians_Fernea_1991_190_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,Nubians,ethnic group,their employers,group,cross_group,"Text describes recurrent ""inside"" jokes by Nubian workers about employers, a patterned teasing between defined social units.","Working as servants, surrounded by non-Nubians, Nubians spoke their own language, a practice which, like the employment itself, was both stigmatizing and at the same time offered a degree of independence. Thus Nubians could discuss their work, criticize their employers and share “inside” jokes, which provided them with a sense of superiority over others, even while their language marked them as barabra.",inside jokes,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,"Brokers/elite employers also mentioned (""elites of Egypt, native and foreign""), but primary patterned joking attributed to Nubian workers.",2026-07-06T09:48:05.507836+00:00
913,/Nubians_Kennedy_1977_40_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,the two families,lineage,the two families,lineage,within_group,Text describes a recurrent mock-battle between the groom's and bride's families as ritualized joking.,"Dancing recommenced near sunset, the two families involved in the wedding each slaughtered a sheep, and still another procession moved from the groom's to the bride's house amid great shouting, gunshot, and drumming. (In earlier times there was at this point a mock battle between the two families in which the groom and his escort were thrashed with whips.)",mock battle between the two families,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Entities recorded as 'the two families' because text frames the ritualized mock battle as between the groom's and bride's families; considered a within-society institutionalized joking practice.,2026-07-06T09:48:17.285330+00:00
914,/Nubians_Kennedy_1977_55_KS,NorthernAfrica,Nubians,Nubians,ethnic group,Europeans,ethnic group,cross_group,"Document says Kanuba men habitually joke about other nationalities, specifically Nubian men joking about Europeans; cross-group patterned teasing.","Indeed, the men of Kanuba joke about the sexual propensities of other nationalities and ethnic groups and say that Nubian men and European women make good partners because the sexual desires of each are strong compared with their counterparts in their own groups.",joke about sexual propensities,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:48:23.427495+00:00
963,/Shluh_Hatt_1993_452_KS,NorthernAfrica,Shluh,am[unknown]ar,group,men who 'want to be' am[unknown]ar (the am[unknown]ar manque9),group,within_group,"Recurrent, assembly-wide teasing targets men aspiring to be am[unknown]ar; patterned licensed joke within the society.","Invariably, my question was announced to the assembly and the comment made that “no, he isne isn't an am[unknown]ar , but he wants to be one”. This, needless to say, provoked great rounds of laughter, and most of all from the am[unknown]ar manqu␀e9 .","""but he wants to be one"" teasing",,non_symmetric,teasing,0.6,Document uses damaged term am[unknown]ar; treated as local leadership category. No other institutional pairs in text.,2026-07-06T09:54:38.824619+00:00
1027,/Tuareg_Lhote_1944_148_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male president,group,ahal attendees,group,within_group,Presidents are culturally authorized to inflict jocular forfeits on meeting participants.,"When everyone has arrived, they elect a male president and a female president, whose job is to see that the usages and customs of the ahal are respected and, as a joke, to inflict forfeits on those who bring on themselves the recriminations of the others.",inflict forfeits as a joke,ahal,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.75,Presidential office sanctions teasing/license to impose forfeits on attendees during ahal meetings.,2026-07-06T10:02:07.150208+00:00
1028,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states recurrent joking relationship specifically between cross-cousins.,"The kinship system in Ahaggar is different from that in Air (fig. 8–9), but the rules of kinship behaviour are very much the same. Joking relationship between cross-cousins, and joking and avoidance between in-laws are practised also in Ahaggar.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:02:28.951226+00:00
1029,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,in-laws,kin_role,in-laws,kin_role,kinship,Author reports recurring patterns of both joking and avoidance specifically between in-laws.,"The kinship system in Ahaggar is different from that in Air (fig. 8–9), but the rules of kinship behaviour are very much the same. Joking relationship between cross-cousins, and joking and avoidance between in-laws are practised also in Ahaggar.",joking and avoidance,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Includes both joking and avoidance; coded as joking_relationship per task instructions.,2026-07-06T10:02:28.951226+00:00
1030,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġouh-en-Tehele,clan,noble clans,clan,within_group,Text names Aġouh-en-Tehele as having recurring joking ties with noble clans (class-based within society).,Other vassal clans and in particular the Aġouh-en-Tehele have joking with noble clans and also with clans of the Ise□emaren class.,joking with,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:02:28.951226+00:00
1031,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_106_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġouh-en-Tehele,clan,Ise□emaren,clan,within_group,Text explicitly records a recurring joking relationship and patterned gift-exchange between these named clans.,Other vassal clans and in particular the Aġouh-en-Tehele have joking with noble clans and also with clans of the Ise□emaren class. Between the Aġouh-en-Tehele and the Ise□emaren clans a fixed rule of gift-exchange is observed when they visit each other.,joking with; fixed gift-exchange,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Includes prescribed gift-exchange as part of the joking relationship.,2026-07-06T10:02:28.951226+00:00
1032,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states institutionalized joking exists between cross-cousins.,Finally cross-cousin marriage is considered the best type of marriage because institutional joking exists between cross-cousins.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:02:45.958012+00:00
1033,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,brothers-in-law,kin_role,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Author states joking occurs between brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law as an institutional pattern.,"Among the Kel Ferouan joking between cross-cousins goes together with joking between brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and also with avoidance between children-in-law and parents-in-law.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:02:45.958012+00:00
1034,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_89_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,kin_role,mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,Text reports institutional joking between a man and his mother's brother's wife in most Kel Ferouan clans.,It is also interesting that in most Kel Ferouan clans there is institutional joking between a man and his mother's brother's wife.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Mother's brother's wife may later become mother-in-law, at which point avoidance replaces joking.",2026-07-06T10:02:45.958012+00:00
1035,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly compares joking to that of cross-cousins, indicating a recurrent kin joking institution.","A joking relationship similar to that of cross-cousins and of brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferouan group.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:17.627561+00:00
1036,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,"Author states joking similar to that of brothers and sisters-in-law, a recurrent in-law joking pattern.","A joking relationship similar to that of cross-cousins and of brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferouan group.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:17.627561+00:00
1037,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,parents-in-law,kin_role,children-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states parents-in-law and children-in-law will avoid each other (institutionalized avoidance).,"These terms are always used reciprocally, and parents-in-law and children-in-law will avoid each other accordingly.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:17.627561+00:00
1038,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,noble Tuareg,group,vassals of the Kel Ferouan group,group,within_group,"Joking between noble Tuareg and their vassals is presented as a recurrent, institutionalized practice within drum-groups.","A joking relationship similar to that of cross-cousins and of brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferouan group.",,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.95,Joking reciprocal but drum-chief could stop joking; conditional on belonging to the same drum-group.,2026-07-06T10:03:17.627561+00:00
1039,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,drum-chief,person,vassals of the Kel Ferouan group,group,within_group,"Drum-chief and vassals engage in joking but chief can cease it, showing institutionalized asymmetric power in the joking relation.","Like other noble Tuareg he would not only associate with the vassals on intimate terms, but he would also joke with them. ... although joking was reciprocal it is said that the drum-chief could stop joking, in which case the vassals would “be afraid of him”.",,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Office/role-based joking; chief's power can suspend joking.,2026-07-06T10:03:17.627561+00:00
1040,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1959_96_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,vassals of the Kel Ferouan group,group,noble Tuareg of the Kel Fadei,group,within_group,"Text states absence of joking and presence of avoidance between these named groups, an institutionalized inter-group relation.",Thus there would be no joking between vassals of the Kel Ferouan group and noble Tuareg of the Kel Fadei. In this latter case there would be avoidance or strict reserve.,,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:17.627561+00:00
1041,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Dag □ali,clan,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states Dag □ali only have joking relationships with cross-cousins.,"People of the Dag □ali to-day have no joking relationships with anyone beyond cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law (cf. p. f., ff.).",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1042,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Dag □ali,clan,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly lists brothers and sisters-in-law as Dag □ali's joking partners.,"People of the Dag □ali to-day have no joking relationships with anyone beyond cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law (cf. p. f., ff.).",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1043,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Ise□emaren,group,noble Tuareg,group,within_group,Most noble Tuareg are said to joke with the Ise□emaren class across the drum-group.,"Most noble Tuareg and vassals will joke with the Ise□emaren, and there is a particular strong joking relationship between the vassal tribe Aġuh-en-tehlé and the Ise□emaren tribes.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1044,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Ise□emaren,group,vassals,group,within_group,Text states vassals will joke with Ise□emaren; fixed gift-exchange noted for visits.,"Most noble Tuareg and vassals will joke with the Ise□emaren, and there is a particular strong joking relationship between the vassal tribe Aġuh-en-tehlé and the Ise□emaren tribes.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1045,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġuh-en-tehlé,clan,Ise□emaren,group,within_group,"Aġuh-en-tehlé is described as having a particularly strong joking relationship with Ise□emaren tribes, including gift rules.","Most noble Tuareg and vassals will joke with the Ise□emaren, and there is a particular strong joking relationship between the vassal tribe Aġuh-en-tehlé and the Ise□emaren tribes. Such joking relationships are functionally related to joking relationships between relatives and in-laws and a fixed gift-exchange is observed between Aġuh-en-tehlé Tuareg and Ise□emaren Tuareg when they visit each other.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Gift-exchange (dates/meat) is part of the institutional tie.,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1046,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Aġuh-en-tehlé,clan,Kel □ela (noble Kel □ela Tuareg),group,within_group,Aġuh-en-tehlé has the most intense joking relationship to the noble Kel □ela; shamfights mentioned between vassals and nobles.,The Aġuh-en-tehlé tribe has the most intense joking relationship to the noble Kel □ela Tuareg of all vassals. These vassals and the noble Kel □ela engage in shamfights (tebbillant) exactly in the same way as cross-cousins.,,tebbillant,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1047,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_402_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,vassals,group,Kel □ela (noble Kel □ela Tuareg),group,within_group,Text describes vassals and noble Kel □ela engaging in shamfights as a form of joking relationship.,These vassals and the noble Kel □ela engage in shamfights (tebbillant) exactly in the same way as cross-cousins.,shamfights,tebbillant,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:03:45.460850+00:00
1048,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,noble Tuareg,caste,vassals of the Kel Ferwan group,caste,within_group,Text describes a culturally recognized adélen joking tie between nobles and vassals within Kel Ferwan.,"A joking-relationship (adélen) related to that of cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferwan group.",adélen,adélen; address as abubaz (ibubazen),mixed,joking_relationship,0.95,"Drum-chief could unilaterally stop joking, producing asymmetry in practice.",2026-07-06T10:04:13.369699+00:00
1049,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,all Tuareg,ethnic group,Imeggedezen,ethnic group,cross_group,Author states joking occurs between Tuareg and the Imeggedezen as a recurrent social practice.,"This is true also of present day joking between noble Tuareg and vassals, and between all Tuareg and various black-skinned people such as the Imeggedezen and even slaves.",,abubaz (ibubazen),symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:04:13.369699+00:00
1050,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,all Tuareg,ethnic group,slaves,caste,within_group,Text explicitly mentions recurrent joking between Tuareg and slaves as a patterned social relation.,"This is true also of present day joking between noble Tuareg and vassals, and between all Tuareg and various black-skinned people such as the Imeggedezen and even slaves.",,abubaz (ibubazen),symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:04:13.369699+00:00
1051,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_429_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Author links adélen joking to cross-cousin and brother/sister-in-law relationships (kinship-based joking).,"A joking-relationship (adélen) related to that of cross-cousins and brothers and sisters-in-law, including sham-fights and minor “thefts”, existed between all noble Tuareg and all vassals of the Kel Ferwan group.",adélen,adélen; address as abubaz (ibubazen),symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Text treats these kin categories as models for the joking institution.,2026-07-06T10:04:13.369699+00:00
1052,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,certain vassal tribes,clan,tribes of the Ise□emaren class,clan,within_group,Text states institutional joking occurs between vassal tribes and Ise□emaren tribes within Tuareg society.,"If on the other hand two tribes have institutional joking with one another, as is the case with certain vassal tribes in relation to tribes of the Ise□emaren class, there will also be joking between their slaves.",institutional joking,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:04:33.787734+00:00
1053,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,Tuareg,ethnic group,slaves,caste,within_group,"Author describes recurrent, culturally patterned joking relationships between Tuareg and their slaves.","In spite of the kinship pattern on which the relationships of Tuareg slaves and slave-owners is based, it is most noteworthy that there is always latent joking between them, as there is between many different classes and castes in Tuareg society. On certain occasions a Tuareg slave owner will joke freely even with his young or older slaves, though he will never joke with his children who have to show their father the outmost respect.",joking relationship,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:04:33.787734+00:00
1054,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_445_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,noble Tuareg,patronym,slaves,caste,within_group,"Text notes nobles may joke with slaves (less inclined), indicating a patterned joking tie between nobles and slaves.",The noble Tuareg may also joke with slaves but seem less inclined to do so than Tuareg of other classes.,,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Entity 'noble Tuareg' coded as patronym/upper-class segment; could also be 'group'.,2026-07-06T10:04:33.787734+00:00
1055,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,true cross-cousins,kin_role,classificatory cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states a named joking relationship between true and classificatory cross-cousins permitting sexual teasing.,"There is a joking relationship (tehan□e□it in Ahaggar, adélen in Ayr) between all true and classificatory cross-cousins. This joking relationship allows free talk about sexual matters, and when cros-cousins tease each other they frequently refer to matters of sexual life, e.g., “You sleep with old slave women!”",tehan□e□it / adélen,tehan□e□it; adélen,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:04:45.901648+00:00
1056,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_454_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,brothers-in-law,kin_role,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Author explicitly states same joking type exists between brothers- and sisters-in-law in both regions.,The same type of joking exists between brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law in Ahaggar and in Ayr.,,tehan□e□it; adélen,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Local terms given earlier apply to this same joking type.,2026-07-06T10:04:45.901648+00:00
1057,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,mother's brother's wife,kin_role,husband's sisters' children,kin_role,kinship,Text describes institutionalized adélen joking in Ayr where mother's brother's wife teases these children.,For in Ayr there is an institutional joking relationship (adélen) with the wives of mother's brothers including the spouses of the classificatory mother's brothers. This type of joking is similar to that of cross-cousins. The initiative for joking is taken by the mother's brother's wife who begins to tease her husband's sisters' children even when they are babies.,adélen (institutional joking relationship),adélen,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Initiator role described but children later retaliate; classified as kinship-based institutional joking.,2026-07-06T10:04:57.344613+00:00
1058,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_455_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,"Author states joking of adélen is similar to that of cross-cousins and earlier mentions cross-cousins can take/tease each other's property, indicating recurrent relation.","This type of joking is similar to that of cross-cousins. ... In addition, cross-cousins, as mentioned above, can take each other's property.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Cross-cousin joking implied as mutual; author links it to adélen practice in Ayr.,2026-07-06T10:04:57.344613+00:00
1059,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_456_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,a man inheriting his parents' kinship status,kin_role,classificatory cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states men adopt parents' joking relationships with classificatory cross-cousins as a rule of kinship behaviour.,"it is normal for a man to inherit the kinship status of his parents on their death, in the way that he adopts their joking relationships with their classificatory cross-cousins, but he will never inherit a joking relationship with the true cross-cousins of his deceased father and mother.",joking relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:05:05.427034+00:00
1060,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_459_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male cross-cousin,kin_role,female cross-cousin,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes a recurrent, institutionalized joking relationship between cross-cousins of both sexes.","The institutionial joking relationship between cross-cousins is considered an important institution in preventing conflicts between married partners. ... There is no doubt at all that cross-cousins of both sexes are extremely good friends. They do not joke just to annoy each other, and when joking they laugh and feel very pleased.",institutional joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:05:12.792609+00:00
1061,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly describes an institutionalized joking relationship between cross-cousins facilitating marriage.,"Parallel cousins are termed brothers and sisters, while there is a particular term for cross-cousins and between them an institutional joking relationship which facilitates contact and marriage.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:05:30.263147+00:00
1062,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,people in terejjek,group,each other,group,within_group,Joking during the culturally named courtship period (terejjek) is described as always occurring between lovers.,"The Tuareg seem to admit that one function of joking between cross-cousins is to promote marriages between them, and they say that in the period during which a man and a woman are in love with each other before marriage — a period which is known to the Kel Ferwan Tuareg as terejjek — there will always be joking between them even though the two persons are unrelated.",terejjek (courtship) joking,terejjek,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Actors are lovers during courtship, culturally patterned joking despite lack of kin ties.",2026-07-06T10:05:30.263147+00:00
1063,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_465_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,person,kin_role,brothers and sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,"Author states joking routinely occurs between a person and their brothers/sisters-in-law, often continuing from cross-cousin ties.","When a man marries a cross-cousin, his cross-cousins will become brothers and sisters-in-law with whom joking continues, and there will always be joking with the brothers and sisters of a spouse unless a man marries a particular kinswoman (cf. p. ).",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:05:30.263147+00:00
1064,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male cross-cousin,kin_role,female cross-cousin,kin_role,kinship,Text states institutionalized joking between cross-cousins of different sex; clearly kinship-based.,"In case of cross-cousins of different sex, the difference in age does not seem to diminish joking between them, and it is most interesting that the Kel Regulation Of Ferwan Tuareg have an institutional joking relationship in the case of a Marriage mother's brother's wife (cf. p. ).",institutional joking between cross-cousins,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:05:54.259630+00:00
1065,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,kin_role,mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,Author describes a specific institutional joking relationship between a man and his mother's brother's wife tied to marriage rules.,it is most interesting that the Kel Regulation Of Ferwan Tuareg have an institutional joking relationship in the case of a Marriage mother's brother's wife ... it seems as if joking between a man and his mother's brother's wife serves the purpose of preventing conflicts and creating a bond of friendship between a man and his future mother-in-law.,institutional joking with mother's brother's wife,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:05:54.259630+00:00
1066,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,kin_role,brothers-in-law,kin_role,kinship,"Text states that when marrying outside blood, a man practices joking with his brothers-in-law; an institutional in-law joking tie.","If a man marries a woman with whom he has no blood relationship he will practise joking with his brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and avoidance towards his parents-in-law.",joking with brothers-in-law,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:05:54.259630+00:00
1067,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,man,kin_role,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Same passage identifies joking between a man and his sisters-in-law when marriage is not with a kinswoman.,"If a man marries a woman with whom he has no blood relationship he will practise joking with his brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and avoidance towards his parents-in-law.",joking with sisters-in-law,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:05:54.259630+00:00
1068,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_466_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,parents-in-law,kin_role,children-in-law,kin_role,kinship,"Author describes a general, fixed system of joking and avoidance between in-laws (parents- and children-in-law).","Joking and avoidance between Tuareg in-laws no doubt constitute a fixed system of behaviour adapted to a particular marriage type ... he will practise joking with his brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, and avoidance towards his parents-in-law.",joking and avoidance between in-laws,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Covers both joking and reciprocal avoidance patterns between parents- and children-in-law described in text.,2026-07-06T10:05:54.259630+00:00
1069,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_468_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,husband's family,kin_role,wife's family,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent joking and avoidance between the two families as in-laws after divorce.,"When two people divorce they continue to have each other's relations as in-laws with whom they have joking and avoidance exactly as if they were still married, and the children continue to have their full rights within both families.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:06:04.331414+00:00
1070,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_470_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,the fathers of the groom,kin_role,the fathers of the bride,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states an institutional joking relationship between the two fathers (cross-cousins/brothers-in-law).,"the fathers of the groom and the bride are cross-cousins and/or brothers-in-law, and consequently there is an institutional joking relationship between them.",institutional joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:06:10.654956+00:00
1071,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,male cross-cousin (groom),kin_role,female cross-cousin (bride),kin_role,kinship,Text states groom and bride who are cross-cousins had an established joking relationship before marriage.,Until their marriage the groom and the bride had a joking relationship to one another.,joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:06:30.853519+00:00
1072,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,husband,kin_role,mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,The text describes an existing joking relation between husband and his mother's brother's wife that changes at marriage.,In Ayr the husband changes his joking relations with mother's brother's wife into avoidance.,,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Joking prior to marriage becomes avoidance afterward; relation asymmetry implied by change.,2026-07-06T10:06:30.853519+00:00
1073,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,newly-married husband,kin_role,brothers-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Author states joking is adopted with brothers-in-law only after marriage for in-laws.,"To other brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, joking is adopted only after marriage.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Text groups brothers- and sisters-in-law together; extracted husband↔brothers-in-law as representative.,2026-07-06T10:06:30.853519+00:00
1074,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_474_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,newly-married wife,kin_role,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Author indicates joking with sisters-in-law is adopted after marriage for in-laws.,"To other brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, joking is adopted only after marriage.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Extracted wife↔sisters-in-law as counterpart to husband↔brothers-in-law.,2026-07-06T10:06:30.853519+00:00
1075,/Tuareg_Nicolaisen_1963_475_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,mother-in-law,kin_role,wife,kin_role,kinship,"Text states mother-in-law ↔ wife relation is joking (does not switch into avoidance in Ayr), a recurrent kinship pattern.","There will then already be a relationship of respect between husband and wife, a respect relationship to brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law which continues unchanged, and in Ayr the relationship to the mother-in-law does not switch from joking into avoidance.",joking (vs avoidance),,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,The passage implies a culturally patterned joking/avoidance norm between mother-in-law and daughter-in-law; in Ayr it remains joking.,2026-07-06T10:06:38.394553+00:00
1076,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,newly-married daughter,kin_role,mother-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent jokes and demands between daughters-in-law and mothers-in-law in household conflicts.,"So the verses encapsulate the contemporary conflict between economics and descent, and division between rich and poor nobility. A man loses his material wealth in the long run, and also his self-respect, if he doesn't please his mother-in-law through gifts. Yet official authority figures such as mothers-in-law and marabouts are also the butt of jokes and vengeful fantasies; they are put down indirectly in verbal art of folktales, possession songs, and riddles. The daughter is caught in the middle of the cross-fire between, first her suitor, the marabout, and her parents, and later, between her husband and her mother. She and her mother feed the husband, it is true, but the latter is nonetheless subjected to continual demands for millet and household implements from his mother-in-law, almost at whim. The newly-married daughter cannot please both her new husband and her mother.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Mother-in-law is described as recurring butt of jokes; daughter-in-law reciprocally enmeshed but power asymmetry implied.,2026-07-06T10:07:05.223208+00:00
1077,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,women,group,marabouts,caste,within_group,Women use songs and gossip to mock and criticize marabouts; this is a recurrent cultural practice.,"It also expresses a resentment toward authority figures (marabouts and noble men), who command official respect and yet do not always fulfill their obligations toward women. ... Yet official authority figures such as mothers-in-law and marabouts are also the butt of jokes and vengeful fantasies; they are put down indirectly in verbal art of folktales, possession songs, and riddles.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Marabouts are an occupational/ritual category; joking is performed by women via song and gossip.,2026-07-06T10:07:05.223208+00:00
1078,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_40_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,women,group,imalayaden,group,within_group,Songs target and mock young suitors (imalayaden); women collectively use verbal art to shame them.,"It says, in effect, “Women, your problems are irritating, but nothing in comparison to the real problem: male outsiders in your tent.” The term used here for young men, imalayaden, is pejorative; it connotes those who deceive women and make false promises to marry, using misleading economic claims to do so.",,imalayaden,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Collective female mockery of a named category of young men is culturally patterned in song.,2026-07-06T10:07:05.223208+00:00
1079,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_41_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,potential marriage partners (cross-cousins),kin_role,kinship,Text states culturally patterned joking occurs between cross-cousins who are potential marriage partners.,"One is not supposed to express anger directly to anyone, but in particular, in respect toward affines and in joking with cross-cousins, potential marriage partners.",joking with,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:07:15.090379+00:00
1080,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,future affines,kin_role,groom,kin_role,kinship,Wedding scene describes licensed joking between groom and his future affines using a joking language.,"The groom asked his sister where the bride was in Tagenagen because he did not wish to be understood by everyone present; the spectators included many future affines, toward whom he would soon practice extreme respect, but with whom he now could joke.",,Tagenagen,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:07:33.472840+00:00
1081,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,women,group,men,group,within_group,"Text states men dread women's public ridicule, indicating recurrent, socially powerful taunting by women toward men.","On the other hand, men dread women's ridicule because they hold power over men's personal esteem in public reputation as proclaimed in poetry and song, as well as the power to throw them out of the domestic tent.",ridicule,,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.7,,2026-07-06T10:07:33.472840+00:00
1082,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1991_43_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,blacksmiths,caste,other Tuareg (general community),group,within_group,"Blacksmiths are described as free from constraints of reserve and dignity, implying licensed liberties in speech toward others.","All Tuareg, except blacksmiths who are free from constraint of reserve and dignity, attempt to measure words, and avoid commenting about everything happening around them.",licensed liberties,,unclear,banter,0.5,Text implies patterned freedom to speak (including teasing) but does not give explicit joking-pair examples.,2026-07-06T10:07:33.472840+00:00
1083,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,nobles,group,smiths,caste,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, ritualized joking between nobles and smiths at weddings; intra-societal social segments.","Next, men of diverse origins approach the circle of singing smiths, each offering money, tea, sugar, and millet, while smiths mock each social segment. The men perform dances identified with different social strata; for example, noble men do a dance called “Foot, do nothing,” as smiths lament, “feet without shoes,” and sing “I do not want jealousy of slaves or smiths.”",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:07:51.825022+00:00
1084,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,nobles,group,former slaves,group,within_group,Ritualized mutual self-debasement and joking between nobles and former slaves at marriage ceremonies.,"This ritual mutual and self-debasement evokes servitude yet simultaneously separates and brings together the different social segments... Joking and familiarity are practiced between nobles, smiths, and former slaves, who are not supposed to intermarry.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:07:51.825022+00:00
1085,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,smiths,caste,former slaves,group,within_group,Ethnographer reports smiths and former slaves engage in patterned joking within wedding rituals among social segments.,"The different social segments come together genially to exaggerate their affection... Joking and familiarity are practiced between nobles, smiths, and former slaves, who are not supposed to intermarry.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:07:51.825022+00:00
1086,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cross-cousins,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states joking is practiced between cross-cousins as potential marriage partners.,"But joking is also practiced between cross-cousins, who are potential marriage partners, and in certain contexts smiths and former slaves are said to be “like cousins to nobles.”",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,entity_b_raw uses 'each other' to indicate the reciprocal kin-role pair of cross-cousins (patterned joking among cross-cousin relations).,2026-07-06T10:07:51.825022+00:00
1087,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_1992_353_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,smiths,caste,"nobles (""like cousins to nobles"")",group,within_group,"Author notes smiths and former slaves are described as 'like cousins to nobles', indicating a culturally patterned joking/familiar tie.","But joking is also practiced between cross-cousins, who are potential marriage partners, and in certain contexts smiths and former slaves are said to be “like cousins to nobles.”",like cousins,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,This encodes the special framed familiarity; symmetry unclear from text whether equal or asymmetrical.,2026-07-06T10:07:51.825022+00:00
1088,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,nobles,caste,smiths,caste,within_group,Text describes a culturally recognized joking relationship between nobles and smiths within Tuareg society.,"Noble violence against smiths is traditionally rare because they are a protected people against whom it is not appropriate to express anger without denigrating oneself. In my early field research in the 1970s and 80s, many Tuareg described smiths as ‘like women … also like cousins (who practise a joking relationship and also may potentially marry) … you must not get angry at them, but (rather) joke with them’.",protected people / joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:08:04.884473+00:00
1089,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2004_328_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,cousins,kin_role,cousins,kin_role,kinship,Author states cousins practise a joking relationship and may potentially marry — a recurrent kinship joking tie.,"many Tuareg described smiths as ‘like women … also like cousins (who practise a joking relationship and also may potentially marry) … you must not get angry at them, but (rather) joke with them’.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Text refers to cousins practicing joking relationships; extracted as kin-role pair (cousins with cousins).,2026-07-06T10:08:04.884473+00:00
1090,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_6_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,abobaz,kin_role,abobaz,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly states culturally recognized joking ties among cousins (abobaz), a recurrent kinship-based institution.","The most frequently heard kinship term is abobaz, denoting “cousin,” used in a classificatory sense. ... There are joking relationships with cousins; relationships with affines are characterized by extreme reserve.",joking relationships with cousins,abobaz,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:08:11.327187+00:00
1091,/Tuareg_Rasmussen_2010_7_KS,NorthernAfrica,Tuareg,maternal uncles,kin_role,small children,kin_role,kinship,"Text reports recurrent play and joking between mother's brothers and young children, a patterned kinship role interaction.","Fathers are considered disciplinarians, yet other men, particularly maternal uncles, often play and joke with small children.",play and joke,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:08:16.860455+00:00
1096,/bemba_richards_131_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,a man,kin_role,his wife's sisters,kin_role,kinship,Text states there are no taboos and he 'laughs and jokes' with his wife's sisters; kin-role based joking.,There are no taboos between a man and his wife’s sisters. He laughs and jokes with them because he may one day marry them.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:08:55.809557+00:00
1097,/bemba_richards_131_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,sons-in-law attached to one household,kin_role,sons-in-law attached to one household (other co-husbands),kin_role,kinship,Document describes recurrent affectionate familiarity among co-resident sons-in-law (Ciufi munandi).,"Between the sons-in-law attached to one household there is a feeling of close friendship and familiarity. These young men eat and work together, and the term Ciufi munandi, ‘the man who married with me’, or into the same family, is one of affection and good fellowship.",Ciufi munandi,Ciufi munandi,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:08:55.809557+00:00
1098,/bemba_richards_188_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,Banungwe,clan,opposite clans (Banungwe),clan,within_group,Text describes Banungwe as opposite clans with reciprocal ritual duties and a joking relationship.,"Opposite clans performing reciprocal ritual duties and standing in a joking relationship to each other have been reported among several of the Central Comparative Evidence Bantu tribes, 1 but the distribution of this type of clan organization in Central and East Africa is as yet unknown, and the whole phenomenon has never been fully described to my knowledge. I am therefore publishing the following notes on the Banungwe, 2 or opposite clans of the Bemba of N.E. Rhodesia in the hope of eliciting further comparative information on the subject.",opposite clans,Banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Document names the category 'Banungwe' (opposite clans) but does not list specific clan names; coded as within_group clan↔clan pattern.,2026-07-06T10:09:02.149933+00:00
1099,/bemba_richards_190_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,crocodile clan,clan,fish clan,clan,within_group,Text describes recurring mutual teasing and formalized reciprocal insults between these clans.,"Banungwe regularly address each other as ‘You, my enemy!’ ( Mwe mulwani mwe! ) and delight in laughter at the habits of the objects, whether animal or plant, of the opposite clan. A member of the crocodile clan will scoff at one of the fish people and deride him as, ‘You! You are just my food ration!’ ( poso, the word used for a government ration given to employees).",banungwe / joking antagonism,Mwe mulwani mwe!,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:11.881162+00:00
1100,/bemba_richards_190_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,fish clan,clan,Paramount Chief,person,within_group,Ritualized court performances in which the fish clan priest dramatizes antagonism toward the chief are recurrent and formalized.,"At the court of the Paramount Chief this type of joking relationship has been formalized, and the head of the fish clan, a mukabilo, or hereditary priest of the tribal ancestral spirits, must dramatize the joking antagonism between the two clans by appearing in special regalia each time he visits the chief, and threatening him with a broad-bladed fishing spear.",formalized court joking / dramatized antagonism,mukabilo,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:11.881162+00:00
1101,/bemba_richards_190_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,dog clan,clan,Paramount Chief,person,within_group,"Head of dog clan performs protective, mock-barking dance for the chief as part of recurrent court ceremony.","On such occasions the Paramount is protected by the head of the dog clan, who has to dance in front of him, pretending to bark in defence of his master.",protective joking performance,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:11.881162+00:00
1102,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,mfula,clan,"clans with plant names (the millet clans, the gourd, the mushroom clans, the grass, the tree, etc.)",clan,within_group,"Text states rain clan (mfula) counts plant-name clans as its banungwe—reciprocal, institutionalized ties.","the rain clan ( mfula ) counts as its opposite numbers all the clans with plant names (the millet clans, the gourd, the mushroom clans, the grass, the tree, etc.) since plants cannot grow without rain.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1103,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,fish clan,clan,rain people,clan,within_group,Text explicitly says fish clan members are banungwe to the rain people—an institutional opposite-clan tie.,"The fish clan members are also banungwe to the rain people, since fish only spawn when the rain has begun to fall.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1104,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron people,clan,"clans with the names of animals hunted or killed with iron spears or arrows (elephant, lion, leopard, etc.)",clan,within_group,Iron people reckon themselves banungwe to animal-named clans—institutional reciprocal ties linked to names/roles.,"The iron people reckon themselves banungwe to members of all clans with the names of animals hunted or killed with iron spears or arrows, i.e., the elephant, lion, leopard, etc.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1105,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,animal clans,clan,iron people,clan,within_group,Animal clans are described as having reciprocal relations with iron people—institutional pairing.,"The animal clans also have reciprocal relations not only with the iron people, but with any mukoa named after an animal smaller than itself which forms its prey ( e.g., the lion and goat)...",reciprocal relations / banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1106,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,lion,clan,goat,clan,within_group,Text gives lion↔goat as an example of inter-animal-clan reciprocal (prey/predator) banungwe ties.,"The animal clans also have reciprocal relations ... with any mukoa named after an animal smaller than itself which forms its prey ( e.g., the lion and goat)...",reciprocal relations / banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1107,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,mourning clan,clan,all the clans,group,within_group,Mourning clan explicitly claims banungwe status to all clans and performs taunting at funerals—institutionalized joking.,The members of the mourning clan laughingly assert they are banungwe of all the clans since they are experts in mortuary ritual! —one of the reciprocal functions performed by banungwe .,banungwe,banungwe,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Text implies wide-ranging ties to all clans; symmetry unclear in practice though claim is reciprocal function.,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1108,/bemba_richards_190a_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,banungwe (opposite clans),group,mourning family (mourners),group,within_group,Banungwe perform ritual duties and taunt the deceased's family at funerals—regularized teasing relationship.,"The banungwe of a dead man’s family both help and harass him at the burial ceremony.... They also taunt them at the funeral, irritate them and make jokes.",banungwe duties / funeral taunting,banungwe,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Function performed by banungwe toward the deceased's family; teasing directed at mourners.,2026-07-06T10:09:31.032961+00:00
1109,/bemba_richards_192_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,millet clan,clan,royal crocodile clan,clan,within_group,Text presents millet clan as banungwe (ritual joking/partnered enemies) of the royal crocodile clan.,"A member of the millet clan is still appointed as the Shimwalule, or hereditary burier, of the principal chiefs of the Bemba to-day, and he is still responsible for the final and most powerful rain ceremony... the millet clan are never described simply as the banungwe of the royal crocodile clan,",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:38.375658+00:00
1110,/bemba_richards_192_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,Wild Pig clan (ngulube),clan,royal clan (tree clan ngoma),clan,within_group,"Text states Wild Pig clan are banungwe of the island's royal (tree) clan, a recurrent ritual/joking partnership.","Thus Chief Matipa on Cilubi Island is buried by the head of the Wild Pig clan ( ngulube ) who are banungwe of the royal clan of that island, which is the tree clan ( ngoma ),",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:09:38.375658+00:00
1111,/bemba_richards_193_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,clan,elephant,clan,within_group,Text describes longstanding paired clans (banungwe) with ritual and teasing ties within Bemba society.,"Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,"Objects (iron, elephant) are used as mukoa clan names; pairing entails ceremonial teasing.",2026-07-06T10:09:48.435568+00:00
1112,/bemba_richards_193_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,clan,leopard,clan,within_group,Text lists object-named clans paired as banungwe with ritualized teasing within the society.,"Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:09:48.435568+00:00
1113,/bemba_richards_193_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,clan,the wild pig,clan,within_group,Author describes extension of banungwe pairing among mukoa (object) clans tied to ceremonial teasing.,"Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,Document uses 'the wild pig' as an object-name; retained exact wording.,2026-07-06T10:09:48.435568+00:00
1114,/bemba_richards_193_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,iron,clan,lion,clan,within_group,Described as paired/hostile mukoa clans (banungwe) performing ceremonial teasing within Bemba society.,"Then he stops to think and adds more names, and then proceeds to argue with himself, for instance, that if iron is [p. (b)] hostile to the elephant, it must be the enemy of the leopard, the wild pig or the lion too.",banungwe,banungwe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,,2026-07-06T10:09:48.435568+00:00
1115,/bemba_richards_337_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,men of the Crocodile,clan,Mpende clan,clan,within_group,Text describes recurring teasing between Crocodile men and the fish (Mpende) clan during fishing rites.,"This, he said, was to please the imipashi ( ukusekeshye mipashi ). When the first fish were caught three were put in the ulufuba for the imipashi . 'Nowadays,' he added, 'we are Christians. We do not build nfuba . We tie knots in the grass instead to imitate ( kupashyana ) an ulufuba . That is our sacred place ( E pa musumba! ) where we put the fish. A native of Shiwa Dandu confirmed this form of the rite. He said it was good to joke with the imipashi as men do with members of the opposite clan (Banungwe) and say to them after calling on them, 'You are the Mpende clan ( mpende is a big fish that eats little ones, mpepe ). You eat mpepe! Muli bene mpende! ) Mulya ne mpepe! This is the way men of the Crocodile rag with the fish clan, pretending they are going to eat them, and it is good to joke ( ukucena ) with the imipashi in this way.'",rag with / joke with,ukucena,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:10:00.419193+00:00
1116,/bemba_richards_337_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,Banungwe,clan,imipashi,group,within_group,"Text explicitly compares joking with imipashi to joking with the opposite clan Banungwe, indicating an institutionalized tease relation.","He said it was good to joke with the imipashi as men do with members of the opposite clan (Banungwe) and say to them after calling on them, 'You are the Mpende clan ( mpende is a big fish that eats little ones, mpepe ). ... and it is good to joke ( ukucena ) with the imipashi in this way.'",joke with,ukucena,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,imipashi are water spirits/offices; treated as named group for teasing practice.,2026-07-06T10:10:00.419193+00:00
1117,/bemba_richards_34_sv,SouthernAfrica,bemba,paired clans,clan,opposite clans,clan,kinship,Text describes recurrent joking ties between matrilineal clans as paired/opposite; clearly kinship-based institutional joking.,"The Bemba are organized in matrilineal clans distinguished by totemic observances, stereotyped forms of greeting between clan members, joking relationships with paired and opposite clans,",joking relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Document does not name specific clans; phrase 'paired and opposite clans' used verbatim as entities.,2026-07-06T10:10:06.454120+00:00
1118,/ila_jaspan_35_sv,SouthernAfrica,ila,members of a misela (musela) age-set,age_set,other members of the same misela (musela) age-set,age_set,within_group,"Text states age-set membership is ""expressed in a joking relationship,"" indicating institutionalized joking among age-set members.",Such sets of people are called misela (sing. musela). Membership is expressed in a joking relationship and by the obligation of mutual aid.,,misela (musela),symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,,2026-07-06T10:10:15.204398+00:00
1119,/ila_tuden_71_sv,SouthernAfrica,ila,"bazhike / mushike (acquired persons, slaves)",caste,owner (called mother's brother) and owner's relatives,kin_role,within_group,Text describes customary use of joking names/kinship terms addressing purchased persons by owners and kin.,"A joking name, kinship term, or spirit name—the customary method of referring to a free person—was the accepted procedure of addressing the individual who had been purchased. The slave's owner was called mother's brother; this genealogical fiction was adopted and extended72 to the other relatives.",joking name / kinship term,mushike; bazhike; nabutema; Bantu bulyo,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,I coded acquired persons as 'caste' (socially distinct acquired group); kin-role is owner's title 'mother's brother' used in the joking/kinship fiction.,2026-07-06T10:10:22.163925+00:00
1120,/khoi_barnard_186_sv,SouthernAfrica,khoi,mother's brother (//naob),kin_role,sister's son,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly labels mother's brother–sister's son as recurrent joking exchange with a named local term.,"The Nama mother's brother (//naob) was the object of intense joking behaviour, including obligatory cattle-snatching and the threat of wife-snatching on the part of a sister's son towards his mother's brother. Among the Nama, this relationship is known as //nuri//as or //nuri//ab (literally ‘//nuri exchange’).",//nuri//as or //nuri//ab ('//nuri exchange'),//nuri//as or //nuri//ab,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:10:28.035825+00:00
1121,/khoi_carstens_63_sv,SouthernAfrica,khoi,mother's brother,kin_role,nephew/niece,kin_role,kinship,"Mother's brother is addressed by a joking term by his sister's children, indicating a recurring kinship joking tie.","Mother's brother, on the other hand, was often addressed as //Náob, a joking term, but also the usual term for grandfather (Hoernlé 1925: 19–22).",,//Náob,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:10:37.164629+00:00
1122,/lozi_gluckman_149_sv,SouthernAfrica,lozi,husband,kin_role,sister-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states a recurrent joking relationship exists with the sister-in-law; clearly kinship-based.,You cannot even do that with a sister-in-law [with whom there is a joking relationship].,joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:10:48.072686+00:00
1123,/lozi_gluckman_176_sv,SouthernAfrica,lozi,mother's brother,kin_role,sister's son,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent avuncular teasing terms and licensed sexualized familiarity between uncle and nephew.,"A mother's brother also calls his sister's daughter ‘my wife’ and his sister's son ‘my fellow-husband’ ( mufubalume ), ... the nephew can sleep in his uncle's hut, fondle his uncle's wife's breasts, and marry her if she wants to when his uncle dies.",fellow-husband,mufubalume,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:10:55.522329+00:00
1124,/lozi_gluckman_176_sv,SouthernAfrica,lozi,father's sister,kin_role,brother's son,kin_role,kinship,Described institutionalized teasing where aunt and nephew call each other 'my husband'/'my co-wife' as patterned relations.,"father's sister also calls her brother's son ‘my husband’ and her brother's daughter ‘my co-wife’ ( muhalizo ), as well as ‘my child’.",my husband / my co-wife,muhalizo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:10:55.522329+00:00
1125,/lozi_gluckman_176_sv,SouthernAfrica,lozi,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren of opposite sex,kin_role,kinship,Text identifies cross-generation teasing between opposite-sex grandparents and grandchildren as part of joking patterns.,"Grandparents and grandchildren of opposite sex can address each other similarly. Between persons of opposite sex this form of teasing ceases when the youngsters, who find it embarrassing, grow up: they use the terms grandparent, father, mother, and child, out of respect.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:10:55.522329+00:00
1126,/lozi_gluckman_177_sv,SouthernAfrica,lozi,brothers-in-law,kin_role,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states directly related brothers- and sisters-in-law 'joke and tease' indicating a recurrent kin-based joking tie.,"Directly related brothers- and sisters-in-law behave freely to each other. They are ‘man and wife’, even when of the same sex, though they more often call each other mulamu than ‘wife’ and ‘husband’. But they should not marry. They joke and tease each other.",‘man and wife’ / mulamu,mulamu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:11:02.279426+00:00
1127,/lozi_gluckman_277_sv,SouthernAfrica,lozi,Mbunda,ethnic group,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states Mbunda maintain joking relations with cross-cousins; kinship-based institutional tie.,Mbunda have joking relations with cross-cousins.,joking relations,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:11:05.826609+00:00
1128,/ovambo_loeb_107_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,two cross-cousins of opposite sexes,kin_role,each other (cross-cousins of opposite sexes),kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly describes recurrent joking between opposite-sex cross-cousins using a shared term.,"Although the Kuanyama do not now approve of cross-cousin marriages, traces of the custom remain in speech and action. For instance, customs, proverbs, and relationship terms appear to indicate that paternal cross-cousin marriage was preferred to maternal cross-cousin marriage; the Kuanyama proverb, “Ka xe kedule kaujoko” (Your father is more than your mother) may have been originally an expression of this preference. It is common practice, moreover, for two cross-cousins of opposite sexes to joke with each other and call each other “bull” (ohmedi).",call each other “bull”,ohmedi,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:11:31.277964+00:00
1129,/ovambo_loeb_107_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,a youth,kin_role,his father's sister's daughter,kin_role,kinship,The ethnography links this specific cross-cousin pair to recurring symbolic/joking behavior tied to the moon.,"The relationship between a youth and his father's sister's daughter is intimately connected with the moon. The moon commonly is called ohani, but it (and moonlight) may be called omuedi (bull-person).",,omuedi,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Text ties the pair to moon symbolism and 'bull' terminology; directionality of joking unclear.,2026-07-06T10:11:31.277964+00:00
1130,/ovambo_loeb_269_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,cross-cousins of opposite sexes,kin_role,cross-cousins of opposite sexes,kin_role,kinship,Reciprocal kin term and sanctioned marriages indicate an institutionalized joking/affection tie between cross-cousins.,The reciprocal term ometzi jangi (my bull) is used for cross-cousins of opposite sexes. Cross-cousins married to one another do not have to avoid their parents-in-law.,ometzi jangi (my bull),ometzi jangi,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:11:42.619607+00:00
1131,/ovambo_loeb_269_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,a man,kin_role,any girl of his own age in his father's clan,kin_role,kinship,"Text says men may jokingly call girls in their father's clan 'my bull'—a recurrent, sanctioned teasing tied to kin/clan positions.",In this tribe a man may joke with any girl of his own age in his father's clan and call her “my bull.”,my bull (joking address),,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Joking directed from men toward girls of the father's clan; patterned with kinship/clan basis.,2026-07-06T10:11:42.619607+00:00
1132,/ovambo_loeb_284_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovambo,father's sister's daughter,kin_role,mother's brother's daughter,kin_role,kinship,Text describes cross-cousins (these kin types) as holding a recurrent joking/bundling relationship.,"Cross-cousins who call each other “bull” stand in a joking and bundling relationship. My informant, an educated school teacher, explained that a man is supposed to take his father's sister's daughter as his first wife, because through her he can inherit from his father. Then as a second wife or as one of his later wives he may marry his mother's brother's daughter.",joking and bundling relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:11:53.141732+00:00
1133,/ovimbundu_edwards_103_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovimbundu,a man,kin_role,his wife's brother,kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally patterned joking prohibition and later friendly joking between husband and wife's brother.,"Traditionally the relationship between a man and his wife's brother was distant and reserved, the two men being forbidden to eat together or joke. Nowadays the relations between a man and his wife's full or half brother are often very friendly;",forbidden to eat together or joke,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.8,Historical prohibition of joking transformed into contemporary friendly/joking relations; recorded as a kin-based institutional pattern.,2026-07-06T10:12:02.778242+00:00
1134,/ovimbundu_edwards_62_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovimbundu,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states cross-cousin relations are expressed in joking and solidarity — a recurrent kinship joking institution.,"The relation between cross-cousins is one of equality and solidarity, expressed in joking and in burial services.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:12:10.253958+00:00
1135,/ovimbundu_handly_154_sv,SouthernAfrica,ovimbundu,The Ovimbundu of Elende,regional group,the people of Bailundu,regional group,within_group,Text states a recurrent joking tie between these named Ovimbundu subgroups; intra-confederacy pattern.,"The Ovimbundu of Elende have a joke against the people of Bailundu, because the latter on first seeing a cow offered the animal some food on a wooden platter. This story suggests that the Bailundu people, who are of the Ovimbundu confederacy, did not know the animal and its habits as early as did the Ovimbundu themselves.",have a joke against,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Bailundu treated as distinctive subgroup within Ovimbundu confederacy; joking described as directed at Bailundu.,2026-07-06T10:12:20.865872+00:00
1136,/san_guenther_166_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,farm Bushmen,ethnic group,Europeans,ethnic group,cross_group,Text reports recurring joking between the two groups as a mechanism curbing aggression; cross-group institutionalized teasing.,"Carstens (1971: –43) has noted this paradoxical psychological reaction in other situations of dependence and dominance—among South African and Canadian reserve dwellers—and discusses a variety of mechanisms of curbing aggression (such as joking behaviour between members of the two groups, work sabotage, and direction of overt aggression unto members of ones own group).",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Document frames joking as a patterned mechanism between dependent Bushmen and dominant Europeans; directionality not specified.,2026-07-06T10:12:27.781064+00:00
1137,/san_guenther_80_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,tssxõ,kin_role,basic kin relations,kin_role,kinship,tssxõ is described as a patterned relation of obscene joking among kinsmen.,"In addition, there are three broad relationships that cut across or modify the 11 specific relations. These are an avoidance relationship (dau'i); a relation of closeness, affection and mutual help (tssxõ)—as well as of obscene joking and teasing—","relation of closeness, affection and mutual help (tssxõ)",tssxõ,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:12:39.895774+00:00
1138,/san_guenther_80_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,name relationship (k'õi),kin_role,tssxõ,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states name relationship interaction follows the tssxõ joking pattern.,"The interaction with ones name relation follows the tssxõ pattern. While the name relations expand the network of kin relations, the joking and avoidance principles stabilize the interrelations of kinsmen.",name relationship follows tssxõ pattern,k'õi,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:12:39.895774+00:00
1139,/san_howell_231_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,joking partners,group,couples,group,within_group,Text describes culturally recognized teasing by 'joking' partners toward couples in village context.,"Couples who are obviously having coitus in the village can expect to be teased about it later by their “joking” partners, but since some of the members of the village are likely to be “respect” relatives, in whose presence no sexual jokes should be made, it is not easy to integrate coitus into village life.",joking partners,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:12:50.751242+00:00
1140,/san_howell_231_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,the couples' own parents,kin_role,couples,group,kinship,Parents are named 'respect' relatives in whose presence sexual jokes are forbidden (institutionalized prohibition).,"but since some of the members of the village are likely to be “respect” relatives, in whose presence no sexual jokes should be made, it is not easy to integrate coitus into village life. The most important “respect” relations are the couples' own parents and own children,",respect (no-joking) relations,respect,symmetric,avoidance,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:12:50.751242+00:00
1141,/san_howell_231_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,the couples' own children,kin_role,couples,group,kinship,Children are listed as 'respect' relatives in whose presence sexual jokes are forbidden (institutionalized prohibition).,"The most important “respect” relations are the couples' own parents and own children, who are precisely the people who are likely to be closest to them in the sleeping arrangements of the village (Shostak 1976).",respect (no-joking) relations,respect,symmetric,avoidance,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:12:50.751242+00:00
1142,/san_lee_246_1979_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,the party,group,a young man who kills much meat,person,within_group,"Repeated, culturally sanctioned group teasing of successful hunters to curb arrogance; internal to the !Kung society.","The party, of course, has no intention of abandoning the kill. The heavy joking and derision are directed toward one goal: the leveling of potentially arrogant behavior in a successful hunter. ... ""When a young man kills much meat, he comes to think of himself as a chief or a big man, and he thinks of the rest of us as his servants or inferiors. We can't accept this. We refuse one who boasts, for someday his pride will make him kill somebody. So we always speak of his meat as worthless.""",heavy joking and derision to level arrogance,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Group practice directed at individual successful hunters; culturally patterned leveling mechanism among !Kung.,2026-07-06T10:13:02.481009+00:00
1143,/san_lee_356_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,≠oma (bearers of the name),group,≠oma (bearers of the name),group,within_group,Text describes bearers of the same personal name (≠oma) enjoying a recurring joking familiarity.,"The basic principle is that bearers of the same name have a special affectionate relation with one another, and they use the kin terms “old name—young name” regardless of the actual biological connection. It obtains even in cases where there is no traceable connection at all. In the Dobe area, 22 of the men are named ≠oma, and whenever ≠omas meet they enjoy the familiarity of the joking relation.",the name relation / joking relation,the name relation,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:13:14.307243+00:00
1144,/san_lee_356_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,/i!ay (bearers of the name),group,/i!ay (bearers of the name),group,within_group,Specific named-name group (/i!ay) are described as enjoying strong bonds and familiar joking when they meet.,"The bonds are particularly strong among men who actually share a common ancestral namesake. For instance, nine of the twelve men named /i!ay in the Dobe area are descended from a single prolific /i!ay. Of him it can truly be said that his name is legion.",the name relation / joking familiarity,the name relation,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Implied joking familiarity among bearers though explicit phrase 'joking relation' appears for ≠oma; here inferred from context about strong bonds among /i!ay.,2026-07-06T10:13:14.307243+00:00
1145,/san_lee_357_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,bearers of ego's father's name,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,"Text states bearers of father's name are treated 'like father', creating a kin-based joking/avoidance category.","This principle is extended widely, so that a person with ego's father's name is treated “like father,” bearers of ego's mother's name are “like mother,” and bearers of ego's siblings' names are “like brother and sister.”",like father,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:13:23.530438+00:00
1146,/san_lee_357_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,bearers of ego's mother's name,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,"Text states bearers of mother's name are treated 'like mother', establishing a kin-based joking/avoidance relation.","This principle is extended widely, so that a person with ego's father's name is treated “like father,” bearers of ego's mother's name are “like mother,” and bearers of ego's siblings' names are “like brother and sister.”",like mother,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:13:23.530438+00:00
1147,/san_lee_357_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,bearers of ego's siblings' names,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,"Text states bearers of sibling names are treated 'like brother and sister', forming patterned kin joking/avoidance relations.","This principle is extended widely, so that a person with ego's father's name is treated “like father,” bearers of ego's mother's name are “like mother,” and bearers of ego's siblings' names are “like brother and sister.”",like brother and sister,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:13:23.530438+00:00
1148,/san_lee_358_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,tun (older),kin_role,kinship,"Text states ego has a relaxed joking relation with tuns, a recurrent kin-role based joking tie.","The tuns and tumas are the girls with whom ego has a relaxed joking relation, the female equivalent of the cordial “old name—young name” relation.",relaxed joking relation,tun,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:13:31.775641+00:00
1149,/san_lee_358_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,tuma (younger than ego),kin_role,kinship,"Text states ego has a relaxed joking relation with tumas, a recurrent kin-role based joking tie.","The tuns and tumas are the girls with whom ego has a relaxed joking relation, the female equivalent of the cordial “old name—young name” relation.",relaxed joking relation,tuma,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:13:31.775641+00:00
1150,/san_lee_367_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,tun (older than ego),kin_role,kinship,Text states tun are kin-term females with whom ego has a joking relation; clearly kin-based institutionalized joking.,tun (older than ego) and tuma (younger than ego): kin terms for females; these are females with whom ego has a joking relation,joking relation,tun,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:13:41.282726+00:00
1151,/san_lee_367_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,tuma (younger than ego),kin_role,kinship,Text states tuma are kin-term females with whom ego has a joking relation; clearly kin-based institutionalized joking.,tun (older than ego) and tuma (younger than ego): kin terms for females; these are females with whom ego has a joking relation,joking relation,tuma,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:13:41.282726+00:00
1152,/san_lee_372_1972_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,appropriate kin,kin_role,appropriate kin,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes za as an affectionate joking form between 'appropriate kin', a recurrent kin-based joking institution.","The third and final phase of conflict on the verbal level is za (“sexual insulting”). The za mode occupies the ambiguous position of being both the highest form of affectionate joking between appropriate kin in the

“joking relationship” and the deadliest affront leading directly to fighting.",joking relationship,za,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T10:13:47.817990+00:00
1153,/san_marshall_19_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,persons termed !gu!na (or tun!ga w.s.),kin_role,kinship,Text states ego uses the term !gu!na/tun!ga w.s. for name-relatives with joking status.,The preferred marriage among !Kung is with a non-consanguineous name-relative whom ego terms !gu!na (or tun!ga w.s.) or tũn m.s.,,!gu!na; tun!ga w.s.,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,Joking status is associated with kin terms; this extracts ego ↔ !gu!na name-relatives.,2026-07-06T10:14:07.719702+00:00
1154,/san_marshall_19_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,persons termed tũn m.s.,kin_role,kinship,Text pairs tũn m.s. with ego as a culturally recognized name-relative category with joking implications.,The preferred marriage among !Kung is with a non-consanguineous name-relative whom ego terms !gu!na (or tun!ga w.s.) or tũn m.s.,,tũn m.s.,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T10:14:07.719702+00:00
1155,/san_marshall_19_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,persons termed tsu,kin_role,kinship,Informants taught children which kin terms (e.g. tsu) carry a prohibition against joking with those relatives.,"Sometimes an informant would not know whether, for instance, a relative he termed tsu was his FaBr, his MoBr or who, but would say that his parents taught him to use that term for the relative and that he must not joke with him.",,tsu,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Text explicitly states prohibition of joking with those termed tsu.,2026-07-06T10:14:07.719702+00:00
1156,/san_marshall_19_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,persons termed //ga,kin_role,kinship,Text notes that persons who use the terms tsu and //ga for each other may marry and these kin terms have joking status.,Persons who use the terms tsu and //ga for each other may marry unless one has the name of the other's parent.,,//ga,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Marriage permissibility indicates culturally recognized kin categories with associated joking rules.,2026-07-06T10:14:07.719702+00:00
1157,/san_marshall_19_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,persons termed sibling terms,kin_role,kinship,Text states sibling terms may marry though not preferred; sibling kin terms have associated joking status.,"Persons who use the sibling terms may marry. These marriages, though not forbidden, are not preferred.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.5,Sibling kin-terms referenced as a class; local labels not given here.,2026-07-06T10:14:07.719702+00:00
1158,/san_marshall_204_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,FaBr,kin_role,ego/any other !Kung,kin_role,kinship,Text states joking status is carried by kin terms (examples: FaBr).,"The !Kung were apparently not always assiduous in teaching their children the exact biological position of their kinsmen (whether a given man was FaBr or MoBr, for instance), and a person would not always know why he applied a certain term to someone, but he would know that the term he used was proper, and he would know the proper joking status to observe;",joking status carried by kin terms,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Represents ego's joking relation with father's-brother kin term (FaBr); text does not specify symmetry.,2026-07-06T10:14:21.755625+00:00
1159,/san_marshall_204_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,MoBr,kin_role,ego/any other !Kung,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly gives MoBr as kin term example whose joking status is specified.,"The !Kung were apparently not always assiduous in teaching their children the exact biological position of their kinsmen (whether a given man was FaBr or MoBr, for instance), and a person would not always know why he applied a certain term to someone, but he would know that the term he used was proper, and he would know the proper joking status to observe;",joking status carried by kin terms,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Represents ego's joking relation with mother's-brother kin term (MoBr); text does not indicate directionality.,2026-07-06T10:14:21.755625+00:00
1160,/san_marshall_204_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,!Kung,ethnic group,!Kung,ethnic group,within_group,Text states every !Kung is permitted or forbidden to joke with every other !Kung by system rules.,"The application of kin terms is so extensive that it comes to involve the whole population of the Nyae Nyae region, with the result that a !Kung, by the rules of the system, is specifically permitted or forbidden to joke with every other !Kung he knows.",permitted or forbidden joking between individuals,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Encodes the ethnographer's statement that joking status is culturally determined for every pair of !Kung individuals.,2026-07-06T10:14:21.755625+00:00
1161,/san_marshall_205_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Grandparents,kin_role,Grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly frames joking between grandparents and grandchildren as a recurring, nonsexual intimacy.","To make blatantly insulting jokes with old people is not proper. The deference due to aged persons is expressed in the joking relationship by a moderate tone, and the joking relationship between an old person and a considerably younger one, especially between grandparents and grandchildren, becomes a cozy, friendly intimacy without the sexual emphasis.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:14:33.342166+00:00
1162,/san_marshall_205_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Men,group,Men,group,within_group,Ethnographer describes recurrent sexual insults and teasing used among men who have the joking relationship.,"Men accuse each other of having genital organs of excessive size or abnormal condition (castration or circumcision), or of being excessively preoccupied with sexual intercourse, unable to leave their wives long enough to go hunting.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:14:33.342166+00:00
1163,/san_marshall_205_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Men,group,Women,group,within_group,"Text describes patterned teasing between men and women (accusations, 'my wife', mutual retaliation) as culturally permitted joking.","Men accuse women with whom they may joke of being lazy, useless, slovenly, wanton, or ugly ... The women retaliate, perhaps accusing the men of being worthy only of castration or of unfaithful wives. Frequently a man joking with a woman calls her “my wife.”",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:14:33.342166+00:00
1164,/san_marshall_205_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Boys,age_set,Girls,age_set,within_group,"Passage notes boys jokingly claim 'my wife' or 'I am going to marry you' to girls with whom they have the joking relationship, a patterned age/sex teasing practice.","A boy might say “my wife” or “I am going to marry you” to a girl with whom he could joke, implying that they had had or would have sexual relations; but it would be considered neither proper nor funny to say such a thing to a very young girl, regardless of whether the boy and girl had the joking relationship.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Age-limited; reciprocity not fully specified.,2026-07-06T10:14:33.342166+00:00
1165,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,parents,kin_role,kinship,Text states ego avoids joking with parents; institutionalized prohibition in kinship.,"In the nuclear family, ego avoids joking with his parents and his offspring.",,_koa,unclear,avoidance,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
1166,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,offspring,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states ego avoids joking with his offspring; institutionalized kinship prohibition.,"In the nuclear family, ego avoids joking with his parents and his offspring.",,_koa,unclear,avoidance,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
1167,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,male,kin_role,brother,kin_role,kinship,Cross/parallel pattern: a male jokes with his brother; recurring sex-differentiated sibling joking.,"Among siblings, according to a cross and parallel pattern in which a sex differentiation is made, a male jokes with his brother, but not with his sister;",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
1168,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,male,kin_role,sister,kin_role,kinship,Text states male does not joke with his sister; institutionalized sibling avoidance.,"Among siblings, according to a cross and parallel pattern in which a sex differentiation is made, a male jokes with his brother, but not with his sister;",,,unclear,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
1169,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,female,kin_role,sister,kin_role,kinship,Female jokes with her sister per sex-differentiated sibling pattern; recurring institutionalized joking.,"Among siblings, according to a cross and parallel pattern in which a sex differentiation is made, a ... female jokes with her sister, but not with her brother.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
1170,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,female,kin_role,brother,kin_role,kinship,Text states female does not joke with her brother; patterned sibling avoidance.,"Among siblings, according to a cross and parallel pattern in which a sex differentiation is made, a ... female jokes with her sister, but not with her brother.",,,unclear,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
1171,/san_marshall_206_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,uncles,kin_role,kinship,Text says ego avoids joking with uncles (alternate generation); institutional prohibition.,"Ego avoids joking with his uncles, aunts, nephews, and nieces, who are, respectively, of his parents' and his offspring's generations.",,,unclear,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:15:06.937670+00:00
