1235,/san_marshall_221_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,tsu,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states tsu is applied to persons with whom ego avoids joking (institutionalized avoidance).,"The terms !gun!a and tũn are applied to the persons with whom ego may joke, tsu and //ga to persons with whom he avoids joking.",tsu (avoidance),tsu,unclear,avoidance,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:17:32.222641+00:00
1236,/san_marshall_221_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,//ga,kin_role,kinship,//ga is given as the term applied to persons with whom ego avoids joking.,"The terms !gun!a and tũn are applied to the persons with whom ego may joke, tsu and //ga to persons with whom he avoids joking.",//ga (avoidance),//ga,unclear,avoidance,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:17:32.222641+00:00
1237,/san_marshall_221_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,male ego,kin_role,BrWiSi,kin_role,kinship,Example states a male ego jokes with BrWiSi because he jokes with BrWi (institutionalized patterned joking).,"Thus, for example, a male ego would joke with his BrWiSi since he jokes with his BrWi, and he would refrain from joking with his BrWiBr.",joking with BrWiSi,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Illustrative example linking joking status of BrWi to sibling of primary affine.,2026-07-06T10:17:32.222641+00:00
1238,/san_marshall_221_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,male ego,kin_role,BrWiBr,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly says male ego would refrain from joking with BrWiBr (institutionalized avoidance).,"Thus, for example, a male ego would joke with his BrWiSi since he jokes with his BrWi, and he would refrain from joking with his BrWiBr.",avoidance with BrWiBr,,unclear,avoidance,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:17:32.222641+00:00
1239,/san_marshall_221_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,female ego,kin_role,BrWiSi,kin_role,kinship,"Text states a female ego would avoid joking with BrWiSi, opposite of male pattern (sex-differentiated institutional joking).","A female ego would avoid joking with BrWiSi since she does not joke with BrWi, and to her BrWiSi she would apply the term //ga-n!untai; she would take the opposite position with BrWiBr, whom she would term !gun!a- (or tũn!ga-) n!unba.",avoidance with BrWiSi,//ga-n!untai,unclear,avoidance,0.85,Local compound term provided in text.,2026-07-06T10:17:32.222641+00:00
1240,/san_marshall_221_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,female ego,kin_role,BrWiBr,kin_role,kinship,Text states female ego would take opposite position and use !gun!a/tũn!ga terms toward BrWiBr (institutionalized joking).,"A female ego would avoid joking with BrWiSi since she does not joke with BrWi, and to her BrWiSi she would apply the term //ga-n!untai; she would take the opposite position with BrWiBr, whom she would term !gun!a- (or tũn!ga-) n!unba.",joking with BrWiBr,!gun!a / tũn!ga,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Local compound terms provided.,2026-07-06T10:17:32.222641+00:00
1241,/san_marshall_222_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Ego,kin_role,Spouses of Relatives,kin_role,kinship,Text states ego adopts terms giving a recurrent joking relationship with spouses of his kin/affines.,"Spouses of Relatives. When a person marries one of ego's kin or affines, ego drops whatever term he may have previously used for that person and adopts the term appropriate to the spouse of his kin or affine. Ego applies a term that gives him the same joking relationship with both members of the married couple.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:17:37.562941+00:00
1242,/san_marshall_225_1962_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,!gu!na,kin_role,!guma,kin_role,kinship,"Text states namesakes (!gu!na and !guma) have an open, recurrent joking relationship.",Behaviour between a !gu!na and a !guma is as open and free from formal restrictions as any in !Kung society. They have the joking relationship.,joking relationship,!gu!na—!guma,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:17:44.913928+00:00
1243,/san_marshall_228_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,nephews and nieces named for ego's parents,kin_role,kinship,"Text states these relatives carry the same joking status/terms as parents, a recurrent kin-based joking position.","Some of ego's nephews and nieces would be named for ego's parents. ... The generational terms which have the same generational association and joking status as the terms for parents and offspring are tsu and //ga, and these are the terms ego uses for relatives named for his parents and offspring.",,"tsu, //ga",unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:17:55.932696+00:00
1244,/san_marshall_228_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,relatives named for ego's sibling,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly says ego applies sibling terms and has same joking position with such relatives as with the sibling.,"If a relative is named for ego's sibling, ego applies sibling terms to the relative, and ego has the same joking position with him that he would have with his sibling.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:17:55.932696+00:00
1245,/san_marshall_228_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,male named for ego,kin_role,kinship,Reciprocal naming relation is described with specific vocatives indicating a patterned joking/relational stance.,"!Gun!a is the term used for a male named for himself; but the term would be in its diminutive form, !guma. A male named for ego would be younger than ego. That male would reciprocate by calling ego !gun!a.",,"!Gun!a, !guma",symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:17:55.932696+00:00
1246,/san_marshall_228_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,cousins named for ego's parents,kin_role,kinship,Text notes cousins named for parents take parental generational terms and associated joking status.,"Cousins would be termed !gun!a or tũn by the generational method; however, if they are named for ego's parents, the terms applied must be tsu or //ga.",,"tsu, //ga",unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Extracted because naming alters kin term and joking position.,2026-07-06T10:17:55.932696+00:00
1247,/san_marshall_229_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,persons named for ego's spouse,kin_role,kinship,Text says persons named for the spouse are classified with ego's generation and given the joking relationship.,"The terms applied to persons named for ego's spouse are also !gun!a (or tũn!ga w.s.) or tũn. The affinal terms !hoa and tsau for husband and wife are used only for ego's actual spouse. Persons named for the spouse, who is terminologically of ego's generation, have the generational terms applied to them that classify them with ego's generation and give them the joking relationship.",joking relationship,"!gun!a, tũn!ga, tũn",symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:18:08.864813+00:00
1248,/san_marshall_229_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,person for whom he is named,kin_role,kinship,"Ego applies !gun!a to the person for whom he is named and that person reciprocates, indicating an institutional joking tie.","The term !gun!a has other applications. Ego applied it to the person for whom he is named, and also to anyone, related or not, who happens to have the same name as his, and to relatives named for him—with two exceptions:",!gun!a usage,!gun!a,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:18:08.864813+00:00
1249,/san_marshall_229_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,relatives named for him,kin_role,kinship,"Author states ego applies same term to relatives named for him, extending joking relationship down generations.","Ego applied it to the person for whom he is named, and also to anyone, related or not, who happens to have the same name as his, and to relatives named for him—with two exceptions:",joking relationship,!gun!a,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Includes descendants named for ego (continuation down generations).,2026-07-06T10:18:08.864813+00:00
1250,/san_marshall_229_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,relatives named for another of ego's kin,kin_role,kinship,"Text explains ego applies same term to kin who are named for other of ego's kin, producing recurring joking classifications.","When some relative is named for another of ego's kin, ego applies the same term to him that he applied to the kinsman.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:18:08.864813+00:00
1251,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states grandparents and grandchildren enjoy a cosy joking intimacy; culturally patterned kin joking.,"It is not thought proper to make blatant or insulting sexual jokes with old persons. The deference which is shown to aged persons is expressed by Kin Relationships moderation, and the joking relationship between an old person and a considerably younger person, especially between grandparents and grandchildren, becomes a cosy friendly intimacy without the sexual emphasis.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:18:26.337594+00:00
1252,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,parents,kin_role,children,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes joking with parents as restricted/forbidden, symbolizing sexual restraint — institutionalized prohibition.","It seems that respect in general is not required between siblings; only sexual restraint is required and this is Kinship Regulation Of symbolized by the restriction in joking imposed on siblings of opposite sex, Sex whereas ego may joke with a sibling of the same sex.",restriction on joking / non-joking,,symmetric,avoidance,0.85,Also stated earlier: 'It is not thought proper to make blatant or insulting sexual jokes with old persons' and prohibition applies to parents of both sexes.,2026-07-06T10:18:26.337594+00:00
1253,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,sibling (male),kin_role,sibling (female),kin_role,kinship,Explicit rule: joking is restricted between siblings of opposite sex; institutionalized sexual-restraint prohibition.,"It seems that respect in general is not required between siblings; only sexual restraint is required and this is Kinship Regulation Of symbolized by the restriction in joking imposed on siblings of opposite sex, Sex whereas ego may joke with a sibling of the same sex.",restriction on joking / non-joking,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:18:26.337594+00:00
1254,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,strangers,group,local people (hosts/contacts),group,within_group,Joking with strangers is withheld until status is known; culturally patterned caution about joking with outsiders.,When they meet strangers they do not dare to joke until they find out whether or not it is proper for them to do so. They gave us !Kung names. This placed us in the scheme of things and people could know what their status was with us.,,koa,unclear,avoidance,0.7,Local term 'koa' used for fearing/avoiding those with whom joking is forbidden.,2026-07-06T10:18:26.337594+00:00
1255,/san_marshall_22_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,name-relatives,kin_role,grandparents/grandchildren (when classified by name),kin_role,kinship,Text notes name-relationship can classify some grandparent–grandchild pairs into the non-joking category—institutionalized exception.,It is possible that the culture allows joking between grandparents and grandchildren (with the rather few exceptions when the factor of the name intervenes to classify them in the non-joking category) because joking is associated with a welcome ease and intimacy of relationship...,name-relationship exception (non-joking),,unclear,avoidance,0.8,Name-relationship is treated as part of kinship terminology influencing joking permissions.,2026-07-06T10:18:26.337594+00:00
1256,/san_marshall_230_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,secondary affines,kin_role,person for whom they were named,kin_role,kinship,Text states generational terms for secondary affines correspond in joking status to the named person.,"Some of ego's secondary affines would be named for his primary affines and some, possibly, for his offspring. These children would be ego's secondary Naming affines, and ego would apply generational terms to them which correspond in joking status to the term he applies to the person for whom they were named.",homonymous joking status correspondence,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:18:47.585027+00:00
1257,/san_marshall_230_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,FaFa,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,FaFa (and FaMo) are described as applying a diminutive term to ego in reciprocated patterned naming (joking terms).,"In Figure 5, ego is named for his FaFa, C. His FaFa and FaMo term ego !guma. He reciprocates calling them !gun!a and tũn.",,!guma ↔ !gun!a,mixed,joking_relationship,0.85,Reciprocal but different term forms for older vs. younger suggest mixed symmetry.,2026-07-06T10:18:47.585027+00:00
1258,/san_marshall_230_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,FaMo,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,FaMo explicitly listed with FaFa as applying the diminutive term !guma to ego (patterned joking terminology).,"In Figure 5, ego is named for his FaFa, C. His FaFa and FaMo term ego !guma.",,!guma,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Older kin apply diminutive; ego reciprocates with different terms.,2026-07-06T10:18:47.585027+00:00
1259,/san_marshall_230_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,A and B,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,Siblings of C are described as using sibling term for ego because of naming—patterned joking/generational terms.,"A and B, siblings of C, term ego tsĩ, younger sibling, because he is named for their sibling; ego reciprocates giving them the sibling terms.",,tsĩ,mixed,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:18:47.585027+00:00
1260,/san_marshall_230_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,E and F,kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,E and F term ego tsuma because he is named for their father; ego gives them corresponding terms—patterned reciprocity.,E and F term ego tsuma because he is named for their father; ego calls them tsu and // ga.,,tsuma ↔ tsu and // ga,mixed,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:18:47.585027+00:00
1261,/san_marshall_233_1961_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Men,group,Women,group,within_group,Text states men and women engage in culturally sanctioned sexual joking together.,Men and women must not speak openly together of sexual matters except as they make jokes in the joking relationship.,joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:18:51.707026+00:00
1262,/san_marshall_235_1961_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,men,group,women,group,within_group,Text describes recurrent sanctioned insults between men and women as a cultural joking relationship.,"In view of this, the joking relationship has its interesting side. Men and women who have the joking relationship insult each other in a facetious way and also point out actual faults or remark on actual episodes which embarrass a person.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:18:56.388887+00:00
1263,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,persons who have the same name,group,each other,group,kinship,Text describes name-relationship between persons with same name enabling joking; clearly kinship-based name-relatives.,"Whatever may be the answer to these questions, the !Kung have put to good use the belief that persons who have the same name partake of each other's entities. This belief has given them the name-relationship.",name-relationship,!gu!na,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:19:16.109729+00:00
1264,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,tun!ga (woman-speaking),kin_role,consanguineous kin,kin_role,kinship,Author states tun!ga applied to consanguineous kin allows joking when woman-speaking.,"Tun!ga is applied to consanguineous kin and affines and allows joking, woman-speaking.",tun!ga (woman-speaking),tun!ga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:19:16.109729+00:00
1265,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,tun!ga (woman-speaking),kin_role,affines,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly groups affines under tun!ga applied relations that allow joking (woman-speaking).,"Tun!ga is applied to consanguineous kin and affines and allows joking, woman-speaking.",tun!ga (woman-speaking),tun!ga,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:19:16.109729+00:00
1266,/san_marshall_23_1957_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Gao,person,/Qui,person,kinship,"Two named men used the term !go for each other due to shared brother-name, indicating an institutional name-relative joking tie.",We know two men who used the term !go for each other because each of them had a brother named Dam.,!go,!go,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Individuals exemplify recurring name-relative joking practice.,2026-07-06T10:19:16.109729+00:00
1267,/san_marshall_246_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,brother,kin_role,sister,kin_role,kinship,"Text states brother and sister use familiar address and joke until they grow up, marking an institutionalized joking tie.","The familiar pronoun, a, is used in addressing any child, and is used between persons who have the joking relationship, regardless of disparities in age. It is also used between brother and sister until they grow up, when they change to the respectful pronoun.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:19:32.141438+00:00
1268,/san_marshall_251_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Parents-In-Law,kin_role,Children-In-Law,kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally mandated speaking avoidance and prohibition on joking between these kin roles.,The speaking avoidance under these circumstances does not impose excessive inconvenience. ... The interactions of people who accord each other reserved behavior are not constrained in any way except in the avoidance of joking and the practice of the other avoidances just described.,speaking avoidance / avoidance of joking,,symmetric,avoidance,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:19:41.118262+00:00
1269,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,WiSiDa,kin_role,kinship,Text states marriage with WiSiDa is prohibited and joking is tied to marriage prohibitions.,"Why, we had asked, is marriage with WiSiDa and HuBrSo specifically prohibited? ≠Toma explained that a man often marries his WiSi if his wife should die, or as a second wife. His being her potential husband makes him “feel like” her husband even though he is not married to her.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1270,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,HuBrSo,kin_role,kinship,HuBrSo is listed with WiSiDa as specifically prohibited for marriage; prohibition aligns with joking avoidance.,"Why, we had asked, is marriage with WiSiDa and HuBrSo specifically prohibited? ≠Toma explained that a man often marries his WiSi if his wife should die, or as a second wife.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1271,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,SiHuFa,kin_role,kinship,SiHuFa is among affines ego must not marry; marriage and joking prohibitions coincide in sibling category.,"Marriage with a SiHu (as second wife or if her SiHu is a widower) or a man's with a BrWi (if the death or divorce of the brother makes the marriage with BrWi possible) is highly appropriate, and the parents of these affines, therefore, seem to ego “like” his spouse's parents.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1272,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,BrWiMo,kin_role,kinship,BrWiMo is listed with SiHuFa as an affine ego must not marry; tied to joking prohibition by table/discussion.,"Marriage with a SiHu (as second wife or if her SiHu is a widower) or a man's with a BrWi (if the death or divorce of the brother makes the marriage with BrWi possible) is highly appropriate, and the parents of these affines, therefore, seem to ego “like” his spouse's parents.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1273,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,SoWiFa,kin_role,kinship,SoWiFa explicitly listed among affines ego must not marry; table links no asterisk to prohibition of joking.,"Among the affines ego must not marry are SoWiFa, DaHuFa, SoWiMo, DaHuMo. Marriage with one of these would turn ego's So and SoWi, Da and DaHu into stepbrothers and stepsisters, which would be most awkward.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1274,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,DaHuFa,kin_role,kinship,DaHuFa listed among affines ego must not marry; marriage/joking prohibitions coincide.,"Among the affines ego must not marry are SoWiFa, DaHuFa, SoWiMo, DaHuMo. Marriage with one of these would turn ego's So and SoWi, Da and DaHu into stepbrothers and stepsisters, which would be most awkward.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1275,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,SoWiMo,kin_role,kinship,SoWiMo explicitly named among prohibited affines; linked to prohibition of joking in sibling category.,"Among the affines ego must not marry are SoWiFa, DaHuFa, SoWiMo, DaHuMo. Marriage with one of these would turn ego's So and SoWi, Da and DaHu into stepbrothers and stepsisters, which would be most awkward.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1276,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,DaHuMo,kin_role,kinship,DaHuMo is among affines ego must not marry; marriage taboos align with joking avoidance.,"Among the affines ego must not marry are SoWiFa, DaHuFa, SoWiMo, DaHuMo. Marriage with one of these would turn ego's So and SoWi, Da and DaHu into stepbrothers and stepsisters, which would be most awkward.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1277,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,HuSiHu,kin_role,kinship,HuSiHu marriage prohibited and explicitly linked to avoidance of joking; local term ≠tum applied to them.,"Marriage is prohibited with HuSiHu and WiBrWi, termed respectively ≠tum and / utsu; ego avoids joking with them as he avoids joking with HuSi and WiBr.",,≠tum,symmetric,avoidance,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1278,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,WiBrWi,kin_role,kinship,WiBrWi marriage prohibited and explicitly linked to avoidance of joking; local term / utsu applied.,"Marriage is prohibited with HuSiHu and WiBrWi, termed respectively ≠tum and / utsu; ego avoids joking with them as he avoids joking with HuSi and WiBr.",,/ utsu,symmetric,avoidance,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1279,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,HuSi,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states ego avoids joking with HuSi; tied to sibling-category joking rules.,"Marriage is prohibited with HuSiHu and WiBrWi, termed respectively ≠tum and / utsu; ego avoids joking with them as he avoids joking with HuSi and WiBr.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1280,/san_marshall_256_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,WiBr,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states ego avoids joking with WiBr; linked to avoidance extended to specified affines.,"Marriage is prohibited with HuSiHu and WiBrWi, termed respectively ≠tum and / utsu; ego avoids joking with them as he avoids joking with HuSi and WiBr.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:20:10.629587+00:00
1281,/san_marshall_258_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,cousins,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states joking is allowed among cousins as a recurrent kin-based pattern.,"In no category is marriage allowed when joking is prohibited, but in two categories marriage is prohibited while joking is allowed, namely, among cousins and among the parents of offspring's spouse.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,The text treats cousins as a named kin category with licensed joking despite marriage prohibition.,2026-07-06T10:20:18.314887+00:00
1282,/san_marshall_258_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,parents of offspring's spouse,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly names parents of offspring's spouse as a recurring joking-allowed kin category.,"In no category is marriage allowed when joking is prohibited, but in two categories marriage is prohibited while joking is allowed, namely, among cousins and among the parents of offspring's spouse.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Phrase indicates a culturally recognized joking tie between those parental affinal categories.,2026-07-06T10:20:18.314887+00:00
1283,/san_marshall_259_1965_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,man,kin_role,woman who has the same name as his mother,kin_role,kinship,Text describes a culturally regulated joking prohibition tied to name-relatives (incest taboo) between these kin roles.,"From the !Kung point of view the custom has two values. It regulates the incest taboo in its furthermost extension, especially the joking relationship that symbolizes the taboo. (For example, a man should not marry or make sexual jokes with a woman who has the same name as his mother.)",name relationship / joking relationship,name relationship,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Example frames joking prohibition tied to shared personal name; broader 'name relatives' network implied across region.,2026-07-06T10:20:25.507571+00:00
1284,/san_marshall_259_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,anyone who has the name of his parents or offspring,kin_role,kinship,Text marks name-relatives as a category with joking allowed between ego and persons sharing parents'/offspring's names.,The fourth category comprises certain name-relatives. Ego should not marry (1) anyone who has the name of his parents or offspring;,name-relative (parent/offspring name),,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:20:37.558367+00:00
1285,/san_marshall_259_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,"the parent, offspring, or sibling of opposite sex of anyone who has the same name as ego",kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly lists these as a fourth category of name-relatives tied to marriage and joking permissions.,"Ego should not marry (2) the parent, offspring, or sibling of opposite sex of anyone who has the same name as ego;",name-relative (relatives of same-name person),,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,,2026-07-06T10:20:37.558367+00:00
1286,/san_marshall_259_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,anyone who has the same name as ego's spouse's parent or ego's son's or daughter's spouse,kin_role,kinship,"Text includes these specific name-based relatives in the same category, indicating patterned joking/licensed familiarity.",Ego should not marry (3) anyone who has the same name as ego's spouse's parent or ego's son's or daughter's spouse.,name-relative (spouse's parent/child's spouse name),,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,Asterisks in the document indicate varying allowances for joking in these name-relationships; specifics not given for each subcase.,2026-07-06T10:20:37.558367+00:00
1287,/san_marshall_260_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,cousins,kin_role,cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states the system allows joking with cousins though marriage is prohibited; this is a recurrent kinship-patterned liberty.,"I believe that the system's allowing joking with cousins, although it prohibits marriage with them, may mitigate the force of the incest taboo and accounts to some extent for the tolerance toward the breakers of this rule.",joking allowed with cousins,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:20:43.014813+00:00
