1299,/san_marshall_290_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Men,group,women,group,within_group,Text states men and women make jokes in recurrent joking relationships about sexual matters.,"As far as we know, only two general subjects are avoided in conversation. Men and women do not discuss sexual matters openly together except as they make jokes in the joking relationships.",joking relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:21:38.474484+00:00
1300,/san_marshall_294_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Men,group,Women,group,within_group,Text describes a culturally recognized recurrent joking relationship practiced between men and women.,"In view of this, the joking relationship has its interesting side. Men and women who have the joking relationship insult each other in a facetious way and also point out actual faults or remark on actual episodes which embarrass a person.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:21:42.827092+00:00
1301,/san_marshall_339_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Parents,kin_role,Offspring,kin_role,kinship,Text states parents and offspring must never make sexual jokes and observe avoidances—an institutionalized kinship avoidance.,"About 18 inches is a minimum but is sufficient. The taboo is applied without sex differentiation: men must not sit near men or women, or women near women or men if they do not have the joking relationship. (3) With the exception of siblings of any age, the same persons who avoid joking avoid using each other's names in direct address. They use a kin term instead or the men may use a respect term for each other—!nau, ‘old man’. (4) Brothers and sisters do not sit near each other when they reach adolescence and, after marriage, when they have dwelling-places of their own, do not enter each other's scherms (shelters). (5) Parents and SoWi and DaHu, in addition to observing the joking, sitting, and name avoidances, do not enter each other's scherms and do not speak to each other in the first few years of their affinal relationship.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,Combined quotes include multiple sentences mentioning parents must never make sexual jokes and general avoidances; explicit statement 'must never make sexual jokes' appears earlier in section.,2026-07-06T10:22:08.088034+00:00
1302,/san_marshall_339_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Parents and SoWi and DaHu,kin_role,Children-in-Law,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes parents and sons-/daughters-in-law (SoWi and DaHu) observing joking, sitting, and name avoidances and not speaking—institutionalized in-law avoidance.","Parents-In-Law And Children-In-Law

...
(5) Parents and SoWi and DaHu, in addition to observing the joking, sitting, and name avoidances, do not enter each other's scherms and do not speak to each other in the first few years of their affinal relationship. They are the only persons in !Kung society who must avoid speaking together.",,SoWi and DaHu,symmetric,avoidance,0.95,SoWi and DaHu appear as indigenous terms for affinal roles; relationship described as mutual avoidance.,2026-07-06T10:22:08.088034+00:00
1303,/san_marshall_339_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Brothers,kin_role,Sisters,kin_role,kinship,Text states brothers and sisters do not sit near each other at adolescence and avoid entering each other's shelters—institutionalized sibling avoidance.,"(4) Brothers and sisters do not sit near each other when they reach adolescence and, after marriage, when they have dwelling-places of their own, do not enter each other's scherms (shelters).",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:22:08.088034+00:00
1304,/san_marshall_340_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,members of the nuclear family and certain consanguineous relatives,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly links joking relationship to nuclear/consanguineous relatives ('one people').,"And, although they must not direct sexual jokes or insults to each other, they may laugh as heartily when they overhear the jokes as relatives do who have the joking relationship together.
The !Kung use the expression ‘We are one people’ when they refer to certain consanguineous relatives—e.g. any lineal kin and the close collaterals listed in Fig. 2",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:22:17.122795+00:00
1305,/san_marshall_341_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,siblings of parents (kin likened to parents' generation),kin_role,kinship,"Text says joking follows same rules with kin likened to parents' generation, a recurrent kinship-patterned liberty.","The kin who are likened, by being of the same generation, to his parents, to his offspring, and to his siblings, are those to whom a !Kung feels the marriage Regulation Of Marriage regulations are particularly directed. ... Joking is allowed or not as it is with ego's kin in these generations.",joking allowed with kin likened to parents' generation,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Entities framed as kin-role categories; symmetry not explicitly stated.,2026-07-06T10:22:29.441325+00:00
1306,/san_marshall_341_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,nephews and nieces (kin likened to offspring generation),kin_role,kinship,"Document links joking permissions to kin likened to one's offspring generation, indicating institutionalized kin joking.","Siblings of parents are ‘like’ parents, cousins are ‘like’ siblings, nephews and nieces are ‘like’ offspring. ... Joking is allowed or not as it is with ego's kin in these generations.",joking allowed with kin likened to offspring generation,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:22:29.441325+00:00
1307,/san_marshall_341_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,parents of ego's SoWi and DaHu,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly states ego may joke with these affines, marking a culturally recognized joking tie.","Parents of ego's SoWi and DaHu are terminologically of ego's generation and he may joke with them but he must not marry them, for that would make his Regulation Of Marriage So and SoWi or Da and DaHu stepbrother and stepsister to each other, which would be most awkward.",may joke with (affines),,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,SoWi and DaHu are affinal kin-terms from text; parents of these affines are treated as ego's generation.,2026-07-06T10:22:29.441325+00:00
1308,/san_marshall_342_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,WiSi,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states ego has a joking relationship with WiSi as an affine (kin-based pattern).,"Throughout the whole system (except among remote name relatives), ego maintains the same joking status with both members of a married couple, for example, with FaBr and FaBrWi, WiSi and WiSiHu, &c.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:22:49.161060+00:00
1309,/san_marshall_342_1959_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,person,HuBr,kin_role,kinship,Author explains ego 'feels like' his WiSiHu and treats related affines (HuBr) with joking status—kin-based institutional pattern.,"Marriages with HuBr or WiSi are highly preferred marriages. This makes ≠Toma ‘feel like’ his WiSiHu, he said, even though he is not married to her, and, as a result, his WiSiDa seems to him like his own daughter.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:22:49.161060+00:00
