1380,/san_silberbauer_65_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,all kin,group,joking or avoidance categories,group,kinship,Text defines kin as being classified into joking or avoidance categories as a reciprocal institutional system.,"All kin are in either the joking or the avoidance categories and the behaviour is reciprocal, i.e. if I am in a cousin's joking category, he is also in mine.",joking vs avoidance classification,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Represents the general institution of joking/avoidance across kin rather than a specific pair.,2026-07-06T10:28:31.454029+00:00
1381,/san_silberbauer_67_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,joking partners,kin_role,opposite sexes (potential marriageable),kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally sanctioned 'joking partners' ties between kin/sex categories tied to marriageability.,"It has been mentioned that joking partners of opposite sexes, if of appropriate ages, are considered to be potentially marriageable. The intimate relationships permitted between them give them a good opportunity68 to get to know each other, and for others to judge their suitability to each other, before marrying.",joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,Document does not name specific kin categories; coder treated 'joking partners' as a kin-role institution involving opposite-sex pairs.,2026-07-06T10:28:38.879326+00:00
1382,/san_silberbauer_68_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Grandparents,kin_role,Grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Text states alternate generations are institutionalized joking partners (grandparents with grandchildren).,"It is a feature of many cultures that a specially friendly or affectionate relationship exists between alternate generations. Among the G/wi, where Regulation Of Marriage these are joking partners, this serves the useful purpose of allowing the grandparents to act as a counterweight to the perhaps somewhat austere authority of the parents and also to explain the parents' point of view to the children on a more friendly basis.",joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:28:44.378712+00:00
1383,/san_sugawara_187_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,siblings-in-law of the opposite sex,kin_role,siblings-in-law of the opposite sex,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent joking/contact (giba-ku) specifically between opposite-sex siblings-in-law.,"The typical pattern of giba-ku is frequently observed between the residents of the same sex in very affinitive relationships with each other. In the context of visiting, the most interesting is the giba-ku interaction between siblings-in-law of the opposite sex.",joking relationship,giba-ku,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:29:22.483150+00:00
1384,/san_sugawara_187_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,adolescent or pubescent boys (residents),age_set,adolescent or pubescent boys (visitors),age_set,within_group,Play-fighting //gaikari-ku between adolescent boys is a recurrent joking relation across resident–visitor boundaries.,"The third type of bodily contact occurs in a distinctive pattern of interaction called //gaikari-ku. Typically, //gaikari-ku means the play fighting between adolescent or pubescent boys which is performed in a way similar to a boxing match, but the verbal exchange of jokes or quasi abuse is also referred to by the same term.",play fighting / joking relation,//gaikari-ku,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:29:22.483150+00:00
1385,/san_sugawara_200_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,adolescent boy,age_set,adult man,age_set,within_group,Greeting by an adolescent to an adult is culturally treated as banter; age roles pattern joking.,"Then an adolescent boy (Tabu:ka) who was lying near the open-air fire called jokingly to Hxara:, “≠Kai, Hxara:be”. Immediately, Hxara: approached and shook Tabu:ka roughly and even attempting to step on his head. Tabu:ka rolled about on the sand. crying “Stop it! Hxara:!” ... In spite of the violent way in which Hxara: treated Tabu:ka, both of them remained smiling.",banter,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:29:36.014444+00:00
1386,/san_sugawara_200_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,newcomer woman,group,attendant visitor,group,within_group,Mock blows and physical teasing followed a 'superfluous' greeting between women who share close ties; patterned social practice.,"After she sat down at a distance of 1 m from one of the resident women, she exchanged greeting with 3 older residents, one after another. And then, she called to !kaoba, “≠Kai”. Immediately, !kaoba stood up and approached kju:, sitting in face-to-face proximity to her. These two women exchanged mock blows with each other, laughing cheerfully.",mock blows / banter,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:29:36.014444+00:00
1387,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,adjacent generation,kin_role,kinship,Text states avoidance relationship applies to adjacent generation (kinship-based prohibition).,"1) Avoidance relationship is applied to the adjacent generation, except for mother's brother, father's sister, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1388,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,mother's brother,kin_role,kinship,"Mother's brother is explicitly excepted from avoidance, implying a distinct joking/ non-avoidance tie.","1) Avoidance relationship is applied to the adjacent generation, except for mother's brother, father's sister, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Text treats mother's brother as exception to avoidance and thus part of joking/allowed familiarity.,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1389,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,father's sister,kin_role,kinship,Father's sister is excepted from avoidance indicating a joking/allowed tie.,"1) Avoidance relationship is applied to the adjacent generation, except for mother's brother, father's sister, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1390,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,their spouses,kin_role,kinship,Spouses of the excepted relatives are also excepted from avoidance — culturally patterned privileged familiarity.,"1) Avoidance relationship is applied to the adjacent generation, except for mother's brother, father's sister, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Entity name 'their spouses' copied exactly from text; refers to spouses of the excepted kin.,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
