1392,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,siblings of the opposite sex,kin_role,kinship,"Opposite-sex siblings are explicitly excluded from same-generation joking, indicating a different relationship (avoidance).","2) Joking relationship is applied to the same generation, except siblings of the opposite sex, parallel cousin of the opposite sex, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1393,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,parallel cousin of the opposite sex,kin_role,kinship,Parallel cousins of opposite sex are excepted from joking; text frames this as a patterned kin-role exclusion.,"2) Joking relationship is applied to the same generation, except siblings of the opposite sex, parallel cousin of the opposite sex, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1394,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,their spouses,kin_role,kinship,Spouses of excluded same-generation relatives are also excluded from joking (explicitly listed).,"2) Joking relationship is applied to the same generation, except siblings of the opposite sex, parallel cousin of the opposite sex, and their spouses.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.8,This refers to spouses of the excluded categories in same generation.,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1395,/san_sugawara_25_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,alternate generations,kin_role,kinship,Author states joking relationship is applied to alternate generations — institutionalized cross-generational joking.,3) Joking relationship is applied to the persons belonging to the alternate generations.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:30:01.307983+00:00
1396,/san_sugawara_26_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,uncle/aunt in 'cross' position,kin_role,nephew/niece,kin_role,kinship,Text states the joking relationship principle applies between cross-position uncles/aunts and nephews/nieces.,"This high frequency of dyads in proximity might be due to the fact that all of these relations between uncle/aunt and nephew/niece are ‘cross’ relations (i.e., relations between ego and ego's mother's brother or ego's father's sister). In other words, the principle that the joking relationship is to be applied to the uncle or aunt in ‘cross’ position, though they are belonging to the adjacent generation to ego's, may be reflected in the above result.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:30:06.793923+00:00
1397,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,parent-in-law,kin_role,child-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text states physical contact is strongly avoided between parent-in-law and child-in-law—an institutionalized kin-role avoidance.,"Although the parent-in-law and child-in-law are very often in proximity with each other, physical contact is strongly avoided between them.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:30:31.205259+00:00
1398,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,siblings of the opposite sex,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Author reports contact is far less frequent between opposite-sex siblings—patterned avoidance based on kin role/sex.,"In summary in comparing close consanguineous or affinal relations, it is evident that contact is far less frequent between the siblings of the opposite sex than between those of the same sex.",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:30:31.205259+00:00
1399,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,"siblings-in-law (i.e., ego and ego's sibling's spouses or spouse's sibling)",kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly groups siblings-in-law and notes reduced contact—an institutionalized avoidance relation among affinal kin.,"This is also true for the relations between siblings-in-law (i.e., ego and ego's sibling's spouses or spouse's sibling).",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:30:31.205259+00:00
1400,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,persons of an adjacent upper generation,kin_role,persons of alternate generations,kin_role,kinship,Text contrasts required 'reserved' behavior toward adjacent upper generation with 'relaxed' behavior toward alternate generations—institutionalized generational interaction norms.,"Generally, it has been accepted that the joking-avoidance relationship is closely related to both sex and generation. Namely, ‘reserved’ or ‘respectful’ behavior is required toward the persons of an adjacent upper generation, while relaxed behavior is tolerated toward those of alternate generations.",joking-avoidance relationship,,mixed,unclear,0.75,Contrast encodes both avoidance (adjacent upper) and relaxed/joking (alternate); relation_type set to unclear because sentence frames both behaviors.,2026-07-06T10:30:31.205259+00:00
1401,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,males and females belonging to genealogically the same generation,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Statistical result shows contact is avoided between males and females of same genealogical generation—institutionalized kin/generation rule.,"Only for male-female dyads, are significant chi-square values obtained from the distributions of number of dyads in contact among both of the genealogical and biological generation categories. Namely, contact is apt to be avoided between males and females belonging to genealogically the same generation (X2=4.8153, p&0.05, df=1).",,,symmetric,avoidance,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:30:31.205259+00:00
1402,/san_sugawara_29_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,males and females belonging to biologically alternate generations,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,Text reports contact frequently occurs between males and females of biologically alternate generations—patterned relaxed/joking interaction.,"At the same time, contact tends to frequently occur between males and females belonging to biologically alternate generations (X2=7.3516, p&0.01, df=1).",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:30:31.205259+00:00
1403,/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,CH-6,person,KR-12,person,within_group,Text explicitly labels CH-6–KR-12 interaction as a 'joking relation' between non-consanguine males.,Especially the relations between CH-6 and these 2 adolescent males very properly fall under the concept of the ‘joking relation’.,joking relation,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Recorded episodes show playful physical teasing initiated by KR-12 and reciprocal tolerant response by CH-6.,2026-07-06T10:30:40.890567+00:00
1404,/san_sugawara_31_1984_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,CH-6,person,HO-13,person,within_group,Ethnographer describes CH-6–HO-13 interactions as part of the recurring 'joking relation'.,Especially the relations between CH-6 and these 2 adolescent males very properly fall under the concept of the ‘joking relation’.,joking relation,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Recorded incidents include CH-6 fumbling HO-13's belly and related playful behavior.,2026-07-06T10:30:40.890567+00:00
1405,/san_tanaka_105_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,babam and masi (grandparents),kin_role,n!odim and n!odisi (grandchildren),kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly places grandparents and grandchildren in the joking category; recurrent kin-role pattern.,"Grandparents (babam and masi) and grandchildren (n!odim and n!odisi), separated from ego by one generation, enter into the joking category.",joking category,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:31:02.229191+00:00
1406,/san_tanaka_105_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,same-sex siblings (for a male ego: brothers kiyaxom and dabaxom),kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,Author states same-sex siblings (brothers for male ego) are held in a joking relationship with ego.,"Siblings are divided according to sex; for a male ego, his brothers (kiyaxom and dabaxom) are held in a joking relationship and his sisters (kiyaxosi and dabaxosi) in an avoidance relationship.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Phrase framed from perspective of male ego; applies similarly for same-sex siblings of other egos.,2026-07-06T10:31:02.229191+00:00
1407,/san_tanaka_105_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,parents' opposite-sex siblings (those called by the terms for grandparents),kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly lists parents' opposite-sex siblings among joking-category kin relative to ego.,"A parent's opposite-sex siblings (those called by the terms for grandparents) and one's cross cousins (those called by the terms for grandchildren) are in the joking category,",joking category,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:31:02.229191+00:00
1408,/san_tanaka_105_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,opposite-sex cross cousins (those called by the terms for grandchildren),kin_role,ego,kin_role,kinship,Author explicitly places opposite-sex cross cousins in joking category with ego.,"A parent's opposite-sex siblings (those called by the terms for grandparents) and one's cross cousins (those called by the terms for grandchildren) are in the joking category,",joking category,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:31:02.229191+00:00
1409,/san_tanaka_105_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,ego,kin_role,"ego's grandparents, same-sex siblings, opposite-sex cross cousins, and parents' opposite-sex siblings",kin_role,kinship,Passage summarizes recurrent free relations (joking) between ego and these specific kin categories.,"Within the joking category, particularly free relations hold between ego and ego's grandparents, same-sex siblings, opposite-sex cross cousins, and parents' opposite-sex siblings, to the extent that they may exchange gossip and criticism about others, tell sexual stories, play pranks on each other, and so on.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Composite assertion grouping multiple explicitly listed joking kin relations described together in the text.,2026-07-06T10:31:02.229191+00:00
1410,/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,nuclear family,kin_role,collaterals,kin_role,kinship,Text states kin terms assign joking or respect between nuclear family and collaterals as culturally patterned.,"Standard !Kung kinship terms are structured by relative age and generation, and each term carries with it a relationship of joking or respect that delimits eligibility for marriage and a range of permitted behavior.",relationship of joking or respect,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:31:27.014320+00:00
1411,/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,collaterals,kin_role,affinal kin,kin_role,kinship,Ethnographer notes kin terms (and corresponding joking/respect) generally distinguish collaterals from affines.,"Standard !Kung kinship terms are structured by relative age and generation, and each term carries with it a relationship of joking or respect that delimits eligibility for marriage and a range of permitted behavior.",relationship of joking or respect,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:31:27.014320+00:00
1412,/san_wiessner_106_1994_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,spouses' siblings and siblings' spouses,kin_role,collateral kin,kin_role,kinship,"Text states spouses' siblings and siblings' spouses are given terms not distinguishing them from collateral kin, implying same joking/respect rules.",Only spouses' siblings and siblings' spouses are given terms that do not distinguish them from collateral kin.,same kinship joking/respect terms,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:31:27.014320+00:00
1413,/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,people sharing their name,group,ego's nuclear family (core),kin_role,kinship,Text says name-sharers are given generational terms carrying joking status relative to the nuclear family.,People sharing their name are given a generational term carrying the appropriate joking status (Marshall 1976).,joking status extended by homonymous method,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Document frames joking status as tied to kin-term extension via shared names; units coded as 'people sharing their name' vs 'core' nuclear family.,2026-07-06T10:31:36.087661+00:00
1414,/san_wiessner_108_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,one who shares a name with a person's sister,kin_role,person's sister,kin_role,kinship,"The text explicitly treats the name-sharer as 'like' the sister, indicating a patterned kin-based joking relation.",The one who shares a name with a person's sister is said to be ‘like’ his sister (note that the San clearly do not say that this person then ‘is’ his sister as the term giving and term receiving groups are kept distinct).,like (name-based joking extension),,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,"Extracted as a patterned, recurrent kin-based teasing/licensed familiarity via the homonymous method.",2026-07-06T10:31:36.087661+00:00
1415,/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,one's own generation,kin_role,one's own generation,kin_role,kinship,Text states peers in the same generation are permitted a reciprocal joking relationship.,"Essentially one is allowed to joke with everybody in one's own generation except siblings of the opposite sex and their spouses, one's spouse's siblings of the same sex and their spouses as well as everybody in alternate generations to yours.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,"Exclusions (siblings, in-laws, alternate generations) are avoidance rules and not extracted as joking ties.",2026-07-06T10:31:48.642132+00:00
1416,/san_wiessner_114_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,homonymous method (individual for whom one is given a term),kin_role,person given the kin term (ego's referent),kin_role,kinship,Homonymous kin-terms transfer the same joking relationship reciprocally to the holder of the term.,"The homonymous method of applying terms again takes precedence over the generational and one has the same joking relationship as the individual for whom one is given a term. The joking relationship is always reciprocal and as soon as one person has a kin term for another, both know within what bounds they must behave.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Models how kin-term assignment creates recurring licensed teasing between ego and referent's relations.,2026-07-06T10:31:48.642132+00:00
1417,/san_wiessner_144_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,hxaro partner,group,joking relative,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes joking behaviour between hxaro partners who are joking relatives, a patterned kin-based licensed teasing tied to exchange.","A San may just wait quietly for a return or if his hxaro partner lives nearby and is a joking relative, after a respectable period of time, a minimum of two weeks, the giver may go to the receiver and jokingly remind him that he has a debt.",,hxaro,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Hxaro is the delayed-exchange relationship; joking reminders are a culturally patterned licensed familiarity between kin-role partners.,2026-07-06T10:31:58.464049+00:00
1418,/san_wiessner_355_1988_sv,SouthernAfrica,san,Nunamiut,ethnic group,joking partners,group,within_group,Text states Nunamiut use joking partnerships as an institutional channel to make non-kin into quasi-kin.,"If it is not possible for them to find a common relative other channels are still open—a name relationship, a dancing partnership, joking partnership, etc..",joking partnership,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,Document lists joking partnership as a culturally recognized channel among the Nunamiut; textual detail on specific paired categories is limited.,2026-07-06T10:32:05.718576+00:00
1419,/shona_aschwanden_175_1982_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,muzukuru,kin_role,Tendai,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly says Tendai and the muzukuru have a culturally established joking relationship tied to marriage roles.,"Tendai treats Muzhara as kindly as she would treat Farai, thus establishing a so-called joking relationship with him.",so-called joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Muzhara is the muzukuru (nephew) acting in the ritual/marriage context toward the bride (Tendai).,2026-07-06T10:32:13.845046+00:00
1420,/shona_aschwanden_74_1982,SouthernAfrica,shona,man,kin_role,varamu,kin_role,within_group,Text states varamu are women with whom a man can have a joking relationship; clearly a patterned cultural tie.,"Adolescent boys have hardly any sexual contact with girls. However, possible partners in intimate relationships (which may lead to sexual intercourse) are the varamu — those women with whom a man can have a joking relationship (cf below).",,varamu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T10:32:22.759974+00:00
1421,/shona_aschwanden_75_1982,SouthernAfrica,shona,the youth,kin_role,his elder brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,Text describes a culturally sanctioned recurring joking relationship between youth and elder brother's wife.,"During this period of his life the boy is also given more thorough instruction on sexual matters by some older members of the family such as, for instance, an elder brother and his wife. With the latter the youth is on special terms — the so-called “joking relationship”.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:32:29.217428+00:00
1422,/shona_aschwanden_78_1982_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,growing Karanga girl,kin_role,her sisters' husbands,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly calls the permitted interaction between girls and sisters' husbands a 'joking relationship'.,"At the beginning of puberty, the girls are separated from the boys, but despite this it is then that the first sexual “concessions” are made and the young girls learn more about their future sex-life. The growing Karanga girl is permitted to mix with her sisters' husbands, to eat and joke with them, the latter including the exchange of obscenities. This is what is called a joking relationship.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Culturally patterned permitted teasing between adolescent females and their sisters' husbands; intended to ease potential future polygyny.,2026-07-06T10:32:36.202140+00:00
1423,/shona_ashwanden_277_1987_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,muzukuru,kin_role,dead man's clan,clan,kinship,Text explicitly links muzukuru to a culturally recognized 'joking relationship' involving the dead man's clan.,"By drinking the “beer of the water”, however, the muzukuru represents still something else. He demonstrates, the Karanga explain, that he himself reaps the “benefit” of the “amniotic fluid” because he can marry women from the dead man's clan (cf p. *, the joking relationship).",the joking relationship,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,,2026-07-06T10:32:44.293018+00:00
1424,/shona_bourdillon_49_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,the sister's son,kin_role,his mother's brother,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent culturally recognized avuncular joking privileges (ukama hwokutamba).,"This relationship is cordial and intimate: the Shona refer to it as ukama hwokutamba (‘a relationship of playing’) and anthropologists call it a joking relationship. The sister's son can normally avail himself of anything belonging to his mother's brother without even asking for it, and no action should be taken against him.",ukama hwokutamba,ukama hwokutamba,mixed,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:32:51.973424+00:00
1425,/shona_bullock_81_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,Maromo,kin_role,Paratema,kin_role,kinship,Explicit avuncular joking license between mother's brother and nephew described as ritualized privileges.,"To Paratema, Maromo is muzukuru, his sister's son, and he is privileged in many respects above the sons of the house. He takes precedence over them at meals, sitting next his sekuru, and, in a joking way, may rob him even of his raiment.",,muzukuru,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:33:11.646036+00:00
1426,/shona_bullock_81_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,Maromo,kin_role,Marutenga,person,kinship,Joking licence extended to the mother's brother's wife; culturally patterned with limits and blame.,"extended to some extent with Marutenga, Paratema's wife; and if Maromo exceeds its limits with her, it is she who will be blamed by Paratema, for there is a very cordial bond between sekuru and muzukuru.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Marutenga is an individual but cited as role-holder (Paratema's wife) in the joking institution.,2026-07-06T10:33:11.646036+00:00
1427,/shona_bullock_81_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,Maromo,person,Whera,person,within_group,Recurrent privileged familiarity: Whera acts as bride-confidante and greets Maromo with licensed familiarity.,"He is on excellent terms with her, and she runs out to greet him when she sees him coming, calling out “Our husbands have arrived”, and takes his impedimenta, putting them in her hut. He is the honoured guest during his visit.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,Whera's exact kin-role is not specified; treated as patterned privileged familiarity within the society.,2026-07-06T10:33:11.646036+00:00
1428,/shona_fortune_72_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,husband,kin_role,ritual friend,group,within_group,Text describes licensed banter between ritual friends where husbands praise their own and tease friends' wives.,The most mature love poetry appears to be that composed during marriage. In the examples collected the praises form part of the licensed banter between ritual friends. Each one praises his own wife for her qualities and criticizes the wife of his friend.,licensed banter,,symmetric,banter,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:33:19.304930+00:00
1429,/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,the nephew (muzukuru),kin_role,his aunt,kin_role,kinship,Text states nephew and his aunt exchange jokes and retorts as a culturally recognized allowance.,"Thus a nephew may become familiar with his aunt because he holds this position in the family and enjoys this peculiar freedom in his uncle's village. He can joke with his aunt who can retort in the same vein saying that he (the muzukuru) is a muroora, implying that he is like a young wife as he is descended from her husband's sister.",joking relationship,muzukuru; muroora,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:33:29.465855+00:00
1430,/shona_gelfand_41_1973_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,the nephew (muzukuru),kin_role,his cousins' wives,kin_role,kinship,Author explicitly notes joking relationship is more in evidence between nephew and his cousins' wives.,"However, the joking relationship is much more in evidence between the nephew and his cousins' wives.",joking relationship,muzukuru,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:33:29.465855+00:00
1431,/shona_gelfand_42_1979_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,ritual friends,group,the bereaved,kin_role,within_group,"Ritual friends repeatedly console mourners and make jokes during funerary events, a culturally patterned role.","The ritual friends try to console the bereaved, and they do most of the cooking and even make jokes.",ritual friends consoling and joking,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:33:39.951023+00:00
1432,/shona_gelfand_51_1968_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,two individuals unrelated to one another,person,sahwira (each other),person,within_group,Text describes ritualized lifelong joking friendship (sahwira) between two unrelated individuals in the same society.,"Of great value to an African is the bond of friendship formed between two individuals unrelated to one another. This lifelong friend is called sahwira ... Social anthropologists have referred sometimes to such a friendship as a joking relationship, thus stressing the fact that the friends can tease or rag one another without either losing his temper.","sahwira (ritual bondship, joking relationship)",sahwira,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,"Document describes a formalized, reciprocal joking friendship (sahwira) with ceremonial establishment and lifetime duration.",2026-07-06T10:33:48.605987+00:00
1433,/shona_gelfand_59_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,ritual friends (sahwira),group,ritual friends (sahwira),group,within_group,"Text states sahwira routinely joke with one another, permitting serious accusations as jest; patterned ritual pair relation.",The only time a person can call another a witch is when two ritual friends ( sahwira) joke with one another and do not take the matter seriously.,sahwira,sahwira,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:33:54.328733+00:00
1434,/shona_gelfand_62_1968_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,ritual friends,kin_role,ritual friends,kin_role,within_group,Text explicitly names a recurring joking form practiced between 'ritual friends'.,"There is, however, a special type of scolding among ritual friends which takes the form of a joke and is not taken seriously.",special type of scolding among ritual friends,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Term 'ritual friends' treated as a culturally defined recurring joking partnership; reciprocity implied.,2026-07-06T10:33:59.759160+00:00
1435,/shona_gelfand_84_1973_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,vasahwira,group,vasahwira,group,within_group,"Text states scolding-as-joke is permitted among ritual friends (vasahwira), a recurrent culturally recognized tie.",The first takes the form of a joke and is permitted only among ritual friends ( vasahwira) who allow derogatory things to be said by each other without their causing annoyance.,ritual friends,vasahwira,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:34:05.936635+00:00
1436,/shona_holleman_46_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,Sekuru,kin_role,muzukuru,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly names a 'joking relationship' between grandparents and grandchildren in Hera kinship.,"I think it is clear that the ‘joking relationship’, between grandparents and grandchildren, that is, between members and uterine members of a lineage, has a logical and structural basis in Hera kinship.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Granddaughters (vazukuru kadzi) mentioned as enjoying privileged treatment similar to brothers.,2026-07-06T10:34:16.150399+00:00
1437,/shona_holleman_46_sv,SouthernAfrica,shona,vakarahwe,kin_role,vakwasha,kin_role,kinship,"Describes recurrent, culturally expected teasing/respect relations between parents-in-law (mukarahwe) and children-in-law (mukwasha).","The basis of this relationship is partly brought out by the term mukarahwe. The literal translation, ‘stone-sister’, is also a literal description of the position of a ‘father-in-law’ in his son-in-law's kraal... The general basis for this relationship is, therefore, clearly established: the vakarahwe are entitled to respectful treatment from their vakwashabecause they are the ‘higher’ and the ‘elder’ ones.",,"mukarahwe, mukwasha, tezwara",non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Text notes mukwasha must honour/fear and tolerate bullying from wife's younger brothers; asymmetric power and licensed liberties.,2026-07-06T10:34:16.150399+00:00
1440,/tonga_cliggett_114_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,grandmother,kin_role,grandson,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly calls grandmother-grandson a common form of 'joking relations' in this society.,"This grandmother-grandson relationship represents a common form of “joking relations,” as research throughout Africa documents (Colson 1951; Murdock 1959; Stucki 1995).",joking relations,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:34:42.887904+00:00
1441,/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a man,kin_role,his mother's brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,Text states a culturally recognized 'free joking relationship' between a man and his mother's brother's wife.,"Here a man has a free joking relationship with his mother's brother's wife, or with his sister's son's wife, and he is often suspect of being her lover.",free joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:34:54.316770+00:00
1442,/tonga_colson_100_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a man,kin_role,his sister's son's wife,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly describes a recurrent joking tie between a man and his sister's son's wife.,"Here a man has a free joking relationship with his mother's brother's wife, or with his sister's son's wife, and he is often suspect of being her lover.",free joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:34:54.316770+00:00
1443,/tonga_colson_140_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,men,group,women,group,within_group,Ritualized cross-dressing in masabe dance creates recurrent joking/entertainment between gender groups.,"During masabe dancing, which may be performed either as a curing ritual or as pure entertainment, either men or women may dress as members of the opposite sex. Women masabe leaders carry an axe.",masabe dancing cross-dressing,masabe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Tied to ritual performance; patterned licensed role-reversal between genders.,2026-07-06T10:35:00.922520+00:00
1444,/tonga_colson_145_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,matrilineal group,lineage,clan joking-partners,clan,within_group,Text describes recurrent ritualized joking by clan joking-partners with the person's matrilineal group after purification.,"If a person tries to commit suicide and fails, then his matrilineal group must find a goat to kill for him so that the clan joking-partners can come and joke with him and eat the meat.",clan joking-partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Generic named units in text; extracted as matrilineal group ↔ clan joking-partners (within society).,2026-07-06T10:35:07.536960+00:00
1445,/tonga_colson_15_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan,clan,paired clan,clan,within_group,Text states clans are linked/paired in bujwanyina where joking is a recurrent aspect between clans.,"Each clan is linked or paired with several others individually in what is known as bujwanyina, a relationship of which joking is only one aspect of a wider range of duties and rights.",bujwanyina,bujwanyina,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Document does not list specific clan names; extracted generic paired-clan joking institution (bujwanyina).,2026-07-06T10:35:14.172710+00:00
1446,/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Baleya,clan,Bantanga,clan,within_group,Text names Baleya and Bantanga as (possible) clan joking-partners within the Valley region.,"Upper River clans do not have joking-partners, with the possible exception of the Baleya and Bantanga,",joking-partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:35:24.363930+00:00
1447,/tonga_colson_177_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a lineage,lineage,various others of its own clan,clan,within_group,Text describes lineages linked in a permanent alliance within their clan as silufulo partners (institutionalized linkage).,a lineage is linked with various others of its own clan in a permanent alliance as silufulo partners. Silufulo can be translated as ‘calabash man’ and refers to the calabash ladle with which offerings are poured.,silufulo partners,silufulo,symmetric,alliance,0.85,Silufulo described as a permanent alliance within the clan; may involve ritualized licensed behavior related to offerings.,2026-07-06T10:35:24.363930+00:00
1448,/tonga_colson_178_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan,clan,clan,clan,within_group,Text describes permanent inter-clan joking-partnerships (bujwanyina) with recurrent roles and obligations.,"Briefly, each clan is linked with a number of others in a permanent joking-relationship known as bujwanyina. Clan joking-partners perform various services for one another, particularly during funerals.",bujwanyina (joking-relationship),bujwanyina,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Generic inter-clan joking partnerships described for Plateau and Valley Tonga; obligations indicate institutionalization.,2026-07-06T10:35:36.950542+00:00
1449,/tonga_colson_178_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Sinafwala,village,Madonda,village,cross_group,Drum-team songs publicly mock another named village; recurring use of song-makers to publicize scandal constitutes patterned licensed insult.,"The young people of Sinafwala danced gaily to a song that described the thievish nature of the people of Madonda Village after the latter had stolen hippo meat apportioned to Sinafwala. Both Sinafwala and Madonda belong to the CHEZIA Drum, so the song of the hippo never became a Drum song.",Drum-song public shaming,,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.75,"Madonda's refusal to dance suggests asymmetric acceptance of the mockery; institutions (Drum, song-makers) enable inter-village public teasing.",2026-07-06T10:35:36.950542+00:00
1450,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clans,clan,the same clans paired together in all the territory stretching from the Zambezi to the Plateau,clan,within_group,"Text describes recurring, constant clan-to-clan joking alliances across Tonga territory.",they are a constant one with the same clans paired together in all the territory stretching from the Zambezi to the Plateau.,clan joking-relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,General statement of institutionalized clan pairs across Tonga area; specific clan names not listed.,2026-07-06T10:35:54.536981+00:00
1451,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan of the sikatongo,clan,Clans in a joking-relationship with the clan of the sikatongo,clan,within_group,Explicitly states certain clans have a recurrent joking-relationship with the clan of the sikatongo and set ritual duties.,Clans in a joking-relationship with the clan of the sikatongo have their set duties in the building or repair of a shrine and in the various rites performed at the shrine.,joking-relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Text links joking-relationship to specific ritual obligations involving the sikatongo clan.,2026-07-06T10:35:54.536981+00:00
1452,/tonga_colson_179_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Tonga,ethnic group,Lozi,ethnic group,cross_group,Author states Lozi treat Tonga men as joking-partners in an intertribal joking-relationship.,"Then Tonga men are treated as joking-partners by Lozi, and the two help one another.",Lozi-Tonga joking-relationship,bujwanyina,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Intertribal joking tie; author notes it may be a relatively recent development among younger men.,2026-07-06T10:35:54.536981+00:00
1453,/tonga_colson_180_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,silufulo lineage (grandchildren),lineage,lineage of the dead (lineage mates of the dead),lineage,within_group,Ritualized recurring teasing by 'grandchildren' of silufulo toward lineage mates at funerals; institutionalized within society.,"Whenever joking is institutionalized in a ritual context, the duty is performed by ‘grandchildren'. These are never the grandchildren of the lineage involved; they are grandchildren of the silufulo lineage. During a funeral grain from the store of the dead man or woman is poured upon the grave. The grandchildren of the silufulo lineage are the ones to scramble for it while they jest with one another and with lineage mates of the dead.",grandchildren (ritual jesters) / clan joking-partners,silufulo,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"Text also notes Middle River variant where clan joking-partners perform this duty, but no specific clan names are given.",2026-07-06T10:36:01.996743+00:00
1454,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Middle River clans,clan,other Middle River clans,clan,within_group,"Text states Middle River clans are linked as clan joking-partners, a recurrent institutionalized tie within the society.","Middle River clans are linked together as clan joking-partners in a manner comparable to that found among the Plateau Tonga,",clan joking-partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Document does not specify particular paired clans; encoded as general inter-clan joking network within Middle River.,2026-07-06T10:36:27.978461+00:00
1455,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Middle River man,kin_role,wives of his sister's sons,kin_role,kinship,Explicitly described recurring joking basis between a Middle River man and these affinal women (institutionalized kin joking).,A Middle River man is on a joking basis with the wives of both his sister's sons and his mother's brothers.,joking basis with affinal wives,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:36:27.978461+00:00
1456,/tonga_colson_22_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Middle River man,kin_role,wives of his mother's brothers,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states recurring joking ties between a Middle River man and these affinal women (institutionalized kin joking).,A Middle River man is on a joking basis with the wives of both his sister's sons and his mother's brothers.,joking basis with affinal wives,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:36:27.978461+00:00
1457,/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,grandfather,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,Text describes elders teasing grandchildren as culturally appropriate joking between kin roles.,"A small child may rush home in dismay under the teasing of an old man or woman, to be told by its mother that this is appropriate joking because the teaser is his ‘grandfather’ or ‘grandmother’ and that he must joke back.",appropriate joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:36:38.382893+00:00
1458,/tonga_colson_268_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,"Explicitly states 'free and easy joking' characterizes dealings of cross-cousins, indicating institutionalized joking.",The free and easy joking which marks the dealings of cross-cousins is very different from the relatively restrained behaviour which spouses should observe towards one another in public.,free and easy joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:36:38.382893+00:00
1459,/tonga_colson_322_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,basimukowa,kin_role,basyanausi,kin_role,kinship,Text describes these two matrilineal generational roles as involved in joking-relationship conflicts.,"for the son would have become a ‘brother-in-law’ of his father's generation, and thus on a joking relationship basis with them. The old rules which prevented the two generations of a matrilineal group from appearing as basimukowa and basyanausi in the same marriage also prevented the possibility of such conflicts.",joking relationship basis,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:36:50.035577+00:00
1460,/tonga_colson_325_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,clan,clan,clan joking-partners,clan,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, institutionalized clan-level joking obligations between partnered clans within the society.","This is still done in clan joking-partners for a public shaming of the culprit.
...joking-partners have a general obligation to cast slighting remarks which touch upon one another's misdeeds.",clan joking-partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Document does not name specific clans; coded as clan↔clan joking partnership described as institutionalized.,2026-07-06T10:37:01.252108+00:00
1461,/tonga_colson_327_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,children of brother,kin_role,children of sister,kin_role,kinship,Text states these groups were formerly 'friendly joking partners'—a recurrent kin-based joking institution.,It is possible that the change reflects the increased tension between the children of brother and sister—for they now see themselves on many occasions as rivals for the wealth of the single man through whom they trace their relationship rather than as friendly joking partners.,friendly joking partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:37:10.955301+00:00
1462,/tonga_colson_45_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,those of the same clan,clan,clan joking-partners,clan,within_group,"Text identifies recurring, institutionalized 'clan joking-partners' relationship between clans within Tonga society.","Those of the same clan, or those who were clan joking-partners, ought not to enslave or hold one another in slavery, but they could turn a blind eye to encroachments upon the life or freedom of one who could only cite his membership in a clan as a claim upon them.",clan joking-partners,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:37:18.920415+00:00
1463,/tonga_colson_53_1967_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,a clan,clan,father's clan,clan,kinship,Text describes culturally recognized 'clan joking partner' status allocated between clans and a father's clan as artificial kinship.,"In Middle River region, I had a quasi-kinship position based on the fact that years before the Plateau Tonga had playfully allocated me to a clan and a father's clan. Almost everyone could therefore address me as a fellow clansman, a child of the clan, a grandmother through clan, an affine, or as a clan joking partner.",clan joking partner,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,,2026-07-06T10:37:28.751638+00:00
1464,/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Children,kin_role,sisters' children,kin_role,kinship,Text states children and sisters' children are cross-cousins with licensed familiarity (institutionalized joking).,"The same generation of basyanausi also has responsibility for and authority over the generation of their sisters' sons, but this authority is different in kind from that which it exercises over its children. Children and sisters' children therefore are in somewhat of an equivocal position in relation to one another. They call one another ‘cross-cousin’ and have a relationship of licensed familiarity.",cross-cousin; licensed familiarity,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:37:42.612851+00:00
1465,/tonga_colson_55_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,Those not of this generation,kin_role,his generation,kin_role,kinship,Explicit statement that members 'not of this generation' are joking partners and equals to the person's generation.,"A child is bound primarily to its own father and through him to his generation whether of the same branch or not. It is to this generation that a person owes respect and to its members he turns for assistance. Those not of this generation are joking partners and equals, and would be treated as ‘fathers’ only in the absence of any member of the paternal generation.",joking partners and equals,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Entities quoted verbatim from text as generational categories rather than named kin terms.,2026-07-06T10:37:42.612851+00:00
1466,/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states joking and easy familiarity pertains to cross-cousin relationships; clearly kinship-tied institutional joking.,"This last, however, causes little difficulty since joking and easy familiarity pertains to all such relationships.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Extracted as general recurrent joking among cross-cousin categories (kin-role based).,2026-07-06T10:37:57.375567+00:00
1467,/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,grandfathers' matrilineal groups,lineage,their grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Passage explicitly describes a joking relationship between grandchildren and their grandfathers' matrilineal groups.,"Between men and women and their grandfathers, and indeed all members of the grandfathers' matrilineal groups, there exists a joking relationship comparable in many ways to that which is found between cross-cousins.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:37:57.375567+00:00
1468,/tonga_colson_56_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,paternal cross-cousins,kin_role,father's mother's brother,kin_role,kinship,"Text equates paternal cross-cousins with the father's mother's brother and notes familiarity, indicating recurring joking relation.","Her brother is usually called ‘grandfather’, though he is likely to be treated with familiarity since he is on the same generation level as paternal cross-cousins, who are equated with him, and he is not of the father's generation.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Relation framed by generational equivalence and familiarity; directionality not specified.,2026-07-06T10:37:57.375567+00:00
1469,/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,sister's husband,kin_role,brother's wife,kin_role,kinship,Text states these affines are grouped as 'mukwaasi' and share a joking relationship comparable to cross-cousins.,"Sister's husband and brother's wife (both sexes speaking), husband's sister, husband's brother, wife's brother and wife's sister are all equated, and with them are grouped spouse's grandparents and grandchild's spouse. All alike are mukwaasi, and they share a joking relationship comparable to that between cross-cousins.",mukwaasi,mukwaasi,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Represents the institutional joking tie among affines of the same generation; the passage groups many roles together.,2026-07-06T10:38:14.281283+00:00
1470,/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,mother's brother,kin_role,father,kin_role,kinship,Mother's brother and father of same children are described as having good fellowship within the mukwaasi relationship.,"Eventually as mother's brother and father of the same children, they may be at odds, but equally the existence of these children forms a common bond between them whatever happens to the marriage.",mukwaasi,mukwaasi,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Text implies both convivial joking and potential rivalry; coded mixed symmetry because roles have different social positions but share joking ties.,2026-07-06T10:38:14.281283+00:00
1471,/tonga_colson_58_1958_sv,SouthernAfrica,tonga,cross-cousins,kin_role,affines of the same generation (mukwaasi),kin_role,kinship,"The term for these affines is interchangeable with that for cross-cousin, indicating comparable joking relations.","All alike are mukwaasi, and they share a joking relationship comparable to that between cross-cousins. Indeed, the term for affine of this generation is interchangeable with that for cross-cousin and for spouse.",mukwaasi / comparable to cross-cousins,mukwaasi,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Covers equivalence of cross-cousin joking relations and the mukwaasi affine category (including spouses possibly).,2026-07-06T10:38:14.281283+00:00
1472,/tsonga_jaques_340_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,hahane,kin_role,nephews and nieces,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent licensed familiarity and joking between paternal aunt and her nephews/nieces.,"For the majority of the Thonga people, a great familiarity with the hahane seems to be the rule. All kinds of jokes can be made in her presence, and a girl, even a boy, will not be ashamed to tell her all the foolish things they have done. She is the proper “confidente” of her nephews and nieces.",confidente / great familiarity,hahane,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:38:26.394500+00:00
1473,/tsonga_jaques_343_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,paternal grandparents,kin_role,maternal grandparents,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes culturally patterned cordial, jocular familiarity between the two grandparent sets.","It may be recorded here that a special pattern regulates the behaviour of my paternal and maternal grandparents towards one another. My paternal grandfathers call my maternal grandfather and grandmother, masebe, in the Khosen country (Portuguese East), Ngwatsweku or Ntswalane in the Transvaal. My maternal grandparents use the same term when addressing my paternal grandparents. The behaviour is of cordial and jocular familiarity. The men joke especially with the women of the other couple.",,"masebe, Ngwatsweku or Ntswalane",symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:38:39.136960+00:00
1474,/tsonga_jaques_343_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,maternal uncles and their wives,kin_role,ntukulu,kin_role,kinship,Described as a culturally defined relationship (kokwana–ntukulu) with maximal familiarity and patterned behaviour.,"It is not our intention to repeat here the excellent account given by Mr. Junod of the nature of the relationship between a kokwana and a ntukulu. (See Life, I, p. – .) I shall only, as a complement to the description of this pattern of behaviour, give a typical account by one of my informants.

(b) The maternal uncles and their wives.—Among the Ronga, Zulu, Sotho, Pedi and Venda, these uncles have the special name of malume. Thongas, of the Transvaal, under the influence of these tribes, use also the appellation of malume, but the proper Thonga term is kokwana. In this relationship, familiarity attains its maximum.",,kokwana,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,ntukulu is the reciprocal kin role (nephew/niece); text implies patterned joking/familiarity.,2026-07-06T10:38:39.136960+00:00
1475,/tsonga_jaques_347_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,a woman (the man's wife),kin_role,his younger brothers (the husband's younger brothers),kin_role,kinship,Text states recurrent joking (bungunya) between a wife and her husband's younger brothers—an institutionalized in-law joking tie.,"Reciprocally also, a woman will avoid her husband's elder brothers, and joke (bungunya) with his younger brothers.",bungunya,bungunya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:38:46.292817+00:00
1476,/tsonga_junod_214_1927,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,ntukulu,kin_role,maternal uncles,kin_role,kinship,Text states ntukulu has culturally sanctioned right to tease/insult his maternal uncles during mourning.,"The ntukulu, as we shall see, is very free with his maternal uncles. He has the right of teasing them, even insulting them.",very free; right of teasing,ntukulu,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:38:52.841052+00:00
1477,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,husband (my),kin_role,daughter of my wife's brother (Shaputa versus Mboza),kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally permitted intimate joking liberties between husband and his wife's brother's daughter (nsati).,In this first category is included the daughter of my wife's brother (Shaputa versus Mboza). With her the relations are perhaps even more free than with my wife's younger sisters. I call her nsati. The intimacy is so great that I can hold her breasts (tamele mabele).,nsati,nsati,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:39:08.603892+00:00
1478,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,husband (my),kin_role,all the girls belonging to my bakoñwana,kin_role,kinship,"Author states all girls of his bakoñwana occupy same open, joking/marriage-possible position (tinamu), implying patterned familiarity.","As already pointed out, all the girls belonging to my bakoñwana are in the same position towards me. I can marry them all… if I am rich enough to “lobola” them.",tinamu / bakoñwana,bakoñwana,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:39:08.603892+00:00
1479,/tsonga_junod_247_1927_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,husband (my),kin_role,wives of my elder brothers,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly: these wives are inherited and 'Relations with them are easy. We joke together', indicating institutionalized joking.","The second category of tinamu includes those women whom I might marry, not on account of the prior right of a group to the women of another group, but because I might inherit them. They are the wives of my elder brothers. If their husbands die, I am their legal heir. Relations with them are easy. We joke together, but there is more reserve towards these tinamu than towards those of the first category.",tinamu,tinamu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:39:08.603892+00:00
1480,/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,a woman,kin_role,his younger brothers (tinamu),kin_role,kinship,"Text states women generally have free, joking intercourse with husband's younger brothers (tinamu).","A woman will generally respect the elder brothers of her husband (bengi), and hold very free intercourse with his younger brothers (tinamu), and vice-versa.",tinamu,tinamu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:39:23.463936+00:00
1481,/tsonga_sunod_248_1927_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,a man,kin_role,those of his elder brothers (tinamu),kin_role,kinship,"Text states men play with the wives of their elder brothers (tinamu), a recurrent licensed familiarity.",One does not marry a ñwingi! ... A man will keep away from his younger brother's wives (bengi) and play with those of his elder brothers (tinamu).,tinamu,tinamu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:39:23.463936+00:00
1482,/tsonga_webster_265_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,women,group,men,group,within_group,"Recurring, culturally patterned public humiliation and banter between women and men (gender categories).","Rock stripping is strictly a female preserve. Any man happening by is subjected to the most humiliating insults, invariably of a sexual nature.

Women have developed the technique of humiliation to a fine art. It is the most frequently used weapon in the gender context and is played out in public, whenever there is a suitable audience.",gendered joking / humiliation,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:39:32.126020+00:00
1483,/tsonga_webster_265_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,women,group,returning migrants,group,within_group,"Regular, hostile humorous parody and verbal humiliation by women directed at returning migrants.","Particularly abrasive and hostile humour is reserved for returning migrants, especially if they are prone to parading around the community in new clothes, fancy shoes and mirrored sunglasses. Drink parties, or the communal waterhole, serve as the stage where women of all ages act out elaborate charades—parodying such dandies, playing with particular relish if one such unfortunate is present.",hostile parody / humiliation,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T10:39:32.126020+00:00
1484,/tsonga_webster_266_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,older women,age_set,migrant men,group,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, ritualised joking by older women toward migrant men within the society.","There is a definite sense in which women close ranks in solidarity against migrant men and attempt to restore them to lowered status by what has become a new form of a secular ritualised joking relationship. They also recognise very clearly their own dependence on the men's migrant earnings, which partly explains why it is the older women, many of whom are widows or divorcees ... who are the main perpetrators of this joking relationship, while younger women tend to be willing and amused witnesses.",secular ritualised joking relationship,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:39:38.215087+00:00
1485,/tsonga_webster_268_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,mother's brother,kin_role,sister's son,kin_role,kinship,"Text cites the mother's brother–sister's son tie as a modelled, ritualised joking relationship among the Thonga.","Perhaps this too, has its role model and legitimacy in the now fading mother's brother-sister's son relationship among the Thonga, so celebrated in the anthropological literature.",mother's brother–sister's son relationship,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T10:39:47.468962+00:00
1486,/tsonga_webster_268_sv,SouthernAfrica,tsonga,women,group,men,group,within_group,"Document describes a new, recurrent male/female joking form with licensed personal licence and humiliation linked to migrant labour.","The new form of joking relationship, with overtones of sexual innuendo, personal licence, and humiliation has arisen out of women's resentment of migrant labour. ... The new form, with its almost ritualised antagonism is particularly apposite, for the relationship which spawns it is the pivotal one in male/female interaction today.",new form of joking relationship (male/female),,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Women-based teasing/hostility against men emphasized; categorized as within-group gender-based institutionalized joking.,2026-07-06T10:39:47.468962+00:00
1496,/zulu_hanretta_403_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,umNdlunkulu,group,powerful men,group,within_group,Text describes umNdlunkulu's sanctioned verbal harassment of men as a recurrent licensed liberty.,"UmNdlunkulu could verbally harass and taunt men to an extent usually permitted to most women only during their omula puberty ceremony, and powerful men who were allowed verbally to assault most women were forced to demur in regard to umNdlunkulu.",verbally harass and taunt,omula,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:40:44.059468+00:00
1497,/zulu_krige_27_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,mother's mother,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent teasing/play privileges between grandchild and mother's mother.,"The mother's mother, umakhulu, umamekhulu or simply ukhulu, or ukoko, is the one who pets and spoils you, with whom you may play and whom you may tease.","petting, spoiling, play and tease","umakhulu, umamekhulu, ukhulu, ukoko",symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:40:51.759465+00:00
1498,/zulu_krige_27_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,father's mother,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,"Text states grandchildren may tease the father's mother, though the relationship is less playful than with mother's mother.","The father's mother, called by the same terms as the mother's mother, may also be teased and played with, but the relationship with her is not quite so good as that between the mother's mother and the grandchild.",may be teased and played with (lesser),,mixed,joking_relationship,0.85,Less permissive joking than with mother's mother; reciprocity unclear.,2026-07-06T10:40:51.759465+00:00
1499,/zulu_krige_33,SouthernAfrica,zulu,a man,kin_role,his brother's wives,kin_role,kinship,Described recurrent 'playmates' relationship with licensed liberties between man and his brother's wives.,"""My brother's wives would be my playmates, and if I find that they have cooked something, I would take it without their permission unless it was intended for my brother. I am allowed to give them work to do for me, e.g., the weaving of a mat or a bead decoration for my sticks.""",playmates,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:41:02.499484+00:00
1500,/zulu_krige_33,SouthernAfrica,zulu,a man,kin_role,his wife's sisters,kin_role,kinship,"Text states wife's sisters are 'allowed to joke widely' with the man—recurring, sanctioned joking with in-laws.","""Similarly all the sisters of my wife are allowed to joke widely with me, while there is some strain between me and my brother-in-law""",allowed to joke widely,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:41:02.499484+00:00
1501,/zulu_raum_106_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,a bride,kin_role,"her HSis, her Sis-in-l",kin_role,kinship,Text states bride is forbidden to joke with in-laws except with husband's sisters—an institutionalized kin joking allowance.,"The bride's respectful conduct towards her F-in-l is clearly defined in speech avoidance.  She must not talk with her HF at all is the first generalized statement made.  ... She avoids any talk that may be considered disrespectful.  Such submissiveness is extended to all in-laws: she may not joke with any of them except her HSis, her Sis-in-l.",exception for joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:41:09.392553+00:00
1502,/zulu_reader_156_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,abazala,kin_role,abazala,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly names reciprocal joking (ukulawula) between abazala, opposite-sex relatives taking licensed liberties.","It is between abazala that the reciprocal joking relationship (ukulawula) formerly obtained, wherein relatives of opposite sexes could take conversational liberties with one another to the extent of insult and indecency.",ukulawula,ukulawula,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:41:27.501720+00:00
1503,/zulu_reader_156_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,okанina,kin_role,okанina,kin_role,kinship,Author states remarks about abazala (including joking liberties) apply to okanina (mother's sister's children) as well.,"The okanina, the children of the mamekazi, mother's sister, rank for practical purposes in the same degree of closeness as the abazala, the children of the babekazi or of the malume. All the above remarks about abazala can be applied to okanina, the latter being distinguished by a special term to emphasize that they are children of one's mother's sister, that is, like children of one's own mother.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Document uses 'okанina' (mother's sister's children); joking liberties inferred via statement that remarks about abazala apply to okanina.,2026-07-06T10:41:27.501720+00:00
1504,/zulu_reader_175_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,boys (young men),group,girls (pagan girls / young women),group,within_group,"Recurring, culturally patterned teasing between adolescent males and females described as learned practices.","There is accordingly an aggressive as well as a compliant element in relations between the sexes during adolescence. ... Pagan girls, as they come into the love-game, accordingly learn from their elder sisters the methods of quick repartee and ridiculing young men by words. Correspondingly the latter, by listening to their brothers and older men, learn how to kilela the girls—to entrap them in speech.",,soma,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,"Patterned, recurrent teasing between age-sex categories during courtship; local verb 'soma' links joking and intercourse.",2026-07-06T10:41:36.035402+00:00
1505,/zulu_vilazaki_44_sv,SouthernAfrica,zulu,brothers-in-law,kin_role,sisters-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text describes a culturally recognized recurring joking familiarity between abalamu (brothers- and sisters-in-law).,"Among the abalamu, i.e. brothers-in-law and sisters-in-law, there is an easy familiarity and joking relationship which marks them as potential spouses. At a gathering among the Qadi, a young man was talking to a group of girls and making quite a lot of verbal play on the word lala, i.e. to sleep, which can also mean sexual intercourse; and an interested person immediately remarked: “Obviously you are abalamu since this fellow is so fond of verbal play on the word lala.”",abalamu,abalamu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:41:44.949291+00:00
388,/Akan_Clark_19_nk,WesternAfrica,Akan,one elderly woman,person,her middle-aged daughter,person,kinship,Recurring teasing between a mother and daughter at the market; kin-role patterned joking.,One elderly women trader continually startled me by interjecting comments on the theme of “My husband! You will bring me cloth!” into my peaceful conversations with her middle-aged daughter at their stall.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Individual dyad described repeatedly; treated as kinship joking between mother and daughter.,2026-07-06T08:53:38.384999+00:00
389,/Akan_Clark_19_nk,WesternAfrica,Akan,elderly women traders,group,male toddlers and preschoolers,kin_role,within_group,"Widespread, recurrent pastime of adults proposing marriage/kin relations to young boys — patterned adult↔child teasing.","very similar joking aimed at male toddlers and preschoolers was a popular pastime, proposing marriage and kin relations considered hilarious because of the boy's age and incomprehension.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,"Described as a popular, recurrent joking pastime among traders/older women toward young boys.",2026-07-06T08:53:38.384999+00:00
390,/Akan_Fortes_273_NK,WesternAfrica,Akan,brothers and sisters,kin_role,each other,kin_role,kinship,"Text states siblings may joke and tease without offence, a culturally patterned kinship allowance.","Brothers and sisters may joke with each other, tease each other, and even quarrel, as they are prone to do in childhood, without causing offence.",easy informality of manners and dress,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:53:48.063139+00:00
391,/Akan_Fortes_273_NK,WesternAfrica,Akan,Ashanti,ethnic group,Nzima,ethnic group,cross_group,Author identifies privileged familiarity between Ashanti and neighbouring Akan peoples like the Nzima as nearest joking relationship.,Conventionalized joking is not customary in any Ashanti kinship relationship. The nearest approach to a joking relationship is the privileged familiarity that obtains between Ashanti and some neighbouring Akan peoples like the Nzima.,privileged familiarity,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,Text lists Nzima as example of neighbouring Akan peoples with privileged familiarity; symmetry not explicitly stated.,2026-07-06T08:53:48.063139+00:00
392,/Akan_Fortes__273_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,brother,kin_role,sister,kin_role,kinship,"Text states brothers and sisters routinely joke and tease without offense, a recurrent culturally permitted kinship behavior.","Brothers and sisters may joke with each other, tease each other, and even quarrel, as they are prone to do in childhood, without causing offence.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:53:57.621905+00:00
393,/Akan_Fortes__273_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,Ashanti,ethnic group,Nzima,ethnic group,cross_group,Author identifies a recurrent 'privileged familiarity' between Ashanti and neighbouring Nzima resembling a joking relationship.,Conventionalized joking is not customary in any Ashanti kinship relationship. The nearest approach to a joking relationship is the privileged familiarity that obtains between Ashanti and some neighbouring Akan peoples like the Nzima.,privileged familiarity,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T08:53:57.621905+00:00
394,/Akan_Mead_303_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,brothers,kin_role,sisters,kin_role,kinship,Text states joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters; clearly a recurrent kin-based joking tie.,"Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",joking and chaffing,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:54:10.830284+00:00
395,/Akan_Mead_303_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,a man,kin_role,his wife's sister,kin_role,kinship,Author explicitly permits joking between a man and his wife's sister — a patterned affinal kin joking relation.,"Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",joking and chaffing,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Entities kept as kin-role phrases exactly as in text.,2026-07-06T08:54:10.830284+00:00
396,/Akan_Mead_303_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,a woman,kin_role,her husband's brother,kin_role,kinship,Text affirms permitted joking between a woman and her husband's brother — recurrent affinal joking institution.,"Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",joking and chaffing,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:54:10.830284+00:00
397,/Akan_Mead_303_NK,WesternAfrica,Akan,brothers,kin_role,sisters,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states joking permitted between brothers and sisters; clearly kinship-based institutional pattern.,"Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:54:22.947153+00:00
398,/Akan_Mead_303_NK,WesternAfrica,Akan,man,kin_role,his wife's sister,kin_role,kinship,Explicitly named recurrent permitted joking between a man and his wife's sister; an affinal kinship pattern.,"Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:54:22.947153+00:00
399,/Akan_Mead_303_NK,WesternAfrica,Akan,woman,kin_role,her husband's brother,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly permits joking between a woman and her husband's brother; an affinal kinship institution.,"Joking and chaffing are permitted between brothers and sisters, between a man and his wife's sister, and between a woman and her husband's brother.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:54:22.947153+00:00
400,/Akan_Ratray_371_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,lender,group,borrower,group,within_group,Recurrent joking allusion tied to the bo mmokwa loan relationship between friends (lender vs borrower).,"there is a joking allusion to the custom which runs, Wo de wo tuo bo mmokwa gye abasa a, ete (‘When you lend your gun in hope of getting a foreleg, it will burst’).",joking allusion,"Wo de wo tuo bo mmokwa gye abasa a, ete",unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Text frames the proverb as culturally recognized joke tied to the loan custom between friends; roles encoded as lender/borrower.,2026-07-06T08:54:30.653435+00:00
401,/Akan_Rattray_99_NK,WesternAfrica,Akan,mother-in-law,kin_role,son-in-law,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurring culturally patterned jokes, sayings and practices between sons-in-law and mothers-in-law.","There is a well-known saying which when repeated generally causes a sly giggle, ‘ ase, fie yenko’, which might be rendered by ‘You don't catch us going to mother-in-law's hut’. ... An Ashanti would not ordinarily eat out of the same dish as his mother-in-law.",mother-in-law tags / sayings,"ase, fie yenko",non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Also includes taboo/avoidance behavior and jocular insults; relationship framed as culturally recurring teasing of mother-in-law by children-in-law.,2026-07-06T08:54:37.394578+00:00
402,/Akan_Warren_34_KS,WesternAfrica,Akan,male related consanguineally or affinally,kin_role,female related consanguineally or affinally,kin_role,kinship,Text states opposite‑sex relatives (consanguineal/affinal) may have a joking relationship allowing platonic 'friend' ties.,"The Bono-Twi term adamfo[unavailable] (usually translated into English as “friend”) can be used to describe relationships between individuals of the same sex; if used by a person to describe a relationship with someone of the opposite sex, that person would usually be related either consanguineally or affinally in such a way that sexual intercourse between the two persons would be either taboo or considered highly deviant. In only this latter sense, involving a joking relationship, may there be platonic, “friendly” relationships between the sexes.",joking relationship,adamfo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T08:54:54.089688+00:00
456,/Bambara_119_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Koulibali,patronym,Kéita,patronym,within_group,Text states Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara—institutional joking between family names within society.,"Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara; Bozo and Dogon as well as Bozo and Somono are also senãnku",senãnku,senãnkuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:00:40.969272+00:00
457,/Bambara_119_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Dogon,ethnic group,cross_group,Document repeatedly describes Dogon–Bozo as a named senãnku/allianced pair across peoples with ritualized joking.,"Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara; Bozo and Dogon as well as Bozo and Somono are also senãnku",senãnku / alliance,"măgu (Dogon), dagana (Bozo), senãnkuya",symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:00:40.969272+00:00
458,/Bambara_119_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Somono,ethnic group,cross_group,"Text explicitly lists Bozo and Somono as 'senãnku', indicating institutionalized joking between these peoples.","Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara; Bozo and Dogon as well as Bozo and Somono are also senãnku",senãnku,senãnkuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:00:40.969272+00:00
459,/Bambara_119_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Peul,ethnic group,cross_group,Text mentions Bozo and Peul as an example of alliances involving exogamy and joking relationships between tribes.,"Moreover, within this last group, several types of alliances can be observed: some involve exogamy (Dogon and Bozo: Bozo and Peul, etc…); others exclude the possibility of carrying out purification rites.",senãnku / alliance,senãnkuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:00:40.969272+00:00
460,/Bambara_119_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Koulibali,patronym,Tangara,ethnic group,cross_group,Koulibali and a Tangara sealed a long durable friendship by blood—explicit institutionalized alliance with joking relationship context.,"Koulibali and a Tangara, bound by a long and durable friendship, decided to seal it in a lasting way. They opened their veins and put a little blood from one wound into the other, thus realizing the alliance through this exchange.",blood alliance,,symmetric,alliance,0.9,Blood-alliance described as sealing long-term joking/alliance tie; treated as cross-group.,2026-07-06T09:00:40.969272+00:00
461,/Bambara_65_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,children of father's sister (kalame),kin_role,children of mother's brother (kalame),kin_role,kinship,"Text states kalame categories routinely 'joke and insult each other' on meeting, a recurring kinship-based joking tie.","The children of the ntene-muso are called kalame, and they call the66 children of their mother's brother kalame. The kalame-wjoke and insult each other when they meet.",,kalame,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:00:47.574446+00:00
462,/Bambara_79_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Dogon,ethnic group,cross_group,"Text explicitly states a recurring joking relationship between Bozo and Dogon, an intertribal tie.","The myths of both groups mention the apparition of the Bozo on earth as coinciding with the beginning of 51 Faro's actions which, in turn, coincides with the development of techniques among the Bambara, and with the “third word” of the Dogon (1). In addition, the “joking relationships” which they maintain with the Dogon and which go back to the origin of a whole system of tribal and intertribal alliances clearly indicate the importance of the symbolic role conferred upon them (2).",joking relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:00:55.044558+00:00
463,/Bambara_Dieterlen_153_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,numu (blacksmiths),caste,other Blacks,group,within_group,Text states numu practice senankuya (joking relationships) with other Blacks within the society.,"They practice endogamy, maintain joking relationships, senankuya, with other Blacks, and are in charge of certain cults dedicated to ancestors and to Faro.",senankuya,senankuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:01:03.333640+00:00
464,/Bambara_Dieterlen_157_NK,WesternAfrica,Bambara,blacksmiths,caste,other Blacks,group,within_group,Text states blacksmiths have senankuya joking ties with other members of the society.,"Every blacksmith is regarded as the holder of an excellent tere. He is well-intentioned, affable, and never hurts anyone. It is easy to make friends with him, to joke with him, even if one knows him but a short time. The senankuya relationships which he has with other Blacks also contribute to the cordiality of these ties.",senankuya relationships,senankuya,unclear,joking_relationship,0.8,Document frames blacksmiths as a social category with culturally recognized joking ties (senankuya) to others in the community.,2026-07-06T09:01:11.629165+00:00
465,/Bambara_Dieterlin_108_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Kouloubali,clan,Diara,clan,kinship,Text links Kouloubali and Diara as family allies with patterned joking/taboo-based rites (senãnku); clearly kinship-family alliance.,"On the other hand, relationships commonly known as “joking relationships” are established between families respecting different taboos and bearing different names. ... One of the men, who belonged to the Diara family, asked them their family names. They answered that they had none. “What does that matter?” he replied. “You will live on a great mountain and take the name of Kouloubali.” They then asked the name of their ally (senãnku).",ally (senãnku) / joking relationship,senãnku,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:01:18.703937+00:00
466,/Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Koulibali,patronym,Kéita,patronym,within_group,Text names Koulibali as a Kéita's senãnku (joking relationship) within Bambara family names.,"Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara;",senãnku,dyo benatye / senãnkuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:01:36.949828+00:00
467,/Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Dogon,ethnic group,cross_group,Text repeatedly describes Dogon–Bozo as a named institutional joking/alliance across peoples (senãnkuya type).,"A Dogon and a Bozo are twins, each carrying a particle of the other in his body and soul.",senãnkuya,senãnkuya / măgu (Dogon) / dagana (Bozo),symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:01:36.949828+00:00
468,/Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Somono,ethnic group,cross_group,Text lists Bozo and Somono as senãnku (joking/allied peoples).,"Thus, a Koulibali is a Kéita's senãnku among the Bambara; Bozo and Dogon as well as Bozo and Somono are also senãnku",senãnku,dyo benatye / senãnkuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:01:36.949828+00:00
469,/Bambara_Dieterlin_119_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Peul,ethnic group,cross_group,Text notes Dogon and Bozo; Bozo and Peul as types of alliances involving exogamy and joking ties.,"Moreover, within this last group, several types of alliances can be observed: some involve exogamy (Dogon and Bozo: Bozo and Peul, etc…)",senãnkuya / alliance,senãnkuya,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Text groups Bozo–Peul as allied/exogamous; implies joking relationship type among such alliances.,2026-07-06T09:01:36.949828+00:00
470,/Bambara_Germaine_79_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,Bozo,ethnic group,Dogon,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly states recurrent 'joking relationships' between Bozo and Dogon as intertribal alliance practice.,"The myths of both groups mention the apparition of the Bozo on earth as coinciding with the beginning of 51 Faro's actions which, in turn, coincides with the development of techniques among the Bambara, and with the “third word” of the Dogon (1). In addition, the “joking relationships” which they maintain with the Dogon and which go back to the origin of a whole system of tribal and intertribal alliances clearly indicate the importance of the symbolic role conferred upon them (2).",joking relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:01:43.686100+00:00
471,/Bambara_Pacques_65_KS,WesternAfrica,Bambara,children of father's sister,kin_role,children of mother's brother,kin_role,kinship,Text states kalame (these two kin categories) 'joke and insult each other'—a recurrent kinship-patterned teasing.,"The children of the ntene-muso are called kalame, and they call the children of their mother's brother kalame. The kalame-wjoke and insult each other when they meet.",,kalame,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:01:53.406403+00:00
506,/Dogon_Calame_280_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,kú¸nõ,kin_role,twins,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly names a recurrent joking relationship between kú¸nõ and twins.,Relations between the kú¸nõ and twins are maintained by a sort of “joking relationship” (mà[unavailable]u). They traditionally exchange jokes on this theme.,“joking relationship”,mà[unavailable]u,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:07:06.428903+00:00
507,/Dogon_Calame_305_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon,ethnic group,Bozo,ethnic group,cross_group,"Text explicitly describes a recurring 'joking relationship' between Dogon and Bozo (cross-ethnic, ritualized insults).","These two people are joined in a “joking relationship” or “cathartic association,” má[unavailable]a which means to “knead into a ball.” According to Griaule this relationship gives rise to several forms of exchange of “material, moral and religious services rendered: hospitality, the administering of food and of purification, and ritual activities” (1948a: ). The relationship also gives rise to various taboos chiefly concerning sexual relationships, spilled blood, and release of tension through taunts and insults.",joking relationship; cathartic association,má[unavailable]a; má[unavailable]u,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,The passage notes reciprocal use of Bozo tongue insults by Dogon and Bozo; institution includes ritual exchanges and sanctioned taunts.,2026-07-06T09:07:15.362689+00:00
508,/Dogon_Calame_373_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon men,group,his maternal uncle's wife,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly lists maternal uncle's wife as a recurrent mà[...]u joking partner (kin-based).,"Dogon tell sexual jokes to women with whom they have joking relationships (mà[unavailable]u), which includes his maternal uncle's wife, and the wives of friends and brothers, but they do not exchange them with their own wives.",mà[unavailable]u,mà[unavailable]u,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:07:35.049446+00:00
509,/Dogon_Calame_373_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon men,group,the wives of friends,group,within_group,"Text names 'wives of friends' as recurring mà[...]u joking partners, a patterned intra-societal relation.","Dogon tell sexual jokes to women with whom they have joking relationships (mà[unavailable]u), which includes his maternal uncle's wife, and the wives of friends and brothers, but they do not exchange them with their own wives.",mà[unavailable]u,mà[unavailable]u,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:07:35.049446+00:00
510,/Dogon_Calame_373_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon men,group,the wives of brothers,kin_role,kinship,Wives of brothers are listed as recurring mà[...]u joking partners—an affinal kin-based joking institution.,"Dogon tell sexual jokes to women with whom they have joking relationships (mà[unavailable]u), which includes his maternal uncle's wife, and the wives of friends and brothers, but they do not exchange them with their own wives.",mà[unavailable]u,mà[unavailable]u,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:07:35.049446+00:00
511,/Dogon_Calame_375_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,couples,kin_role,couples,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly reports frequent, culturally patterned joking between husband–wife couples.","Although frequent joking between couples would give the impression that their relationships are always harmonious, in reality there remains a latent tension. Dogon men live in constant fear that their wives will leave them, and Dogon women are aware that on leaving they will be left entirely to their own resources.",joking between couples,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:07:41.987728+00:00
512,/Dogon_Calame_402_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,women,group,two young men,age_set,within_group,Text states recurrent joking relationships between women and specific young men in musical contexts.,"Two women beat the dance rhythms, with more or less competence; usually they merely begin the rhythms and then get two young men with whom they have joking relationships to carry it on by offering them beer.",joking relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Women solicit specific young men for ritualized teasing/banter during musical performances; treated as a patterned cultural practice.,2026-07-06T09:07:49.878441+00:00
513,/Dogon_Calame_450_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon,ethnic group,Bozo,ethnic group,cross_group,Text states Dogon joking institution models kinship ties with the Bozo (institutionalized interethnic alliance).,"We should recall that this institution among the Dogon is the model for their kinship ties with the Bozo and that a system of relationship of the same type can be found internally between regions, villages, and families (Griaule 1948b: ).",kinship ties modeled on joking relationship,mà[unavailable]u mà[unavailable]a,unclear,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:08:13.447819+00:00
514,/Dogon_Calame_450_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Upper Ogol,village,Lower Ogol,village,cross_group,Describes recurring inter-village 'village taunts' in contest leading to taunts and near-violence; institutionalized inter-village teasing.,"In the competition the men from Upper Ogol were proved inferior to the contestants from Lower Ogol, and the winners assailed the losers with taunts. A veritable pitched battle ensued with an exchange of blows using staffs and rocks, and only the intervention of the masks brought it successfully to an end.",village taunts / inter-village teasing,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:08:13.447819+00:00
515,/Dogon_Calame_450_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,people of different dialect groups,group,each other,group,cross_group,"Text explicitly notes 'village taunts' exchanged by different dialect groups when they meet as a patterned, merry dialogic practice.",It is the same with “village taunts” exchanged by people of different dialect groups when they meet. Straight away they stamp their dialogue with a merry spirit.,village taunts,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.75,Entity_b_raw uses literal phrasing from text to indicate reciprocal joking between dialect groups.,2026-07-06T09:08:13.447819+00:00
516,/Dogon_Griaule_107_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon,ethnic group,Bozo,ethnic group,cross_group,Text describes an institutionalized 'mangu' joking/cathartic alliance between Dogon and Bozo.,"The original situation of the ideal couple—twins enclosed in the same egg—is also illustrated in a religious and economic relationship maintained between the Dogon and the Bozo which has existed, it is said, from the earliest times. This institution, which flourishes over a wide area, is known as a ‘joking relationship’, though the term ‘cathartic alliance’ has been suggested for it.",mangu; joking relationship,mangu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:08:24.790960+00:00
517,/Dogon_Griaule_215_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Bozo,ethnic group,Dogon,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly states a recurrent 'joking relationship' between Bozo and Dogon; cross-community ethnic groups.,"Fish and men are twins, and the Bozo is himself of water, being the first fisherman of the Niger and the master of the river. The Bozo maintain what is called a ‘joking relationship’ with the Dogon who traditionally refer to them as ‘fish that walk on land’.",‘joking relationship’,fish that walk on land,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:08:31.543824+00:00
518,/Dogon_Griaule_222_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Sanga,village,Ireli,village,cross_group,Sanga habitually mock/taunt Ireli in sung jibes; inter-community patterned teasing.,"The people of Sanga mock the people of Ireli by saying:

yogo ireli s˛o yakoh[unknown]
ad[unknown]n[unknown] ¯na ku diana.
Tomorrow there will be tales at Ireli
Because (someone named) Adene has cooked an Ox's head.
Sanga.",mockery,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:08:43.661576+00:00
519,/Dogon_Griaule_222_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Sanga,village,Yendoumman,village,cross_group,Sanga sing songs celebrating superiority over Yendoumman; recurring inter-community mockery.,"According to the people of Sanga, the villages of Yendoumman were built
upon ground that is without rocks, which to their eyes is an inconvenience,
perhaps because of the termites which cannot live in a rocky place but swarm
in the loose earth.

In order to celebrate their superiority, the people of Sanga sing:

yendummã de sãga ir[unknown]
sãgana taba yak˛o.
Sanga is worth more than Yendoumman
At Sanga, there are rocks.",mockery / superiority song,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:08:43.661576+00:00
520,/Dogon_Griaule_26_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon,ethnic group,Bozo,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly describes a recurrent 'joking kinship' (cathartic relationship) with prohibitions and mutual aid.,"The Dogon, who are farmers, maintain special relations with the Bozo, fishermen of the Niger, which the Dogon call sorogon; they are united through a cathartic relationship or “joking kinship,” which implies the observance of a certain number of prohibitions and also mutual aid. both rigorously defined.",joking kinship / cathartic relationship,sorogon,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:08:52.131545+00:00
521,/Dogon_Griaule_289_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,very young children,age_set,the Fox (manu),group,within_group,"Text explicitly names a recurrent 'joking relationship' between young children and the Fox, a cultural play partner.","This idea of the expansion of the human domain, progressively impinging upon that of the Fox (who show the way), is illustrated or inaugurated (one may say) in children's games. The role of the very young children, who have a “joking relationship” with the Fox ( manu), is to play with his “earth,” both to look for him and to chase him away.",joking relationship,manu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:09:00.359390+00:00
522,/Dogon_Parin_506_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,Dogon,ethnic group,the White,ethnic group,cross_group,Text describes ritual/mock joking directed at a White as an institutionalized restorative mocking relationship.,"For example, if an attitude of the analyst led to the fact that the analysand felt dependent on him, as on a patron, he might address a ritual joke to the White. This immediately restored his self-awareness and made possible another form of transference.",mocking-relationship,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Document treats ritualized 'mocking-relationship' as a cultural institution; only explicit cross-group example involves addressing a White (analyst).,2026-07-06T09:09:08.886199+00:00
523,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,All Dogon,ethnic group,the Bozo,ethnic group,cross_group,Text states all Dogon have mock-kinship (mangu) with the Bozo; cross-community institutional joking.,"All Dogon have a mock-kinship with the Bozo, members of a tribe of fishermen who live somewhat beyond, on the Niger.",mock-kinship,mangu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:09:32.658879+00:00
524,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,boys,age_set,girls,age_set,within_group,"Describes recurring mock-relationship between boys and girls during shared work, a patterned licensed joking.","If boys and girls have a mock-relationship with one another while they are sharing some work such as harvesting po, the innuendos are even more obscene.",mock-relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:09:32.658879+00:00
525,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,paternal family,lineage,maternal family,lineage,within_group,Text explains the two mocking each other are regarded as paternal vs maternal family; recurring institutional role.,"It explains why, according to legend, the two mocking each other are usually regarded as the paternal family and the maternal family.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:09:32.658879+00:00
526,/Dogon_Parin_59_NK,WesternAfrica,Dogon,wife of the maternal uncle,kin_role,wife of the grandfather (nani),kin_role,kinship,Both kin roles are listed as kinfolk who must be treated by mock-relationship (licensed joking) with ego.,"Kinfolk who must be treated in this way are the ones who, in other circumstances, might be thought of as a married couple, such as the brother's wives or those with whom a sexual approach would be the same as incest, such as the wife of the maternal uncle and the wife of the grandfather ( nani).",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,Text indicates these kin roles are treated with mock-relationship toward ego; pairwise joking between these two roles is inferred as both are listed as treated thus.,2026-07-06T09:09:32.658879+00:00
527,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,woman,kin_role,husband's grandfather,kin_role,kinship,Explicit culturally patterned 'playmate' address (asúchi) between a woman and her husband's grandfather.,"In the present instance, information concerning these configurations developed from an analysis of the significance of the terms employed by a person toward his spouse's grandparents. In address, a woman calls her husband's grandfather asúchi, “my husband,” and this usage reflects the existence of a playmate relationship",playmate relationship,asúchi,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
528,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,wives,kin_role,husband's brothers,kin_role,kinship,Text states wives may joke with all their husband's brothers as part of the playmate configuration.,"Thus, a woman may joke with all her husband's brothers, whether these brothers have the same mother or not, regardless of age, the reason for this being that when the husband dies, some of his wives become the wives of his brothers.",playmate relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
529,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,man,kin_role,mother-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Author notes men may joke with their mother-in-law and that mothers-in-law and sons-in-law often joke.,"A man may joke with his mother-in-law, in spite of the fact that the feeling between mother-in-law and son-in-law is ordinarily not too cordial. It was stated, indeed, that mothers-in-law and sons-in-law joke all the more for this reason.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
530,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,mother-in-law,kin_role,daughter-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states a mother-in-law may joke with her daughter-in-law as part of supervised intimacy.,"A mother-in-law may joke with her daughter-in-law, because the mothers of both the bride and the groom supervise the first sexual contacts between a man and his wife.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
531,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,brothers-in-law,kin_role,each other,group,kinship,Brothers-in-law are described as often very close friends who joke with one another.,"Brothers-in-law are often very close friends, and sisters-in-law usually joke with each other, but this is only an indication of the freedom bred of more or less intimate everyday contact.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
532,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,sisters-in-law,kin_role,each other,group,kinship,Sisters-in-law are stated to usually joke with each other as a patterned social freedom.,"Brothers-in-law are often very close friends, and sisters-in-law usually joke with each other, but this is only an indication of the freedom bred of more or less intimate everyday contact.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
533,/Fon_Herskovits_151_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,man,kin_role,brothers of his grandfather,kin_role,kinship,The text permits joking between a man and his grandfather's brothers (patterned kin joking).,"A man may not joke with his father's brothers, but he may do so with the brothers of his grandfather.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:18.976219+00:00
534,/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,operator,person,boys of a village,kin_role,within_group,Operator performs ritual horse-play and teasing toward initiated boys during circumcision ceremony.,"The operator, according to this account, goes through some horse-play with the boys before he actually operates. He may, for example, take hold of the foreskin and tug at it, all the while watching his charge to see if the boy is nervous; and then tug harder still, or he will place the knife on the thigh of his subject, and look into his face to see if he shows fear.",horse-play / teasing by operator,,non_symmetric,teasing,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:34.220827+00:00
535,/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,friends and relatives,kin_role,boys of a village,kin_role,within_group,Friends and relatives publicly tease boys during the circumcision ceremony as a patterned practice.,"The ceremony is public, and friends and relatives stand about teasing the boys and joking with them, in order to dissipate any squeamishness, and more than that, to make them ashamed of showing fear.",teasing / joking,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:10:34.220827+00:00
536,/Fon_Herskovits_296_NK,WesternAfrica,Fon,men (holding the boys' shoulders),group,boys of a village,kin_role,within_group,Men hold boys to steady them during operation and are described as part of the ritual setting involving teasing.,"Ordinarily, two men are beside each boy, holding his shoulders to steady him. The wound is washed on the third day, and again the organ is pinched so that undue scarification will not occur, and this is repeated daily until the wound is healed.",holders / steadyers (ritual interaction),,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,"Men are involved in the ritual context; explicit teasing mainly attributed to bystanders and operator, but holders are part of patterned interaction.",2026-07-06T09:10:34.220827+00:00
537,/Fulani_Dupire_349_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,Bororo,ethnic group,dillalai-mahautaa,caste,cross_group,Text describes recurring dendiraagal joking alliance between Bororo and dillalai-mahautaa as institutionalized.,"Among the Bororo, servile relations have become very much lessened in content and have actually been assimilated into a sort of joking relationship (dendiraagal).",dendiraagal,dendiraagal,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:49.103082+00:00
538,/Fulani_Dupire_349_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,brother's children,kin_role,sister's children,kin_role,kinship,"Described as an internal, asymmetrical dendiraagal joking relation between specified kin roles (masters vs ""slaves"").","Even within Bororo society this relationship is asymmetrical, the brother's children playing the role of “masters” in relation to the sister's children, called the “slaves.”",dendiraagal,dendiraagal,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:10:49.103082+00:00
539,/Fulani_Dupire_349_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,sedentary Fulani,ethnic group,sarakolle,group,cross_group,Author notes a parallel institutional protected/trader joking relation in Fouta-Jalon between sedentary Fulani and 'sarakolle'.,In Fouta-Jalon this relationship existed in a parallel form between the sedentary Fulani and the traders whom they protected and whom they called “their sarakolle.”,sarakolle,sarakolle,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,"Parallel, regional example rather than core Tahoua case.",2026-07-06T09:10:49.103082+00:00
540,/Fulani_Dupire_71_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,daughter-in-law,kin_role,husband's younger brothers,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly describes a recurring joking relationship between these kin roles in camp context.,"She has, on the other hand, a joking relationship with her husband's younger brothers, and they call each other “old fellow” and “my wife”.",joking relationship,“old fellow” and “my wife”,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:10:56.725691+00:00
541,/Fulani_Dupire_72_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,a married woman,kin_role,her younger brothers-in-law,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states a recurrent joking relationship between married women and younger brothers-in-law.,"Thus, deferent towards her husband's parents and her elder brothers, a woman is on equal terms often of an affectionate and friendly nature with her sisters-in-law, and has a joking relationship with her younger brothers-in-law.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:11:04.431954+00:00
542,/Fulani_Kaartinen_121_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,Dadi,person,Mariyama,person,kinship,Text explicitly calls their rapport a joking relationship likened to cross-cousins (kinship-based).,"Dadi, one of my friends from the city who works as a migrant laborer, is back to stay at his mother's house for a few weeks. He is a little older than I am, and our relationship has developed into a kind of a joking relationship, almost as if we were cross-cousins (denDiraaBe).",joking relationship (cross-cousin),denDiraaBe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:11:12.766526+00:00
543,/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,brothers,lineage,sister's children,lineage,within_group,Text describes dendiraagal/cross-cousin alliance producing joking relations between these lineage categories.,"Dendiraagal, “cross-cousinship”, between two distinct “lineages” can imply either alliance and a joking relationship, or a competitive opposition between the groups. Two primary lineages that have intermarried in the past and are thus related through cognatic ties are “brothers” and “sister's children”, or “men” and “women” towards each other (Dupire 1962, – ).",dendiraagal / cross-cousinship,Dendiraagal,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:11:37.620051+00:00
544,/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,Wodaabe,ethnic group,Beriberi,ethnic group,cross_group,Ancient marital alliances produced dendiraagal (joking/alliance) between Wodaabe and Beriberi neighbors.,"Ancient marriages8 between Wodaabe headmen and their Beriberi and Kanouri neighbors, as well as between Wodaabe and the slaves they acquired during the period of jihad, have remained as a basis of dendiraagal between Woodaabe and their ancient allies and serfs (ibid.: 27, 29, 37, ; 1970, ).",dendiraagal,Dendiraagal,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Text links these alliances with hierarchical relations; subordination is implied.,2026-07-06T09:11:37.620051+00:00
545,/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,Wodaabe,ethnic group,Kanouri,ethnic group,cross_group,Marital ties with Kanouri are cited as basis for dendiraagal (joking/alliance) between groups.,"Ancient marriages8 between Wodaabe headmen and their Beriberi and Kanouri neighbors, as well as between Wodaabe and the slaves they acquired during the period of jihad, have remained as a basis of dendiraagal between Woodaabe and their ancient allies and serfs (ibid.: 27, 29, 37, ; 1970, ).",dendiraagal,Dendiraagal,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Hierarchy between groups is mentioned alongside these alliances.,2026-07-06T09:11:37.620051+00:00
546,/Fulani_Kaartinen_7_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,Wodaabe,ethnic group,the slaves they acquired during the period of jihad,group,cross_group,Text states marriages between Wodaabe and their acquired slaves form dendiraagal tying Wodaabe and serfs into joking/allied relations.,"Ancient marriages8 between Wodaabe headmen and their Beriberi and Kanouri neighbors, as well as between Wodaabe and the slaves they acquired during the period of jihad, have remained as a basis of dendiraagal between Woodaabe and their ancient allies and serfs (ibid.: 27, 29, 37, ; 1970, ).",dendiraagal,Dendiraagal,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.6,Serf relationship implies hierarchical subordination; text frames as basis for dendiraagal.,2026-07-06T09:11:37.620051+00:00
547,/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,WoDaaBe cross-cousins,kin_role,WoDaaBe cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states cross-cousins stand in culturally defined joking-relationships within WoDaaBe.,"Young men, for example, who stand in culturally defined joking-relationships (either in the same age group or cross-cousins) often refer to one another as Hausa or Tuaregs, intended then as a friendly insult.",joking-relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:11:57.735594+00:00
548,/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,WoDaaBe young men in the same age group,age_set,WoDaaBe young men in the same age group,age_set,within_group,Author notes same age-group young men have culturally defined joking-relationships.,"Young men, for example, who stand in culturally defined joking-relationships (either in the same age group or cross-cousins) often refer to one another as Hausa or Tuaregs, intended then as a friendly insult.",joking-relationships,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:11:57.735594+00:00
549,/Fulani_Kristin_159_NK,WesternAfrica,Fulani,WoDaaBe,ethnic group,Beriberi,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly describes interrelations between WoDaaBe and Beriberi as a joking-relationship.,"Interrelations with the Beriberi in Niamey are characterized by a joking-relationship, which is similar to that between WoDaaBe cross-cousins (Dupire 1962a:27).",joking-relationship,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:11:57.735594+00:00
