612,/Hausa_Baba_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,bride,kin_role,the bridegroom’s grandmothers,kin_role,kinship,"Text labels the bridegroom's grandmothers explicitly as the bride's ""new joking relations"" at marriage.","But when they arrive at her hut door they find her new joking relations, the bridegroom’s grandmothers, sitting there and saying that they can’t come in with the bride! When they are given a little money they get up and let her in.",new joking relations,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:19:23.902243+00:00
613,/Hausa_Baba_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,the girls,group,the bridegroom’s friends,group,within_group,Recurring ritualized teasing: girls stop the groom's friends and extract money three times during the procession.,"The girls hold up the bridegroom’s friend, they won’t let him start off until he has given them some money—three times he has to give them money before they will let him go, with the bride on her horse.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,"Patterned, ceremonial teasing between specified participants in marriage procession.",2026-07-06T09:19:23.902243+00:00
614,/Hausa_Baba_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,bride,kin_role,the bride's girl-friends,group,within_group,"Bride's girl-friends perform a recurring ritual of blocking her entrance and singing, a licensed teasing role.","Then it is the turn of the bride’s girl-friends to prevent her from entering her hut, they sing and the chief kawa hangs onto the bride.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,"Ceremonial, patterned behavior tied to marriage rite.",2026-07-06T09:19:23.902243+00:00
615,/Hausa_Baba_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,bride,kin_role,bridegroom’s younger sister,kin_role,kinship,Text names the groom’s younger sister as “the bride’s new joking relation” at marriage ceremonies.,"Then the groom’s younger sister, the bride’s new joking relation, comes quickly with a vessel of water with a threepanny piece in it, she puts it down at the Life History Materials door of the hut and runs away.",bride’s new joking relation,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:19:37.349653+00:00
616,/Hausa_Baba_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,bride’s kawa,group,bridegroom’s younger sisters,kin_role,kinship,Bride’s kawa and girls engage in patterned teasing and chasing with the groom’s younger sisters during the wedding.,"The bride’s friends and the groom’s younger sisters are playing and teasing, the friends won’t let the bridegroom’s younger sisters enter the bride’s hut, and when food is being eaten, if the younger sisters come near, the bride’s friends will smear them with porridge and stew and oil and they will have to run away and wash.",playing and teasing,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:19:37.349653+00:00
617,/Hausa_Baba_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,kawa,group,mawankiya,kin_role,kinship,Kawa steals the bride’s slippers and the mawankiya must pay to redeem them as part of wedding ritual teasing.,"The chief kawa steals the bride’s slippers and hides them, and her mawankiya has to pay her to bring them back.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.7,mawankiya interpreted as a kin-role; kawa treated as ritual/party role involved in joking.,2026-07-06T09:19:37.349653+00:00
618,/Hausa_Baba_99_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,the bride,kin_role,the bridegroom's younger brother,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly calls the groom's younger brother 'her joking relation' during bride teasing.,"Before the marriage had ‘taken’ the bridegroom’s younger brother would come and tease the bride, he would look at her spinning and say ‘It’s no good!’ he would break the thread and she would get up and hit him and chase him away. He is her joking relation.",joking relation,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Institutionalized teasing tied to affinal role (groom's younger brother); source describes ritualized permissive behavior during marriage transition.,2026-07-06T09:19:43.398993+00:00
