636,/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,"one clan (d an wâ, “son of the older brother”)",clan,"partner clan (d án k ánwa, “son of the younger sister”)",clan,within_group,Text describes recurring joking partnership between paired clans with traditional intermarriage.,"This usage is extended to pairs of clans between whom there is traditional intermarriage. All clans have at least one partner in such a relationship, occasionally — several, and a pair of clans thus connected are said to be ’abó[unknown]kán wa[unknown]sá[unknown] to each other. Of the two clans, one is said to be d an wâ[unknown], “son of the older brother,” and the other one d án k ánwa[unknown], “son of the younger sister”; and any member of the latter clan, as the inferior, is privileged to take from any member of the former.",’abó[unknown]kán wa[unknown]sá[unknown] (paired clans),’abó[unknown]kán wa[unknown]sá[unknown],mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Both clans claim superiority though ritual/privilege assigns inferior/superior roles.,2026-07-06T09:21:26.607951+00:00
637,/Hausa_Greenberg_203_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,K w áryá[unknown]wá[unknown],clan,Zá[unknown]fá[unknown]wá[unknown],clan,within_group,Specific clan pair example where Kwáryáwá consider Záfáwá their superior cross-cousins.,"One clan at least, the K w áryá[unknown]wá[unknown], state that they are descendants of a Baribari (Kanuri) slave, the great-great-grandfather of the present mature generation, who married a daughter of a member of the Zá[unknown]fá[unknown]wá[unknown] clan whom the K w áryá[unknown]wá[unknown] consider their superior cross-cousins.",,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Explicit superiority claim makes relation asymmetrical.,2026-07-06T09:21:26.607951+00:00
