681,/Hausa_Smith_45_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Cross-cousins repeatedly engage in playful superiority/inferiority (slave/master) as institutionalized joking.,cross-cousins often make play with the allusion to [unavailable] and slave mentioned above - “the brother’s son is the slave of the sister’s son”.,playful superiority/inferiority,auren dan mace da dan kane,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Text frames brother's son vs sister's son with master/slave allusion; treated as asymmetric superiority in the joking.,2026-07-06T09:24:37.903281+00:00
682,/Hausa_Smith_45_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,wife's kin,kin_role,husband's kin,kin_role,kinship,Joking relations centred about married people relieve tensions between the spouses' kin—institutional in-laws teasing.,The joking relations centred about a married people serve to relieve the tensions which are expressed in the [unavailable] (suruki) relationships holding between most of the parties concerned.,,suruki,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Refers to joking relations among kin of married people (in-laws); exact behavioral symmetry not specified.,2026-07-06T09:24:37.903281+00:00
683,/Hausa_Smith_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states cross-cousins are culturally recognized joking relations with privileged familiarity.,"In both the Fulani and Hausa kinship systems, cross-cousins are joking relations whose relationship involves privileged familiarity.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:24:47.029956+00:00
684,/Hausa_Smith_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Hausa,the chief,person,cross-cousins,kin_role,within_group,"Chiefs appoint and rely on cross-cousins, using their joking/privileged familiarity in political roles.","Accordingly, Fulani and Hausa rulers like to appoint cross-cousins to their staffs, thus converting these warm and solidary kinship relations to political use. ... the Fulani or Hausa chief relies heavily on his cross-cousins for confidential information on such affairs within and beyond the palace and looks to them for frank advice on appropriate courses of action.",,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Extracts institutionalized privileged familiarity as used politically between office (chief) and kin-role (cross-cousins).,2026-07-06T09:24:47.029956+00:00
685,/Igbo_Bastian_202_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,elder women,group,younger people,age_set,within_group,Text states elder women 'can joke—and often have joking relations with younger people' as recurring patterned teasing.,"Elder women can joke—and often have joking relations with younger people—about sexual relations, but they are expected to lose interest in pursuing such matters after they are beyond child bearing age.",joking relations,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:24:54.288997+00:00
686,/Igbo_Henderson_297_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,the Onitsha,regional group,Nri,regional group,cross_group,Text explicitly describes a recurrent joking relationship between Onitsha and Nri kings across communities.,"These patterns suggest a structured ambiguity in the relations between the Onitsha and Nri kings. By early exploratory times a kind of joking relationship had developed between the two, in which the king of Onitsha permitted the king of Nri to sacrifice at the major shrines to land and river in recognition of his spiritual superiority, but seized the latter's followers for ransom as a sign of his superior strength.",a kind of joking relationship,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:25:00.102037+00:00
687,/Igbo_Okere_78_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,child/offspring (of a woman born in Umuonyeike),kin_role,mother's patrilineage,lineage,kinship,Text explicitly states a recurrent joking relationship tied to maternal patrilineage and offspring moving between lineages.,"A proverb encapsulates this procedure: Emeruo Umunna, ejhee umunne (If one harms one's father's people, one goes to one's mother's people). There is a jokingrelationship with the mother's patrilineage.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Entity A formulated as child/offspring kin role because the passage refers to sons moving to their mother's patrilineage and then joking ties.,2026-07-06T09:25:13.014161+00:00
688,/Igbo_Ottenberg_267_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,cross-cousins,kin_role,cross-cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text links a recurring joking relationship specifically tied to cross-cousin kinship and its effects on marriage.,"Mitchell writes of the Yao that they consider marriage between cross-cousins an unstable type of relationship, attributing this to the continuance of a jokingrelationship that existed before.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,"Statement ties institutionalized joking to the cross-cousin kinship relation (general, with Yao example).",2026-07-06T09:25:21.874564+00:00
689,/Igbo_Ottenberg_273_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,men of the village,village,men of the compound that the boy comes from,clan,within_group,Patterned teasing between village men and specific compound men about initiand failure.,"If an initiand dies, faints, or gets sick there, men of the village joke with men of the compound that the boy comes from, saying, “Your compound is weak. What have you been doing? Your boy is left behind.”",joke,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Compound treated as a social unit within the village; jokes are directed at the compound by village men.,2026-07-06T09:25:29.105612+00:00
690,/Igbo_Uchendu_67_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,the sister's son,kin_role,the mother's brother's lineage,lineage,kinship,Text describes a culturally sanctioned joking/poaching privilege of nephew within mother's-brother lineage.,"While he stays with his mother's brother, he is treated with much affection and allowed a wide latitude in his behavior. He jokes at the expense of everybody, his mother's father, his mother's brother and their wives. This joking relationship is extended to all the members of the mother's brother's lineage.",joking relationship,,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:25:43.570502+00:00
691,/Igbo_Uchendu_86_NK,WesternAfrica,Igbo,ΘmΘɔkpΘ,kin_role,ndom alΘ alΘ,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly identifies a culturally patterned joking relationship between these two women's categories.,The relation between these two classes of women is one of “potential” conflict covertly expressed in the joking relationship between them and overtly manifested in the institutionalized authority of the ΘmΘɔkpΘ over the ndom alΘ alΘ during the mourning rites marking the latter's widowhood.,joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:25:50.781923+00:00
692,/Kanuri_Cohen_173_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,married man,kin_role,zairo (unmarried/circumcised person),kin_role,within_group,Cultural practice of jokingly calling certain married/divorced men 'zairo' — a recurrent licensed teasing label.,"When a young man is divorced and has no other wives, or even if he is married but is known to be especially attentive to other women he is jokingly referred to as zairo (any person who has not been married,  but who has been circumcised, more often referring to the sexually active).",jokingly referred to as zairo,zairo,non_symmetric,insulting_license,0.9,Teasing targets married or divorced men behaving like bachelors; recurrent cultural label rather than single anecdote.,2026-07-06T09:26:00.438635+00:00
693,/Kanuri_Cohen_42_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,junior,kin_role,senior,kin_role,kinship,Text describes formalized permissive joking where junior becomes equal to senior within kin-based respect scale.,"In such relationships the customary restrictions and demands upon behavior based on seniority, generation, sex, or any other status differences, are relaxed. They are instead replaced by formalized patterns of permissiveness in which the junior becomes the equal of the senior, and therefore a putative and formalized kind of reciprocity can be established.",joking relations,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,,2026-07-06T09:26:11.808246+00:00
694,/Kanuri_Cohen_42_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,relatives,kin_role,relatives,kin_role,kinship,"Author states numkam denotes related kin relationships not governed by respect scale, allowing joking and egalitarian ties among kin.","Later, however, I realized that such people are friends because they are related. Relatedness ( numkam ) can consequently be used in a number of ways. ... it can be used to refer to relationships within the kinship network that are not necessarily governed by the respect scale.",,numkam,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Covers kin relationships marked by numkam permitting joking; text treats these as a class rather than specific pairs.,2026-07-06T09:26:11.808246+00:00
695,/Kanuri_Cohen_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,young man or boy,kin_role,his junior female cross-cousin,kin_role,kinship,Text describes culturally patterned joking between opposite-sex junior cross-cousins.,"A young man or boy jokes with one of his junior female cross-cousins calling her wife, concubine, lover, etc., and she returns the joke calling him slave, husband, lover.",joking wife,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:26:32.207276+00:00
696,/Kanuri_Cohen_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,young man or boy,kin_role,"""wife's"" younger sibling (tiu)",kin_role,kinship,"Joking extended to the junior siblings of the chosen joking wife, who can ask joking money.","Because there is a joking relation in the “real” kinship system between a man and his wife’s junior siblings (real and classificatory), this joking is extended to the junior siblings of the junior cross-cousin who is chosen as the joking wife. They, too, can ask for joking money on Sala Laya.",joking relation with wife's younger siblings,tiu,mixed,joking_relationship,0.8,Juniors solicit joking money; reciprocity present but roles asymmetrical.,2026-07-06T09:26:32.207276+00:00
697,/Kanuri_Cohen_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,young man or boy,kin_role,"""wife's"" older sibling (k[unknown]esai)",kin_role,kinship,Older siblings treated jokingly as senior in-laws with respect/avoidance; part of the patterned joking system.,"Siblings older than the wife are treated jokingly as if they were senior in-laws, i.e., with respect and avoidance.",joking treated as senior in-laws,k[unknown]esai,mixed,joking_relationship,0.7,"Text indicates joking but also respect/avoidance, producing mixed interactional norms.",2026-07-06T09:26:32.207276+00:00
698,/Kanuri_Cohen_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,grandparents or great-grandparents,kin_role,grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,Explicit statement of an established joking relationship between grandparents and grandchildren.,"As Table 4:1 indicates, there is almost total agreement among respondents that a jokingrelationship exists between ego and his grandparents or great-grandparents if these are alive.",jokingrelationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:26:32.207276+00:00
699,/Kanuri_Cohen_46_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,namesake (person after whom a child is named),kin_role,namesake child (chubuna),kin_role,kinship,Text states that a person after whom a child is named has a (milder) joking relationship with that child.,"Conversely, anyone after whom a child has been named has a jokingrelationship (albeit somewhat milder) with his namesake.",namesake jokingrelationship,chubuna,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:26:32.207276+00:00
700,/Kanuri_Cohen_49_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,older sibling,kin_role,younger sibling’s wife,kin_role,kinship,Text states a recurrent joking relation specifically between an older sibling and his younger sibling’s wife; kinship-based.,"Indeed, in a minority of cases (13%) there are joking relations between the older sibling and his younger sibling’s wife, while hardly anyone jokes with wife’s older sibling.",joking relations,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:26:39.364458+00:00
701,/Kanuri_Cohen_50_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,Ego,kin_role,spouse's younger siblings,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly names a formal, recurrent joking relation between ego and spouse's younger siblings; clearly kinship-based.","There are formal joking relations between ego and his or her spouse’s younger siblings. Ego jokes about who his spouse’s younger sibling is going to marry, or have sexual relations with, or about possible affairs that the younger one should or might have.",formal joking relations,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Younger spouse-siblings are the primary targets; younger remains respectful toward older sibling's spouse.,2026-07-06T09:26:46.583525+00:00
702,/Kanuri_Cohen_51_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,grandparent,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,"Text explicitly names a reciprocal, culturally patterned joking relationship between grandparent and grandchild.","Due to the reciprocal nature of the joking relationship between grandparent and grandchild, the term of address (grandparent speaking) is kaka, and is used reciprocally, while the differentiated term for grandchild (diwo) is used primarily as a term of reference.",joking relationship,kaka / diwo,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:26:54.146377+00:00
703,/Kanuri_Cohen_60_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,Ol-Sis-Hu,kin_role,Wi-Yo-Bro,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly states a reciprocal joking relationship between these two kin roles within kinship.,"The Ol-Sis-Hu and Wi-Yo-Bro have a reciprocal joking relationship with one another. However, the junior in the situation must pay respect( nona ), or give respect ( b[unknown]rzum ) to the senior, even though their traditionally formalized interaction is one of reciprocal joking.",reciprocal joking relationship,b[unknown]rzum,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,Joking is reciprocal but embedded in asymmetric respect obligations (senior must give something).,2026-07-06T09:27:00.204702+00:00
704,/Kanuri_Cohen_68_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,grandparents and great-grandparents,kin_role,ego and grandchildren,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes formalized, recurrent permissive joking (suli) between grandparents/great-grandparents and grandchildren/ego.","The most diffuse or broadly based joking relationship is that between ego and his grandparents and great-grandparents, if any of the latter are alive. ... The child usually has the most intimate and fondest joking relationship with his namesake grandparent, if this person lives close by or adopts him.",suli,suli,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,"Reciprocity extends across alternate generations (third ascendant/descendant, 'yeiya'), but asymmetry in respect/fondness is noted.",2026-07-06T09:27:08.098596+00:00
705,/Kanuri_Cohen_69_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,grandparent,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,"Recurring, culturally recognized teasing between grandparents and grandchildren in social life and caregiving.","It is not uncommon to see a grandparent visiting his or her friends always in the company of their little joking partners. ... As the child grows older the joking aspect becomes more noticeable. The grandparent will point out a young playmate of the opposite sex and say to him - that is your wife, is it?, or to her - that is your husband, eh? ... You may pull your kaka’s (grandparent’s) beards, or go into his house and take his possessions, and he will not mind - he is happy because his children have borne children. If you have a yeiya (great-grandparent) alive, it is the same with him.",joking partners; grandparent joking-relationship,kaka / yeiya,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:27:14.477629+00:00
706,/Kanuri_Cohen_70_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,grandparent,kin_role,grandchild,kin_role,kinship,Text identifies chuwuna grandparent–grandchild as the principal suli (joking) relationship.,"Although the suli (joking) relationship holds for all members of the second and third ascendant and descendant generations it focuses for an individual in its most memorable and frequently emphasized form, between himself and his grandparent-namesake, his chuwuna.",chuwuna; suli (joking),chuwuna,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:27:34.928041+00:00
707,/Kanuri_Cohen_70_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,second and third ascendant generations,kin_role,second and third descendant generations,kin_role,kinship,Author states suli holds for members of the second and third ascendant and descendant generations (institutionalized kin joking).,Although the suli (joking) relationship holds for all members of the second and third ascendant and descendant generations...,suli (joking),suli,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Generalized generational joking relationship across ascendants and descendants.,2026-07-06T09:27:34.928041+00:00
708,/Kanuri_Cohen_70_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,father's brother,kin_role,child named after him,kin_role,kinship,Naming a child after father's brother creates a permissive chuwuna joking relation between them.,"If a child is named after a father’s brother or his father’s friend, he and that person have a mildly permissive joking relationship. They address each other as chuwuna and refer and act towards one another in a similar fashion to that of grandparents and grandchildren.",chuwuna,chuwuna,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:27:34.928041+00:00
709,/Kanuri_Cohen_70_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,father's friend,person,child named after him,kin_role,within_group,Text describes chuwuna joking extended to father's friends when a child is named after them (patterned social tie).,"If a child is named after a father’s brother or his father’s friend, he and that person have a mildly permissive joking relationship. They address each other as chuwuna and refer and act towards one another in a similar fashion to that of grandparents and grandchildren.",chuwuna,chuwuna,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Father's friend treated as a patterned social role creating joking license.,2026-07-06T09:27:34.928041+00:00
710,/Kanuri_Cohen_70_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,District Heads (people named after),person,children named after them,kin_role,within_group,Children named after District Heads become chuwuna with them; institutionalized namesake joking/adoption relations.,"Consequently, in the Kanuri state there are a great many children called after District Heads, aristocrats of the state, rich men, and people in high status positions generally.",chuwuna (namesake joking/adoption possibility),chuwuna,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Political leaders' namesakes create patterned social/joking ties and potential adoptions.,2026-07-06T09:27:34.928041+00:00
711,/Kanuri_Cohen_72_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,ego (male),kin_role,matrilateral junior cross-cousin of opposite sex,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes a recurrent, culturally patterned joking relation between male ego and matrilateral junior cross-cousin.","There is a jokingrelationship which takes place between cross-cousins of opposite sex. It takes the following form. Ego (male speaking) jokes with his Regulation Of matrilateral junior cross-cousin of opposite sex, calling her wife, concubine, Marriage lover, etc., and she returns the joke calling him slave, husband, lover, etc., and extracting joking money ( kungana sulibe ) from him on the Sala Laya (roughly the New Year).",jokingrelationship,kungana sulibe,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:27:54.572271+00:00
712,/Kanuri_Cohen_72_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,ego (male),kin_role,joking-wife's younger siblings (tiu / tiunyi),kin_role,kinship,Described as a recurring play relation where ego treats his joking-wife's younger siblings as 'tiu' (junior in-laws).,"This brings on a kind of play behaviour with the younger siblings of ego’s cousin which follows the adult rules of marriage. That is to say, a joking relation with Wi-Yo-Siblings who in this case are his junior cross-cousins as well as being his joking-wife’s younger siblings. Thus he will call them tiu (junior in-laws) and refer to them (in the situation) as tiunyi (my junior in-laws).",tiu / tiunyi,tiu,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:27:54.572271+00:00
713,/Kanuri_Cohen_72_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,ego (male),kin_role,patrilateral cousins,kin_role,kinship,Text states ego may enact the same patterned joking-play with patrilateral cousins when no matrilateral joking-wife exists.,"Given no joking-wife among the matri-kin, ego may go through exactly the same joking73 play with his patrilateral cousins. As in the materilateral case, if ego changes his joking-wife, the tiu joking relationships shift also in association with the change.",joking play,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:27:54.572271+00:00
714,/Kanuri_Cohen_72_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,woman,kin_role,husband's younger brothers,kin_role,kinship,Author states joking relations exist between a woman and her husband's younger brothers but are stringently avoided for cultural reasons.,Exactly the same conditions for the same reasons apply to the joking relations between a woman and her husband's younger brothers. Although the joking takes many forms the most common is for ego to joke about obtaining a sexual and/or a marriage partner for his spouse’s younger siblings.,,,unclear,avoidance,0.8,Text indicates the relation exists but is 'stringently avoided' (institutionalized avoidance).,2026-07-06T09:27:54.572271+00:00
715,/Kanuri_Cohen_73_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,master,group,slave,group,within_group,Text explicitly states master–slave joking exists outside kinship; classified as within-society occupational/role tie.,"Outside the kinship system a joking relationship may hold between a master and his slave, although this is usual only when the slave is an entertainer of some kind. In the above relationships which seem in all cases to cover an area of interrelations involving potential conflicts, the joking takes the form (usually) of the grandparent-grandchild relationship.",,kaka/diwo,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:29:00.892332+00:00
716,/Kanuri_Cohen_73_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,smiths,caste,butchers,caste,within_group,Explicit list of occupational joking pairs; within-society occupational relationship.,"Amongst the various occupations, joking relations obtain between smiths and butchers; barber-doctors and smiths; mallams (koranic teacher-priests) and hunters; kola-nut wholesalers and retailers; dyers and tailors; a builder of mud houses and his labourers; a fire-wood seller and his customers; pepper sellers and kola-nut wholesalers. In the above relationships which seem in all cases to cover an area of interrelations involving potential conflicts, the joking takes the form (usually) of the grandparent-grandchild relationship.",,kaka/diwo,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Multiple occupational pairs listed in same sentence; separate assertions created per pair.,2026-07-06T09:29:00.892332+00:00
717,/Kanuri_Cohen_73_NK,WesternAfrica,Kanuri,barber-doctors,caste,smiths,caste,within_group,Occupational joking pair explicitly listed.,"Amongst the various occupations, joking relations obtain between smiths and butchers; barber-doctors and smiths; mallams (koranic teacher-priests) and hunters; kola-nut wholesalers and retailers; dyers and tailors; a builder of mud houses and his labourers; a fire-wood seller and his customers; pepper sellers and kola-nut wholesalers. In the above relationships which seem in all cases to cover an area of interrelations involving potential conflicts, the joking takes the form (usually) of the grandparent-grandchild relationship.",,kaka/diwo,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:29:00.892332+00:00
