884,/Mossi_Hammond_263_KS,WesternAfrica,Mossi,age-sets,age_set,other age-sets,age_set,within_group,"Text describes structurally separate, reciprocal relations and ambivalent rivalry between age-sets implying institutionalized joking.","Age-sets are structurally separate within the age-grade. Their relations are reciprocal: individual members co-operate in the arrangement of one another's sexual liaisons; they pool their labor and share the profits they earn as members of their co-operative work organizations; and young men often leave Yatenga as migrant laborers in groups organized on the basis of age-grade membership, thereby reinforcing many of the reciprocal ties established in the work groups.",joking between age-sets,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Age-sets described as structurally equivalent and independent; joking framed as part of their relations.,2026-07-06T09:45:02.441064+00:00
885,/Mossi_Hammond_264_KS,WesternAfrica,Mossi,the Mossi,ethnic group,the Yarse,ethnic group,cross_group,"Text states joking is institutionalized between Mossi and Yarse—distinct ethnic groups, recurrent cultural tie.","I believe that joking is also institutionalized between the Mossi and the Yarse, a highly acculturated ethnic minority of Mande origin who live throughout Yatenga.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Author hedges belief and reports inadequate data.,2026-07-06T09:45:12.135683+00:00
886,/Mossi_Hammond_264_KS,WesternAfrica,Mossi,the Mossi,ethnic group,the indigenous blacksmith caste,caste,within_group,Text asserts institutionalized joking between Mossi and indigenous blacksmith caste; caste is a within-society social unit.,There is also joking between the Mossi and the indigenous blacksmith caste.,,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.5,Author states existence but notes data are inadequate.,2026-07-06T09:45:12.135683+00:00
887,/Mossi_Hammond_265_1_KS,WesternAfrica,Mossi,his wife,kin_role,a man's younger brothers,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes culturally expected, reciprocal teasing between a man's wife and his younger brothers (in-law joking).","Similarly, when a man's younger brothers come into the presence of his wife, she is expected to insult them in the same way—to deride with humor their appearance, their lack of energy, their skill at their work, or their intelligence. ... His brothers can be relied upon to relay to her husband what she has said, if he has not himself over-heard the exchange. ... And her husband's younger brothers, by retorting with hostile banter of the same sort, can convey to her the dissatisfaction of both her husband and their other kinsmen with her comportment, thus providing her, too, with a chance to mend her ways.",,,mixed,insulting_license,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:45:20.539356+00:00
888,/Mossi_Hammond_265_KS,WesternAfrica,Mossi,husband,kin_role,wife's younger sisters,kin_role,kinship,"Recurring, culturally patterned joking between a husband and his wife's younger sisters as communication channel.","The wife's younger sisters cannot take offense at this joking, and they can be relied upon to relay the husband's complaints to their sister—if, indeed, she does not herself overhear the joking exchange. Furthermore, since a wife's younger sisters are usually aware of the sources of her grievance with her husband, they can use the same means to communicate to him the reason for her resentment and consequent failure to perform her domestic tasks to his satisfaction.",joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:45:27.781454+00:00
889,/Mossi_Hammond_267_KS,WesternAfrica,Mossi,younger siblings of each spouse,kin_role,each spouse,kin_role,kinship,Text identifies 'sibling-in-law joking' as a traditional institution between spouses and their younger siblings.,"Although the potential for conflict in the marriage relationship remained high, or was even augmented by the stresses of adjusting to a changed milieu, sibling-in-law joking, the institution through which such tension is traditionally regulated among the Mossi, was no longer operative. Unless the younger siblings of each spouse had accompanied them to the resettlement area, those individuals were absent to whom they might have communicated their grievances and expressed their aggressive sentiments in a socially harmless, psychologically satisfactory, and culturally acceptable way.",sibling-in-law joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,Interpreted as an institutional joking tie involving spouses and their younger siblings (sibling-in-law relations); text frames it as kinship-based regulatory institution.,2026-07-06T09:45:40.620192+00:00
890,/Mossi_Skinner_195_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,Mossi,ethnic group,Gourounsi,ethnic group,cross_group,Explicitly described recurrent joking by Mossi calling Gourounsi 'ligidi' indicating an institutionalized tease across groups.,"Mossi often jokingly refer to the Gourounsi as their ligidi (meaning money), implying their status as individuals to be sold as slaves.",,ligidi,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:45:50.994252+00:00
891,/Mossi_Skinner_195_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,Mogho,regional group,Samogho,regional group,within_group,"Text identifies a recurrent, symmetrical joking relationship between Mogho and Samogho within the society.","The joking relationship between Mogho and Samogho is more nearly symmetrical. Both groups declare that their two ancestors quarreled over the bones of a dog, and this quarrel still continues.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,"No rituals dramatize this joking relationship, per text.",2026-07-06T09:45:50.994252+00:00
892,/Mossi_Skinner_333_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,dakiba,kin_role,the family rituals,group,kinship,Dakiba are named 'joking relatives' performing licensed antics at funerals within family rituals.,"the family rituals (such as the role of the dakiba [joking relatives], the preparation of the body, and matters of this kind) which take place within the confines of the homes of the deceased.",joking relatives,dakiba,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Dakiba are an institutionalized kin-role performing joking at funerals; text frames them as part of family ritual contexts.,2026-07-06T09:45:59.758399+00:00
893,/Mossi_Skinner_335_nk,WesternAfrica,Mossi,Catholic missionaries,group,their African converts (former students),group,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, culturally recognized joking/comradery between Catholic missionaries and their converts.","Whereas the Catholic priests made a total commitment to their charges (mind, body, and soul), the American Portestants were interested only in their souls. The result was that few American Protestants developed that easy comradery or “joking” relationship with their African converts that one witnesses so often between the Catholic missionaries and their former students.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:46:07.025334+00:00
894,/Mossi__263_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,Age-sets,age_set,Age-sets,age_set,within_group,"Text describes recurrent, reciprocal joking relationships between structurally separate age-sets.",Age-sets are structurally separate within the age-grade. Their relations are reciprocal: individual members co-operate in the arrangement of one another's sexual liaisons; they pool their labor and share the profits they earn as members of their co-operative work organizations;,Joking Between Age-Sets,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T09:46:32.124345+00:00
895,/Mossi__263_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,villages,village,villages,village,within_group,Intervillage joking described as institutionalized between members (and chiefs) of different villages within the polity.,"When intervillage joking its institutionalized it may involve all the members of each community. However, I believe it is particularly expected of their respective chiefs.",Intervillage Joking,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Document refers to villages within same political system; treated as within_group.,2026-07-06T09:46:32.124345+00:00
896,/Mossi__263_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,the Mossi,ethnic group,the Gurunsi,ethnic group,cross_group,Explicitly states Mossi maintain joking relationships with Gurunsi as separate ethnic groups with reciprocal expectations.,"The joking relationship which the Mossi maintain with the Gurunsi and the Samogho peoples, among others, appears to involve the same structural characteristics.",Joking Between Ethnic Groups,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:46:32.124345+00:00
897,/Mossi__263_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,the Mossi,ethnic group,the Samogho,ethnic group,cross_group,Text names Mossi–Samogho as having institutionalized joking with expectations of hospitality and assistance.,"The joking relationship which the Mossi maintain with the Gurunsi and the Samogho peoples, among others, appears to involve the same structural characteristics.",Joking Between Ethnic Groups,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T09:46:32.124345+00:00
898,/Mossi__263_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,the Mossi,ethnic group,the Yarse,ethnic group,cross_group,"Author states belief that joking is institutionalized between Mossi and Yarse, an acculturated ethnic minority.","3. I believe that joking is also institutionalized between the Mossi and the Yarse, a highly acculturated ethnic minority of Mande origin who live throughout Yatenga.",,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Author reports belief but notes inadequate data; coded lower confidence.,2026-07-06T09:46:32.124345+00:00
899,/Mossi__263_NK,WesternAfrica,Mossi,the Mossi,ethnic group,the indigenous blacksmith caste,caste,within_group,Text explicitly mentions institutionalized joking between Mossi and the indigenous blacksmith caste within the society.,There is also joking between the Mossi and the indigenous blacksmith caste. Unfortunately my data on these two instances of joking are inadequate.,,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.6,Author notes inadequate data; treated as within-group (caste within society).,2026-07-06T09:46:32.124345+00:00
926,/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK,WesternAfrica,Nupe,daughters-in-law,kin_role,the deceased,person,kinship,"Text states daughters-in-law are 'joking relations' exempt from mourning, indicating institutionalized kin joking with the deceased.","This, the Nupe will tell you, is quite in order: for the workers are always in-laws and ‘joking relations’ of the deceased (daughters-in-law or the wives of younger brothers), and are hence exempt from mourning.",joking relations,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Extracted as kinship-based licensed teasing/familiarity between deceased and daughters-in-law; symmetry not specified.,2026-07-06T09:50:25.191354+00:00
927,/Nupe_Nadel_126_NK,WesternAfrica,Nupe,wives of younger brothers,kin_role,the deceased,person,kinship,"Text explicitly names wives of younger brothers as 'joking relations' exempt from mourning, a recurrent kin-based joking tie.","This, the Nupe will tell you, is quite in order: for the workers are always in-laws and ‘joking relations’ of the deceased (daughters-in-law or the wives of younger brothers), and are hence exempt from mourning.",joking relations,,unclear,joking_relationship,0.9,Wives of younger brothers coded as kin_role; relation framed as institutional exemption from mourning.,2026-07-06T09:50:25.191354+00:00
928,/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK,WesternAfrica,Nupe,boys and young men,group,the girls,group,within_group,"Document describes recurrent, culturally licensed teasing between youths and girls at the navũ festival.","There is cruder horseplay, too, the boys trying to seize the girls or to bump into them as though unwittingly; while in the shadows and under the bridge couples pursue their amorous play unseen. But though there is plenty of teasing and joking, the language is never obscene.",,,mixed,teasing,0.9,Patterned festivalized teasing/licensed erotic play between male youths and female youths during navũ.,2026-07-06T09:50:49.874828+00:00
929,/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK,WesternAfrica,Nupe,age-grade association under its leader,age_set,age-group,age_set,within_group,Text reports recurring organized mock battles and playful attacks between age-grade associations during the navũ.,"The girls come in little groups, while the boys and young men are formed into companies, each composed of an age-grade association under its leader.... now and again this clowning gives way to a mock battle, when the boys will thrust and throw the torches at one another, age-group fighting age-group, not seriously, but keenly and with marked excitement.",,navũ,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,Age-grade companies engage in ritualized competitive play/fighting as part of navũ.,2026-07-06T09:50:49.874828+00:00
930,/Nupe_Nadel_241_NK,WesternAfrica,Nupe,the threefold dynastic division of the city,regional group,rival territory,regional group,within_group,"Navũ bathing places and torch processions follow the three dynastic divisions, whose factions ritualized nightly raids and mock battles.","The three different bathing places are chosen in accordance with the threefold dynastic division of the city, and are visited by the age-grade associations in the respective local division.... Thus the torch processions invariably led to organized and heated fights between the three groups; on the way back from the river, especially, one would ambush the other or try to invade the rival territory.",factional jealousies / mock battle,navũ,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Ritualized inter-faction fighting was licensed during navũ; government later forbade excesses.,2026-07-06T09:50:49.874828+00:00
