1016,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Yam(ə)l(ə)g,village,Tongo,village,cross_group,Repeated teasing/forcible appropriation between these villages described as accepted practical joke tied to clanship rights.,"When there was a funeral at Yam(ə)l(ə)g a party of relatives-in-law (deenam) would sometimes pass through Tongo from Wakii or T□nzugu carrying the obligatory food contributions ... Tongo youths would then, if they had a chance, intercept them and compel them to give up most of the food, leaving only a small portion to prove to the Yam(ə)l(ə)g people that their relatives-in-law had discharged their obligations.",inter-village joking/forcible appropriations (sunz(ɔ)p rights),sunz(ɔ)p,mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:00:50.273278+00:00
1017,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Yam(ə)l(ə)g,village,Nab(ə)rug,village,cross_group,"Yam(ə)l(ə)g people waylaid Nab(ə)rug parties as a customary, accepted practical joke.",Next time a similar party of relatives-in-law bound for Tongo from Nab(ə)rug passed through Yam(ə)l(ə)g they would be waylaid and robbed in the same way.,waylaying/teasing of passing relatives-in-law,sunz(ɔ)p,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Yam(ə)l(ə)g actors described as instigators in this instance.,2026-07-06T10:00:50.273278+00:00
1018,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Tongo,village,Wakii,village,cross_group,Tongo youths intercepted Wakii relatives-in-law passing through Tongo as an accepted practical joke.,"When there was a funeral at Yam(ə)l(ə)g a party of relatives-in-law (deenam) would sometimes pass through Tongo from Wakii or T□nzugu carrying the obligatory food contributions ... Tongo youths would then, if they had a chance, intercept them and compel them to give up most of the food ... It was thought of as a practical joke, and accepted as such by the victims.",interception/teasing of passing relatives-in-law,deenam,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.85,Victims are described as relatives-in-law (deenam) from Wakii.,2026-07-06T10:00:50.273278+00:00
1019,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Tongo,village,T□nzugu,village,cross_group,Tongo youths intercepted relatives-in-law from T□nzugu passing through Tongo; described as accepted practical joking.,"When there was a funeral at Yam(ə)l(ə)g a party of relatives-in-law (deenam) would sometimes pass through Tongo from Wakii or T□nzugu carrying the obligatory food contributions ... Tongo youths would then, if they had a chance, intercept them and compel them to give up most of the food ... It was thought of as a practical joke, and accepted as such by the victims.",interception/teasing of passing relatives-in-law,deenam,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.8,T□nzugu named in same pattern as Wakii; orthography preserved from source.,2026-07-06T10:00:50.273278+00:00
1020,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Tongo,village,Yam(ə)l(ə)g,village,cross_group,Tongo people were permitted to pluck crops in Yam(ə)l(ə)g as an exaggerated assertion of clanship ties; described as accepted teasing.,"In the same way, twenty years ago, a Tongo man who chanced to be passing through Yam(ə)l(ə)g when the guinea corn was ripening or the ground-nuts ready for harvesting, might pluck a few twigs of guinea corn or pull up a bunch of ground-nuts to stay his hunger. He would shout out, ‘Whose corn is this, yo-o-o? I am going to pluck some.’ The owner ... would not raise a hue and cry, for this was not theft but an exaggerated assertion of clanship ties.",licensed crop-plucking/teasing,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Later political hostility ended this custom between the two places.,2026-07-06T10:00:50.273278+00:00
1021,/Tallensi_Fortes_93_NK(1),WesternAfrica,Tallensi,a woman (wife),kin_role,"sisters-in-law (p(ɔ)□akie, pl. p(ɔ)□akies)",kin_role,kinship,Text describes recurrent joking/play between a wife and her sisters-in-law as a culturally patterned relation.,"With them, often her equals in years and her companions in work, she can laugh and play and joke.  Like herself, they are already, or are fated to be, married out of their natal families to ‘strangers’.",joke and play,p(ɔ)□akie,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:01:03.775003+00:00
1022,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Gbizug,lineage,Tongo,lineage,within_group,Text describes recurrent playful taunting between complementary politico-ritual lineages in ceremonies.,"Gbizug and Tongo men, for example, have a habit of doing this. The Chief of Tongo and the Gbizug t□ndaana, on the rare occasions when they meet in public, will interrupt a long and ceremonious discussion with a95 jesting altercation about their mythologically founded bonds of mutual coercion.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:01:26.153552+00:00
1023,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Chief of Tongo,lineage,Gbizug t□ndaana,lineage,within_group,Explicit ritual-office pair engages in recurrent public jesting during ceremonies.,"The Chief of Tongo and the Gbizug t□ndaana, on the rare occasions when they meet in public, will interrupt a long and ceremonious discussion with a95 jesting altercation about their mythologically founded bonds of mutual coercion.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Ritual-office pair extracted as institutionalized joking in ceremonial contexts.,2026-07-06T10:01:26.153552+00:00
1024,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Kpata'ar,lineage,Gundaat,lineage,within_group,Author states elders of these politico-ritual lineages often tease one another in ceremonies.,"Elders of Kpata'ar and Gundaat, which have a similar politico-ritual relationship to that of Tongo and Gbizug, often tease one another in the same way when they meet to perform a ceremony together.",,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:01:26.153552+00:00
1025,/Tallensi_Fortes_94_NK,WesternAfrica,Tallensi,Yam(ə)l(ə)g,village,Sie,village,cross_group,Text reports patterned mutual actions between named communities prior to political conflict.,The people of Yam(ə)l(ə)g and of Sie used to act in the same way towards each other before the political jealousies that to-day poison their relationship arose.,,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.7,Passage links their prior reciprocal behaviour to same customary pattern; less explicit as joking but described as 'act in the same way'.,2026-07-06T10:01:26.153552+00:00
1092,/Wolof_181_nk,WesternAfrica,Wolof,Diop,patronym,Ndiaye,patronym,within_group,Text gives kall joking greeting exchanged between Diop and Ndiaye surnames; recurring group-level institution.,"A. Diop!(B’s name)

B. Ndiaye! (A’s name)

Diop!

Ndiaye!

A. Sant ba neehul!

That surname is not pleasing!

B. Neeh na kaay.

It is so pleasing.

A. Lekk tyeb!

(The Diops) eat rice! [luxury food; this
means they are greedy]

B. Ndiaye rek, nyoo ko lekk!

Only the Ndiayes, it is they who eat it!",kall / joking greeting,kall,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:08:28.862764+00:00
1093,/Wolof_181_nk,WesternAfrica,Wolof,griot,group,young or low-ranking noble,kin_role,within_group,Author states joking greeting may occur between a griot and a young/low-ranking noble as a recognized practice.,"It is rare to see a joking greeting between a griot and the Imam of the mosque, for instance, although such a greeting might well occur between a griot and a young or low-ranking noble.",,kall,unclear,joking_relationship,0.65,Text frames this as a permissible instance of kall between occupational/status roles rather than specific lineages.,2026-07-06T10:08:28.862764+00:00
1094,/Wolof_182_nk,WesternAfrica,Wolof,Diop,patronym,Ndiaye,patronym,kinship,Document gives kall joking greeting example and states certain surname pairs perform it; Diop–Ndiaye shown.,"A. Diop! (B’s name)

Diop! (B’s name)

B. Sa sant Ndiaye neehul!

Your surname Ndiaye is not pleasing!

The joking relationship provides, however, only a very limited opportunity to express equality. The kall involves only certain pairs of surnames, and any one person has only one or two surname groups in which to find joking partners.",kall,kall,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:08:35.052920+00:00
1095,/Yoruba_67_nk,WesternAfrica,Yoruba,the grave diggers,group,his children,kin_role,within_group,"Repeated, culturally patterned joking by grave-diggers toward bereaved children during funerals.","If the deceased was elderly, the grave diggers joke while they work to make his children forger some of their sorrow and laugh a little; but if he was their own age or younger, they do not joke while they work and they do not ask for food and drink.",joke while they work,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,Occupational group (grave diggers) performs licensed joking directed at bereaved kin (children) as a funerary practice.,2026-07-06T10:08:40.922199+00:00
1438,/tiv_12_nk,WesternAfrica,tiv,Tiv,ethnic group,Fulani,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly names a recurrent joking relationship (sengen) between Tiv and Fulani; cross-ethnic partners.,"Tiv in almost all parts of Tivland say today that they came into their present
country from the south-east. While they were “ coming down ” ( sen, that is,
going north-west) they met the Fulani, with whom they lived for a time and
from whom they parted peacefully. As evidence they cite the present “ joking
relationship ” ( sengen ) between Tiv and Fulani.",joking relationship,sengen,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:34:21.366455+00:00
1439,/tiv_1_nk,WesternAfrica,tiv,Tiv,ethnic group,Fulani,ethnic group,cross_group,Text explicitly states a recurring joking relationship between Tiv and Fulani—an institutionalized interethnic tie.,"""Coming down,"" as they put it, they met the Fulani, with whom they still recognize a joking relationship.",joking relationship,,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:34:31.936726+00:00
1487,/wolof_180_nk,WesternAfrica,wolof,Diop,patronym,Ndiaye,patronym,kinship,Text identifies Diop–Ndiaye as a named kall (jokingrelationship) between surnamed kin groups with mother–son origin.,"Certain pairs of surnames, and the kin groups which bear them, stand in a special relationship to each other, called kall ‘jokingrelationship.’ The families Diop and Ndiaye, for example, are in jokingrelationship because, supposedly, ‘the Diops are the mothers of the Ndiayes.’",kall (joking relationship),kall,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:39:52.277031+00:00
1488,/wolof_35_nk,WesternAfrica,wolof,grandfathers,kin_role,grandsons,kin_role,kinship,Text explicitly describes a culturally allowed recurring joking tie between grandfathers and grandsons.,"There is a jokingrelationship between grandfathers and grandsons. A grandson is permitted to act disrespectfully toward his grandfather, pulling his beard, calling him by a nickname, making fun of his physique, and contradicting him.",jokingrelationship,,non_symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:39:59.361775+00:00
1489,/wolof_47_nk,WesternAfrica,wolof,child of the mother9s brother,kin_role,child of the father9s sister,kin_role,kinship,"Text describes culturally recognized, recurrent joking liberties between these cross-cousin kin roles.","Like many other peoples, the Wolof have an institution-alized cross cousins relationship. It is especially marked by a jokingrelationship between the child Regulation Of of the mother9s brother who is called ""master"" ( borom) and the child of the father9s sister who is called ""slave"" ( jam).",master/slave (borom/jam) joking relationship,"borom, jam",mixed,joking_relationship,0.9,"Oppositional labels (master/slave) exist, but joking liberties described as mutual, especially between opposite-sex cross-cousins.",2026-07-06T10:40:05.511891+00:00
1490,/wolof_55_nk,WesternAfrica,wolof,jam (father's sister's child),kin_role,b[unknown]r[unknown]m (mother's brother's child),kin_role,kinship,"Text describes recurring, institutionalized jam ↔ b...r...m joking rights in kin relations and ceremonies.","Among the Wolof the relationship is expressed in terms of jam (slave) and b[unknown]r[unknown]m (master), a father’s sister’s child will be jam, a mother's brother’s child b[unknown]r[unknown]m. On ceremonial occasions the jam come to help their b[unknown]r[unknown]m, and are rewarded for their services.",,jam / b[unknown]r[unknown]m,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.95,,2026-07-06T10:40:20.335372+00:00
1491,/wolof_55_nk,WesternAfrica,wolof,Njai,clan,Gei,clan,within_group,"Text explicitly states Njai are b...r...m of the Gei, a recurrent clan-level joking relationship.","In clan relationships, the Njai are the b[unknown]r[unknown]m of the Gei, the Gei b[unknown]r[unknown]m of the Seka, the Seka b[unknown]r[unknown]m of the Job, and so on.",,b[unknown]r[unknown]m,symmetric,joking_relationship,0.9,,2026-07-06T10:40:20.335372+00:00
